var e=JSON.parse("[[1601,\"Isolated testing of features means checking one specific piece of functionality without the influence of external dependencies such as a database, network requests or other modules. The main approach is unit tests combined with techniques for substituting dependencies, for example mocks and stubs. This lets you test the feature's logic in a controlled environment, find errors quickly and make results predictable. This approach is the foundation of the testing pyramid and is recommended for most projects.\",\"Isolated testing of features is a strategy in which every functional unit (feature) is checked separately from the rest of the system. The goal is to make sure its internal logic works correctly, independently of the state of external services, databases or other application modules.\\n\\nKey principles and approaches\\n\\nThe following techniques are used to achieve isolation:\\n\\n- Unit testing: Writing small tests for individual functions, methods or classes.\\n\\n- Dependency injection: Supplying dependencies through interfaces or parameters, which makes it easy to replace the real implementations with test doubles.\\n\\n- Test doubles: The general name for objects that replace real dependencies in tests. They include:\\n\\n- Mocks: Objects that verify how the code under test interacts with them (for example, whether a method was called with particular arguments).\\n\\n- Stubs: Objects that provide pre-defined answers to calls.\\n\\n- Fakes: Simplified but working implementations of dependencies (for example, an in-memory database).\\n\\nA practical example in Python using unittest.mock\\n\\nConsider a simple feature — a service for sending notifications to users, which depends on an external email client.\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\n\\nIsolated testing of features is applied at the unit-testing stage, which is the base of the testing pyramid. It is especially useful:\\n\\n- When developing with the TDD (Test-Driven Development) methodology.\\n\\n- For testing complex business logic that must not depend on external systems.\\n\\n- In a microservice architecture, where every service must be tested independently.\\n\\n- For fast feedback during development, since such tests run very quickly.\\n\\nConclusion: Isolated testing of features is worth applying to verify the core business logic of any application. It ensures reliability, speeds up development and makes refactoring easier, since errors are localised to a specific module rather than to the integration with the outside world.\",\"# feature.py\\nclass NotificationService:\\n    def __init__(self, email_client):\\n        self.email_client = email_client\\n\\n    def notify_user(self, user_email, message):\\n        # The feature's logic: validation and sending\\n        if not user_email or \\\"@\\\" not in user_email:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid email\\\")\\n        return self.email_client.send(to=user_email, body=message)\\n\\n# test_feature.py\\nimport unittest\\nfrom unittest.mock import Mock\\nfrom feature import NotificationService\\n\\nclass TestNotificationService(unittest.TestCase):\\n    def test_notify_user_success(self):\\n        # 1. Create a mock email client\\n        mock_email_client = Mock()\\n        # 2. Configure it to return a successful result\\n        mock_email_client.send.return_value = {\\\"status\\\": \\\"sent\\\"}\\n\\n        # 3. Inject the mock into the service under test\\n        service = NotificationService(mock_email_client)\\n\\n        # 4. Call the feature's method\\n        result = service.notify_user(\\\"user@example.com\\\", \\\"Hello!\\\")\\n\\n        # 5. Check that the logic worked correctly\\n        self.assertEqual(result, {\\\"status\\\": \\\"sent\\\"})\\n        # 6. Check that the mock was called with the expected arguments\\n        mock_email_client.send.assert_called_once_with(\\n            to=\\\"user@example.com\\\", body=\\\"Hello!\\\"\\n        )\\n\\n    def test_notify_user_invalid_email(self):\\n        mock_email_client = Mock()\\n        service = NotificationService(mock_email_client)\\n        # Check that an invalid email raises an exception\\n        with self.assertRaises(ValueError):\\n            service.notify_user(\\\"invalid-email\\\", \\\"Hello!\\\")\\n        # Make sure sending was not called\\n        mock_email_client.send.assert_not_called()\"],[1602,\"Circular dependencies in FSD arise when modules from different layers or slices refer to each other directly or indirectly, creating a closed loop. To avoid them you need to follow the direction of dependencies strictly: from the upper layers (shared, entities) to the lower ones (features, widgets, pages). Use dependency inversion through abstractions (interfaces) or each slice's public API. Check the project regularly with linters (for example madge) to spot cycles at an early stage.\",\"Feature-Sliced Design (FSD) is an architectural methodology for front-end applications that organises code by business logic (slices) and technical layers. Circular dependencies break FSD's main rule — a unidirectional flow of data and dependencies — which leads to difficulties in maintenance, testing and code reuse.\\nCauses and consequences of cycles\\nA cycle arises when module A imports module B, and module B, directly or through a chain of other modules, imports module A. In the context of FSD this can be between layers (for example, a widget imports from a feature, and that feature imports something from the same widget) or between slices of a single feature. It makes the code coupled, complicates refactoring and can lead to errors during initialisation.\\nThe main prevention strategies\\n\\n- Follow the direction of the layers: Dependencies must go only from the upper layers to the lower ones: `shared <- entities <- features <- widgets <- pages <- app`. Do not let modules from the lower layers import anything from the upper ones.\\n- Use public APIs: Every slice should export its functionality through an `index.ts` file (the public API). The internal implementation is hidden, and imports from other slices are made only through that public interface.\\n- Introduce abstractions and dependency inversion: If two modules need to interact, move the shared contract (an interface, a type) into the `shared` or `entities` layer. Then both modules depend on that abstraction rather than on each other.\\n- Apply linters and static analysis: Tools such as `madge` or `dependency-cruiser` can detect circular dependencies in a project automatically and fail the build.\\nPractical example\\nConsider the problem: the `UserCard` widget (the widgets layer) uses the `useUserData` hook from the `user-profile` feature (the features layer), and that hook in turn tries to use the `Avatar` component from the same `UserCard` widget — a cycle arises.\\n\\nThe solution: move the `Avatar` component into a shared layer (for example, `shared/ui`), so that both modules depend on it and not on each other.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAvoiding circular dependencies in FSD is critically important for maintaining a clean, scalable architecture. Strictly observing the direction of imports, using public APIs and moving shared abstractions into the lower layers keep the code decomposed and testable. Apply these practices from the very start of a project and automate the dependency checks.\",\"// WRONG: a circular dependency\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/Avatar.tsx\\nexport const Avatar = ({ src }) => <img src={src} alt=\\\"\\\" />;\\n\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/UserCard.tsx\\nimport { useUserData } from 'features/user-profile';\\nexport const UserCard = () => {\\n  const user = useUserData(); // An import from features\\n  return <Avatar src={user.avatar} />;\\n};\\n\\n// features/user-profile/lib/useUserData.ts\\nimport { Avatar } from 'widgets/user-card/ui/Avatar'; // An import back the other way!\\nexport const useUserData = () => {\\n  // ... logic that uses Avatar\\n};\\n\\n// RIGHT: removing the cycle via the shared layer\\n// shared/ui/Avatar/Avatar.tsx\\nexport const Avatar = ({ src }) => <img src={src} alt=\\\"\\\" />;\\n\\n// widgets/user-card/ui/UserCard.tsx\\nimport { useUserData } from 'features/user-profile';\\nimport { Avatar } from 'shared/ui/Avatar';\\nexport const UserCard = () => {\\n  const user = useUserData();\\n  return <Avatar src={user.avatar} />;\\n};\\n\\n// features/user-profile/lib/useUserData.ts\\n// No longer imports Avatar from widgets\\nimport { Avatar } from 'shared/ui/Avatar'; // If needed - an import from shared\\nexport const useUserData = () => {\\n  // ...\\n};\"],[1603,\"An async function always returns a Promise. If there is no return statement inside the async function, it implicitly returns a Promise that resolves with the value undefined. This is analogous to an ordinary function, which returns undefined without a return, but wrapped in a Promise. For example, `async function foo() {}` returns a Promise that settles as a fulfilled `Promise<undefined>`.\",\"Async functions in JavaScript are functions declared with the `async` keyword. They are intended to simplify working with asynchronous operations based on promises. The key rule: an async function always returns a Promise object, regardless of what happens inside its body.\\nBehaviour when there is no return\\nIf an async function has no `return` statement (or has a `return` with no value), JavaScript implicitly returns `undefined`, but wraps it in a resolved Promise. This is analogous to the way an ordinary function without a return returns `undefined`, but with the addition of a promise wrapper.\\nCode example\\nConsider a simple example:\\n\\nIn this code the `noReturn` function contains no `return`, so it returns a Promise that resolves automatically with the value `undefined`. We can use the `.then()` method or `await` to obtain that value.\\nPractical application\\nThis behaviour is useful in scenarios where an async function performs side effects (for example, logging or sending data) but should not return a specific value. For example, a function for sending an analytics event:\\n\\nHere we can handle the completion of the operation, or errors, through the Promise, even though the function returns no data.\\nConclusion\\nAn async function with no return returns a Promise resolved with the value `undefined`. This behaviour guarantees consistency: any async function can be handled as a Promise, which simplifies asynchronous control flow and error handling. Use it when you need to perform an asynchronous action without needing to return a specific result.\",\"async function noReturn() {\\n  // No return statement\\n}\\n\\nconst result = noReturn();\\nconsole.log(result); // Promise { <state>: \\\"fulfilled\\\", <value>: undefined }\\n\\nresult.then(value => {\\n  console.log(value); // undefined\\n});\\n\\nasync function sendAnalytics(event) {\\n  await fetch('/api/analytics', {\\n    method: 'POST',\\n    body: JSON.stringify(event)\\n  });\\n  // No return, but the function still returns a Promise<undefined>\\n}\\n\\n// Usage:\\nsendAnalytics({ action: 'click' })\\n  .then(() => console.log('Analytics sent'))\\n  .catch(err => console.error('Error:', err));\"],[1604,\"In JavaScript, callbacks were historically used for asynchrony, but they led to \\\"callback hell\\\". Promises provided a more structured approach with the `.then()` and `.catch()` methods. The modern async/await syntax lets you write asynchronous code that looks synchronous, which improves readability. All these mechanisms work on top of the Event Loop, which manages the execution of code in a single-threaded environment.\",\"Asynchrony in JavaScript is a fundamental concept that lets you perform long-running operations (for example, network requests or reading files) without blocking the main thread of execution. It is critically important for building responsive web applications.\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- Callbacks: Functions passed as arguments that are invoked after an asynchronous operation completes. This is the basic mechanism, but one that often leads to complex nested code (\\\"callback hell\\\").\\n- Promises: Objects representing the future result of an operation. They have states (pending, fulfilled, rejected) and the methods `.then()` for handling success and `.catch()` for handling errors, which lets you build chains of calls.\\n- Async/Await: Syntactic sugar over promises, introduced in ES2017. The `async` keyword declares an asynchronous function, and `await` suspends its execution until a promise settles, making the code linear and easy to read.\\nCode examples\\nLet's compare the approaches using a simulated data fetch:\\n\\nWhere they are used\\nThese tools are used everywhere: to perform HTTP requests (the fetch API, axios), to work with databases, to read files in Node.js, to handle user events and for any operation whose duration is unknown or long.\\nConclusion: For new code async/await is preferable because of its cleanliness and readability, but an understanding of promises is essential, since async/await is built on them. Callbacks still turn up in old code and in some APIs, so you need to know them too.\",\"// 1. Callback (the old style)\\nfunction fetchDataCallback(callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    callback(null, 'Data received');\\n  }, 1000);\\n}\\n\\nfetchDataCallback((err, data) => {\\n  if (err) console.error(err);\\n  else console.log(data); // After a second: 'Data received'\\n});\\n\\n// 2. Promise\\nfunction fetchDataPromise() {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    setTimeout(() => resolve('Data received'), 1000);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\nfetchDataPromise()\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(err => console.error(err));\\n\\n// 3. Async/Await (the modern style)\\nasync function getData() {\\n  try {\\n    const data = await fetchDataPromise(); // Wait for the promise to settle\\n    console.log(data);\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.error(err);\\n  }\\n}\\ngetData();\"],[1605,\"When the dependencies of useEffect change, React compares the current dependency values with the previous ones. If at least one value has changed, React runs the cleanup function of the previous effect (if one was defined) and then runs the effect itself again. If the dependency array is empty, the effect runs only when the component mounts and unmounts. If no dependency array is passed, the effect runs on every render.\",\"The `useEffect` hook in React lets you perform side effects in functional components. The key element controlling its behaviour is the dependency array passed as the second argument.\\n\\nHow the dependency comparison works\\n\\nReact stores the dependency values from the previous render. Before every render (including re-renders) it performs a shallow comparison (`Object.is`) of each element of the array with its previous value. If at least one value has changed, the following sequence is triggered:\\n\\n- The cleanup function from the previous run of the effect is executed, if one was returned.\\n\\n- The body of the effect itself (the synchronous function passed to `useEffect`) runs again.\\n\\nPractical examples of the behaviour\\n\\nConsider a component that subscribes to data by user ID.\\n\\nOn the first render with `userId=1` the effect runs and the subscription is created. If on the next render `userId` stays 1, the effect does not run again. If `userId` changes to 2, React first runs the cleanup function (unsubscribing from user 1's data) and then runs the effect again, subscribing to user 2's data.\\n\\nSpecial cases of the dependency array\\n\\n- An empty array `[]`: The effect runs once after the component mounts, and the cleanup runs on unmount. The equivalent of `componentDidMount` and `componentWillUnmount`.\\n\\n- No array at all: The effect runs after every render of the component. This can lead to performance problems.\\n\\n- Objects/arrays in the dependencies: Since the comparison is shallow, a new object with the same contents counts as a change. This is a frequent cause of infinite loops.\\n\\nConclusion: The dependency array in `useEffect` is a tool for precise control over when a side effect should run. Using it correctly prevents unnecessary computation and memory leaks and keeps the data in the component up to date, synchronising effects with specific props or state.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('The effect runs for userId:', userId);\\n  const subscription = subscribeToUserData(userId);\\n\\n  // The cleanup function\\n  return () => {\\n    console.log('Cleanup for the previous userId:', userId);\\n    unsubscribeFromUserData(userId);\\n  };\\n}, [userId]); // The dependency: userId\"],[1606,\"If there is a return statement in a finally block, it overrides the result returned from the try or catch block. That means the value returned from try or catch is ignored and the function returns the value from finally. This behaviour can be non-obvious and is often considered an anti-pattern, since it hides the original result of execution, or the error.\",\"In JavaScript the `finally` block is part of the `try...catch...finally` construct and is intended for code that must run regardless of whether an exception occurred in the `try` block. It is normally used to release resources, for example closing files or database connections.\\n\\nThe precedence of a return in finally\\n\\nThe key behaviour is that if a `return` statement is used inside the `finally` block, that value becomes the return value of the whole function. This happens even if the `try` or `catch` block has already performed its own `return` or an exception was thrown. In this way `finally` \\\"intercepts\\\" control of the return.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nConsider an example where a `return` in `finally` overrides the return from `try`:\\n\\nAs you can see, the function returned the string \\\"from finally\\\", and the value \\\"from try\\\" was ignored.\\n\\nIf an exception is thrown in the `try` block and there is a `return` in `finally`, the exception is also suppressed:\\n\\nThe error was caught in `catch`, but the value from `finally` was returned. This can hide important information about the error, which is a dangerous side effect.\\n\\nWhere this is used, and the conclusion\\n\\nIn practice, using `return` inside `finally` is strongly discouraged. It breaks the expected control flow and can lead to bugs that are hard to debug, especially when exceptions are hidden. The main purpose of `finally` is to perform cleanup, not to return values.\\n\\nSummary: Avoid the `return` statement in a `finally` block. Use that block only to guarantee that cleanup code runs, so as not to mask the results of the main logic or its errors.\",\"function example1() {\\n  try {\\n    console.log('Inside try');\\n    return 'from try';\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('Inside catch');\\n    return 'from catch';\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('Inside finally');\\n    return 'from finally';\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(example1());\\n// Console output:\\n// Inside try\\n// Inside finally\\n// from finally\\n\\nfunction example2() {\\n  try {\\n    throw new Error('Error in try');\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('Caught:', err.message);\\n    return 'from catch';\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('Finally block');\\n    return 'from finally';\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(example2());\\n// Output:\\n// Caught: Error in try\\n// Finally block\\n// from finally\"],[1607,\"The forEach method is not designed to work with asynchronous functions, because it does not wait for the promises returned by the callback to settle. It simply starts each asynchronous operation and immediately moves to the next, without waiting for it to finish. As a result the operations run in parallel (or in an arbitrary order) rather than sequentially, and you cannot handle the results or the errors properly. To run asynchronous operations sequentially you should use an ordinary `for...of` loop with `await`.\",\"The `forEach` method is a synchronous array iterator in JavaScript. When you pass an asynchronous function (marked `async`) to it, that function returns a promise, but `forEach` has no way of \\\"waiting\\\" for that promise. Instead it immediately invokes the next iteration, which results in all the asynchronous operations starting in parallel with no guarantee about the order of completion.\\n\\nHow forEach behaves with async\\n\\nConsider an example:\\n\\nThe output will be: \\\"Start\\\", \\\"End\\\", \\\"Done after 500ms\\\", \\\"Done after 1000ms\\\", \\\"Done after 2000ms\\\". Note that \\\"End\\\" appears straight away, because `forEach` does not wait for the asynchronous tasks to finish. The tasks run in parallel, and the order of the output depends on the delay times, not on the order in the array.\\n\\nWhy this is a problem\\n\\n- No sequencing: There is no way to guarantee that the operations run one after another.\\n\\n- No way to handle errors: If one operation fails, `forEach` does not stop, and the error is hard to catch.\\n\\n- You cannot use the results: Since the operations are fired and forgotten, collecting their results into an array after the `forEach` will not work — the array will be empty.\\n\\nAlternatives for asynchronous operations\\n\\nFor sequential execution, use a `for...of` loop:\\n\\nFor parallel execution while keeping the results, `Promise.all` with `map` is suitable:\\n\\nConclusion: Use `forEach` only for synchronous operations. For asynchronous code choose `for...of` (sequential) or `Promise.all` with `map` (parallel), depending on the task.\",\"const delays = [1000, 500, 2000];\\n\\nasync function asyncTask(ms) {\\n  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\\n  console.log(`Done after ${ms}ms`);\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log('Start');\\ndelays.forEach(async (ms) => {\\n  await asyncTask(ms);\\n});\\nconsole.log('End');\\n\\nasync function processSequentially() {\\n  for (const ms of delays) {\\n    await asyncTask(ms); // Wait for each one to finish\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nasync function processInParallel() {\\n  const promises = delays.map(ms => asyncTask(ms));\\n  await Promise.all(promises); // Wait for them all at once\\n}\"],[1608,\"The implementation of objects in JavaScript is based on a data structure similar to a hash table, or dictionary. That allows properties to be stored and accessed efficiently by their names (keys). Modern engines such as V8 use sophisticated optimisations, for example hidden classes, to speed up work with objects whose structure does not change dynamically. Understanding this helps you write more performant code.\",\"Objects in JavaScript are the fundamental structure for storing collections of data as key-value pairs. At a low level, JavaScript engines (for example V8 in Chrome and Node.js) implement objects using mechanisms reminiscent of hash tables. That provides fast access to properties by their string (or symbol) keys.\\nThe internal implementation and its optimisations\\nThe simplest implementation is a classic hash table, where the key is hashed to determine an index in an array (a \\\"bucket\\\") where the value is stored. To improve performance, however, engines apply sophisticated optimisations:\\n\\n- Hidden classes (or shapes): If objects are created with the same sequence of property additions, the engine creates an internal \\\"hidden class\\\" for them, describing the layout of the properties in memory. That lets properties be accessed by offset, as in a C struct, rather than by a dynamic hash-table lookup.\\n- Inline caches (IC): The engine caches information about where an object's property is located, based on its hidden class, which sharply speeds up repeated access.\\nA practical example and the impact on performance\\nLet's look at how coding style can affect performance because of changes of hidden class:\\n\\nThis approach applies everywhere objects are used: to store configurations, model data, as records, and of course as prototypes for other objects.\\nConclusion: Understanding that objects are based on hash tables with hidden-class optimisations helps you write code that works better with the JavaScript engine. This is especially useful when working with a large number of objects in performance-critical sections, for example in game engines or Node.js server applications.\",\"// Bad: different hidden classes are created\\nfunction createBadPoint(x, y) {\\n  const point = {};\\n  point.x = x; // Hidden class C0 -> C1\\n  point.y = y; // C1 -> C2\\n  return point;\\n}\\n\\n// Good: the same order of properties - a single hidden class\\nfunction createGoodPoint(x, y) {\\n  return { x, y }; // Always created with the x and y properties at once\\n}\\n\\nconst badPoints = [];\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n  badPoints.push(createBadPoint(i, i*2)); // Many objects, but one hidden class\\n}\\n// Performance here will be good, since the order in which properties are added is the same.\\n// But if we sometimes added a 'z' property in the middle, performance would drop.\"],[1609,\"Object.keys returns an array of strings containing only the object's own enumerable properties. A `for...in` loop iterates over all the object's enumerable properties, including those inherited from the prototype chain. Object.keys is usually safer, since it does not touch prototype properties. For a guaranteed iteration order it is better to use Object.keys followed by forEach or for...of.\",\"In JavaScript there are several ways to iterate over an object's properties. The two most common are the `Object.keys()` method and the `for...in` loop. Although both give you access to the properties, their behaviour and their areas of application differ.\\nObject.keys()\\nThe `Object.keys(obj)` method returns an array of strings representing the own enumerable properties of the object `obj`. \\\"Own\\\" means the properties belong to the object itself and are not inherited from its prototype. \\\"Enumerable\\\" means properties whose internal `enumerable` flag is set to `true` (most properties created by ordinary assignment are enumerable).\\n\\nThe for...in loop\\nThe `for (const key in obj)` loop iterates over all the object's enumerable properties, including those inherited through the prototype chain. That is the key difference. If the object's prototype has enumerable properties, they too will be listed, which is often an unwanted side effect.\\n\\nTo make a `for...in` loop iterate only over own properties, you need to use an `obj.hasOwnProperty(key)` check inside the loop.\\nPractical application and recommendations\\n\\n- Object.keys is ideal when you need an array of keys for further processing (for example, with `map` or `filter`), or when it is important to work only with the object's own properties.\\n- for...in can be useful for debugging or in specific cases where you need to analyse the whole prototype chain. Using it, however, requires caution.\\n- Modern JavaScript also offers `Object.values()` to get the values and `Object.entries()` to get [key, value] pairs.\\nConclusion: Use `Object.keys()` (or `Object.entries()`) to iterate safely over an object's own properties, especially when working with data whose structure you do not fully know. Use the `for...in` loop deliberately, keeping its behaviour with inherited properties in mind.\",\"const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj)); // ['a', 'b']\\n\\n// Use with for...of\\nfor (const key of Object.keys(obj)) {\\n  console.log(key, obj[key]); // a 1, then b 2\\n}\\n\\nconst parent = { inheritedProp: 'from parent' };\\nconst child = Object.create(parent);\\nchild.ownProp = 'own';\\n\\n// for...in prints both the own and the inherited property\\nfor (const key in child) {\\n  console.log(key); // 'ownProp', then 'inheritedProp'\\n}\\n\\n// Object.keys shows only the own property\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(child)); // ['ownProp']\"],[1610,\"To run asynchronous operations sequentially in modern JavaScript, promise chaining or the async/await syntax is used. A promise chain lets you link operations with `.then()`, where the result of the previous one is passed into the next. Async/await makes the code more linear and readable, letting you write asynchronous code that looks synchronous. This guarantees that each operation starts only after the previous one has completed successfully.\",\"In JavaScript, operations such as network requests, reading files or querying databases are often performed asynchronously. That means they do not block the main thread, but they may complete in an arbitrary order. Many scenarios, however, require a strict sequence: for example, you first need to get the user's data, then their orders, and then send a notification. Particular patterns are used to control that order.\\nPromise chaining\\nPromises represent the future result of an asynchronous operation. The `.then()` method returns a new promise, which lets you chain calls. Each subsequent `.then` runs only after the promise from the previous step settles. Errors can be handled with `.catch()` at the end of the chain.\\n\\nThe async/await syntax\\nThe `async` and `await` keywords provide a more declarative way of working with asynchronous code. A function declared `async` always returns a promise. Inside it, `await` suspends execution until a promise settles, making the flow of execution sequential and comprehensible.\\n\\nThis approach is widely used when working with APIs, processing data in stages, or in any scenario where the result of one operation is the input for the next. It makes the code less prone to errors associated with race conditions.\\nConclusion: Use async/await for new code, since it improves readability and simplifies error handling via try/catch. Promise chaining remains a fundamental pattern and is useful, for example, when composing operations dynamically.\",\"function fetchUser(id) {\\n  return fetch(`/api/users/${id}`).then(res => res.json());\\n}\\n\\nfetchUser(1)\\n  .then(user => {\\n    console.log('User:', user);\\n    return fetch(`/api/orders?userId=${user.id}`); // Return a new promise\\n  })\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(orders => {\\n    console.log('Orders:', orders);\\n    // The next operation...\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    console.error('Error in sequence:', error);\\n  });\\n\\nasync function processUserData(userId) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`).then(res => res.json());\\n    console.log('User:', user);\\n\\n    const ordersResponse = await fetch(`/api/orders?userId=${user.id}`);\\n    const orders = await ordersResponse.json();\\n    console.log('Orders:', orders);\\n\\n    // Other operations can then be performed sequentially\\n    return orders;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Failed to process data:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nprocessUserData(1);\"],[1611,\"When asynchronous operations run in parallel, the main problem is a race condition, where the result depends on the order in which the threads execute. That can lead to corrupted data. Another problem is a deadlock, where threads wait for each other's resources and the program hangs. There can also be problems synchronising access to shared resources, and debugging becomes harder because of the non-deterministic behaviour.\",\"Running asynchronous operations in parallel lets you use system resources efficiently, but introduces a number of classic concurrency problems. These problems arise when several threads or tasks access shared data or resources without proper coordination.\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Race condition: The most frequent problem. It arises when the result of a program's execution depends on the order in which the parallel operations run. For example, if two threads simultaneously read, modify and write the value of a shared variable, the final value may be incorrect.\\n- Deadlock: A situation where two or more threads are blocked, waiting for resources held by each other. The program then \\\"hangs\\\".\\n- Starvation: A thread cannot gain access to a shared resource because other threads constantly monopolise it.\\n- Priority inversion: A low-priority thread holds a resource needed by a high-priority thread, which blocks an important task from running.\\nAn example of a race condition in JavaScript\\nHere is a simple example demonstrating the problem when a shared resource is modified asynchronously without synchronisation:\\n\\nSince the read and write operations are separated by an asynchronous pause, several calls to the function may read the same initial value and then overwrite the result, which leads to some increments being lost.\\nHow and where an understanding of these problems is applied\\nThese problems are relevant in any multithreaded or asynchronous environment: web servers (Node.js, Django, Spring), desktop applications, systems programming, databases. Synchronisation mechanisms are used to solve them: mutexes, semaphores, locks, atomic operations, as well as paradigms that avoid shared mutable state (for example, immutable data structures or the actor model).\\nConclusion: Understanding concurrency problems is critically important when developing high-load, responsive applications. To prevent them you must design access to shared resources carefully, using synchronisation primitives or architectural approaches that minimise shared state.\",\"let counter = 0;\\nasync function increment() {\\n    // Read the current value\\n    let current = counter;\\n    // Simulate an asynchronous operation (for example, an API request)\\n    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, Math.random() * 10));\\n    // Write the incremented value\\n    counter = current + 1;\\n}\\n// Start 10 parallel calls\\nPromise.all(Array(10).fill().map(() => increment()))\\n    .then(() => console.log('Final counter:', counter)); // Often prints less than 10!\"],[1612,\"There are several ways to get an object's keys in JavaScript. Object.keys() returns an array of string keys of the enumerable properties. Object.getOwnPropertyNames() returns all the string keys, including non-enumerable ones. A for...in loop iterates over the enumerable keys, including inherited ones. Reflect.ownKeys() returns all the keys, including symbol ones. Object.getOwnPropertySymbols() returns only the symbol keys.\",\"In JavaScript objects are collections of properties, each of which has a key (a name) and a value. There are several built-in methods for getting the list of an object's keys, each with its own particular behaviour and area of application.\\nThe main methods\\n\\n- Object.keys(obj) — returns an array of string keys of the object's own enumerable properties. This is the most common way, used to iterate over an object's properties.\\n- Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj) — returns an array of all the string keys of the object's own properties, including non-enumerable ones (for example, properties created with the flag enumerable: false).\\n- The for...in loop — iterates over all the object's enumerable properties, including those inherited from the prototype chain. A hasOwnProperty check is usually used inside the loop to filter down to own properties only.\\n- Reflect.ownKeys(obj) — returns an array of all the keys of the object's own properties, including both string and symbol keys. This is the most complete method.\\n- Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(obj) — returns an array of only the symbol keys of the object's own properties.\\nCode examples\\nConsider an object with different kinds of properties:\\n\\nWhere they are used\\nThe choice of method depends on the task. `Object.keys()` is ideal for most cases where you need to work only with an object's enumerable data, for example for serialisation or display. `for...in` with a `hasOwnProperty` check is used more rarely, but can be useful when analysing the prototype chain. `Object.getOwnPropertyNames()` and `Reflect.ownKeys()` are needed for low-level operations, metaprogramming, or when working with libraries that define hidden properties. `Object.getOwnPropertySymbols()` is specific to working with symbols, which are often used to create unique identifiers or to hide internal implementation details.\\nConclusion: Use `Object.keys()` for everyday property iteration. When you need to get every property, including hidden or symbol ones, choose `Reflect.ownKeys()` or the more specific methods.\",\"const obj = {\\n  name: 'John',\\n  age: 30,\\n  [Symbol('id')]: 123\\n};\\n\\n// Make a property non-enumerable\\nObject.defineProperty(obj, 'hidden', {\\n  value: 'secret',\\n  enumerable: false\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(obj)); // ['name', 'age']\\nconsole.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj)); // ['name', 'age', 'hidden']\\nconsole.log(Object.getOwnPropertySymbols(obj)); // [Symbol(id)]\\nconsole.log(Reflect.ownKeys(obj)); // ['name', 'age', 'hidden', Symbol(id)]\\n\\n// for...in\\nfor (let key in obj) {\\n  console.log(key); // 'name', 'age'\\n}\"],[1613,\"A re-render in React is the process where a component calls its render function again to update the user interface. It is triggered when the component's state changes or when it receives new props. React compares the result of the new render with the previous one (the virtual DOM) and makes the minimum necessary changes to the real DOM. By default, a re-render of a parent component causes a re-render of all its child components.\",\"A re-render is React's key mechanism for updating the user interface in response to changes in data. It does not always mean direct manipulation of the browser's real DOM tree, which is what makes it efficient.\\n\\nWhat triggers a re-render?\\n\\n- A change to the component's state via `setState` (or the `useState` hook).\\n\\n- A change to the props passed to the component from its parent.\\n\\n- A change to a context the component is subscribed to.\\n\\n- A re-render of the parent component (unless optimisation measures have been taken).\\n\\nThe re-render process\\n\\nWhen a re-render starts, React calls the component's render function (the render method, or the body of a functional component). That function returns a new description of the UI based on JSX, which consists of React element objects (the virtual DOM). React then starts the reconciliation process, comparing the new element tree with the previous one. It determines which parts of the tree actually changed and computes the minimal set of operations needed to update the real DOM. That makes updates fast, even if the application is large.\\n\\nCode example\\n\\nOptimising re-renders\\n\\nTo avoid unnecessary re-renders and improve performance, the following are used:\\n\\n- `React.memo` to memoise functional components.\\n\\n- `useMemo` and `useCallback` to memoise values and functions.\\n\\n- Structuring state correctly so that changes affect only the components that need them.\\n\\nSummary: Understanding re-rendering is critically important for writing performant React applications. It should be approached deliberately, using optimisations to prevent unnecessary updates of components that have not in fact changed.\",\"import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // State\\n\\n  // This function triggers a re-render on every call\\n  const handleClick = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // A state change -> a re-render\\n  };\\n\\n  // This call happens on every re-render\\n  console.log('Counter rendered, count:', count);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n      <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The parent component\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [message, setMessage] = useState('Hello');\\n  // Changing message triggers a re-render of App, and then of Counter,\\n  // since Counter is its child component.\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Counter />\\n      <button onClick={() => setMessage('Updated')}>Update Parent</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1614,\"A `for...in` loop iterates over all an object's enumerable properties, including those inherited from the prototype chain. To avoid that, you need to check each property inside the loop with the `obj.hasOwnProperty(key)` method. A more modern and safer way is to use `Object.keys(obj)`, `Object.values(obj)` or `Object.entries(obj)`, which return an array of only the object's own enumerable properties.\",\"In JavaScript objects are prototypal in nature, and when iterating over properties with a `for...in` loop you may capture not only the object's own properties but also enumerable properties inherited from its prototype chain. That can lead to unexpected behaviour if you want to work only with the data belonging directly to the object.\\nThe problem with the for...in loop\\nConsider an example where an object has an own property and one inherited from a prototype:\\n\\nAs you can see, the `eats` property from the prototype was also listed.\\nThe solution with hasOwnProperty\\nThe classic way of filtering is to use the `hasOwnProperty` method, which checks whether a property belongs to the object itself rather than to its prototype:\\n\\nThis method is reliable, but requires an extra check inside the loop.\\nThe modern Object methods\\nStarting with ES5, more convenient methods appeared that return only the object's own enumerable properties directly:\\n\\n- `Object.keys(obj)` — returns an array of the keys.\\n- `Object.values(obj)` — returns an array of the values.\\n- `Object.entries(obj)` — returns an array of [key, value] pairs.\\n\\nThese methods do not include properties from the prototype, which makes them preferable for most iteration tasks.\\nWhen to use which?\\n\\n- Use `Object.keys` and its counterparts when you need only the object's own properties. It is cleaner and safer.\\n- A `for...in` loop with a `hasOwnProperty` check can be handy in the rare cases where you also need to analyse the prototype chain, but with filtering.\\n- Remember that `Object.keys` does not return non-enumerable properties (those with the flag `enumerable: false`). To get all own properties, including non-enumerable ones, you can use `Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj)`.\\nConclusion: To iterate safely over only an object's own properties, always prefer the `Object.keys`, `Object.values` or `Object.entries` methods, since they exclude prototype properties by default and make the code more readable and reliable.\",\"const animal = { eats: true };\\nconst rabbit = { jumps: true, name: 'Bunny' };\\n\\nObject.setPrototypeOf(rabbit, animal); // rabbit.__proto__ = animal\\n\\nfor (let key in rabbit) {\\n  console.log(key); // Prints: jumps, name, eats\\n}\\n\\nfor (let key in rabbit) {\\n  if (rabbit.hasOwnProperty(key)) {\\n    console.log(key); // Prints: jumps, name\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(Object.keys(rabbit));   // ['jumps', 'name']\\nconsole.log(Object.values(rabbit)); // [true, 'Bunny']\\nconsole.log(Object.entries(rabbit)); // [['jumps', true], ['name', 'Bunny']]\"],[1615,\"In React, state — usually an array — is used to add and remove list items. To add, you create a new array containing the old items plus the new one, using the spread operator or concat. To remove, you use filter, which returns an array without the item being removed. After the state changes through setState or a state updater function (in functional components), React automatically re-renders the component with the updated list. The key point is to avoid mutating the original array, so that React tracks the changes correctly.\",\"In React, managing dynamic lists is one of the fundamental tasks. Since React relies on a declarative approach, you describe how the UI should look depending on the state, and React takes care of updating the DOM itself. To work with lists, state holds an array of data, and the rendering is done with the map method.\\n\\nThe basic approach with state\\n\\nIn a functional component, use the useState hook to hold the array of items. Initialise it with an empty array or with initial data.\\n\\nAdding an item\\n\\nTo add an item, create a new array containing all the existing items plus the new one. Never modify the original array with push or splice directly, since React will not detect the change. Use the spread operator or concat instead.\\n\\nRemoving an item\\n\\nTo remove an item by index or by a unique identifier (for example an id), use the filter method, which returns a new array without the specified item.\\n\\nRendering the list\\n\\nTo display the list, use map, giving every element a unique `key` (usually an id, or the index if the items are static). The key helps React update the DOM efficiently.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nThis pattern is used everywhere in interfaces: todo lists, shopping baskets, news feeds, data tables. It is important to remember the immutability of state — always create new arrays or objects when updating, so React determines changes correctly and optimises re-renders.\\n\\nConclusion: Use useState to hold the array, the spread operator or concat to add, filter to remove and map to render. This approach gives predictable UI updates and is the standard in React development.\",\"import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoList() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState(['Item 1', 'Item 2']);\\n  // ... the add/remove logic\\n}\\n\\nconst addItem = (newItem) => {\\n  setItems([...items, newItem]); // The spread operator\\n  // Or: setItems(items.concat(newItem));\\n};\\n\\n// An example call: addItem('Item 3');\\n\\nconst removeItem = (id) => {\\n  setItems(items.filter(item => item.id !== id));\\n};\\n\\n// If there is no id, you can remove by index (less reliable):\\n// const removeItemByIndex = (index) => {\\n//   setItems(items.filter((_, i) => i !== index));\\n// };\\n\\nreturn (\\n  <ul>\\n    {items.map((item, index) => (\\n      <li key={item.id || index}>\\n        {item.name}\\n        <button onClick={() => removeItem(item.id)}>Delete</button>\\n      </li>\\n    ))}\\n  </ul>\\n);\"],[1616,\"The try/catch construct works synchronously and catches errors only in the current block of execution. The function passed to setTimeout runs later, in a different turn of the event loop, when the try/catch context has already finished. So the error arises outside the catch block's field of view. To handle such errors you need to put the try/catch inside the asynchronous function itself, or use other mechanisms, for example 'unhandledrejection' event handlers for promises.\",\"The `try/catch` construct in JavaScript is intended for synchronous error handling. It works within the current synchronous flow of execution, capturing exceptions that arise directly inside the `try` block in the very same tick of the event loop.\\n\\nHow setTimeout and the event loop work\\n\\nThe `setTimeout` function is asynchronous. It does not run the callback passed to it immediately but schedules its execution for the future. Once the specified delay has elapsed, the callback is placed in the task queue. The event loop will take it from there and run it only when the main call stack is empty. That happens long after all the synchronous code, including the `try/catch` block, has finished its work.\\n\\nWhy the error is not caught\\n\\nConsider an example:\\n\\nHere `setTimeout` is called synchronously and completes successfully (it simply schedules a task). The callback with the `throw` runs asynchronously, a second later, in a completely new execution context. By that time control has long since left the `try` block, so catching that error with a standard `catch` is impossible.\\n\\nHow to handle asynchronous errors properly\\n\\n- Put the `try/catch` inside the asynchronous function:\\n\\n- Use promises with `.catch()`:\\n\\n- For global interception of unhandled errors you can use `window.onerror` (in the browser) or `process.on('uncaughtException')` (in Node.js).\\n\\nConclusion: Use `try/catch` only for synchronous code. To handle errors in asynchronous operations (timeouts, promises, events), apply the corresponding asynchronous mechanisms — promises' `.catch()` handlers — or wrap the risky code in a `try/catch` directly inside the asynchronous callback.\",\"try {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    throw new Error('An error inside the timeout!');\\n  }, 1000);\\n} catch (error) {\\n  // This block will NOT run\\n  console.log('Error caught:', error);\\n}\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  try {\\n    throw new Error('Now it will be caught!');\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.log('Error caught inside:', error.message);\\n  }\\n}, 1000);\\n\\nnew Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    reject(new Error('An error in the promise'));\\n  }, 1000);\\n}).catch(error => {\\n  console.log('Caught via the promise catch:', error.message);\\n});\"],[1617,\"The useEffect hook expects its callback either to return undefined or to return a cleanup function. An asynchronous function always returns a Promise, which breaks that expectation and can lead to unpredictable behaviour. React cannot correctly handle a Promise as the return value for cleaning up an effect. Instead, the asynchronous logic should be placed inside the synchronous useEffect callback, for example by declaring and calling an asynchronous function inside it.\",\"The `useEffect` hook is a fundamental tool in React for performing side effects, such as loading data, subscribing to events or manually changing the DOM. Its internal logic expects the function passed as the first argument either to return nothing (that is, `undefined`) or to return another function, which will be run to clean up the effect before the next render or when the component unmounts.\\n\\nThe problem with asynchronous functions\\n\\nAn asynchronous function, declared with the `async` keyword, always returns a `Promise` object. If React allowed an `async` function to be passed directly to `useEffect`, it would receive a Promise as the return value. That would break the hook's contract, because:\\n\\n- React cannot use a Promise as a cleanup function. Cleanup must be a synchronous operation that releases resources immediately.\\n\\n- React does not \\\"wait\\\" for a Promise inside an effect to settle. Effects run synchronously after the render, and React moves on to the next stage of the life cycle without waiting for asynchronous operations.\\n\\n- It can lead to memory leaks or a race condition if the component unmounts before the asynchronous operation finishes.\\n\\nThe right approach\\n\\nInstead, the asynchronous logic should be encapsulated inside the synchronous `useEffect` callback. The most common way is to declare and immediately invoke an asynchronous function (an IIFE), or to create a named function and call it.\\n\\nWhere this applies\\n\\nThis pattern is critically important when working with any asynchronous operations in React components: loading data from an API, working with timers (`setTimeout`/`setInterval`), subscribing to external events or to a WebSocket. Understanding this limitation helps you avoid hidden bugs and manage the component's life cycle correctly.\\n\\nConclusion: You cannot make `useEffect` an asynchronous function directly, because that breaks the return contract React expects (either undefined or a cleanup function). Always place asynchronous logic inside the effect's synchronous callback, which ensures cleanup works correctly and prevents memory leaks.\",\"useEffect(() => {\\n    // Declare an asynchronous function inside the effect\\n    const fetchData = async () => {\\n        try {\\n            const response = await fetch('/api/data');\\n            const data = await response.json();\\n            setData(data);\\n        } catch (error) {\\n            setError(error.message);\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    // Call it\\n    fetchData();\\n\\n    // If needed, return a synchronous cleanup function\\n    return () => {\\n        // Cancelling the request or clearing subscriptions\\n    };\\n}, []); // The dependencies\"],[1618,\"To run several asynchronous requests in parallel in JavaScript, the `Promise.all()` method is used. It takes an array of promises and returns a new promise that resolves when all the promises passed in have completed successfully, or rejects if at least one of them fails. This lets you send the requests simultaneously rather than sequentially, which significantly speeds up getting the data. For example, you can send several fetch requests to different APIs and wait for all the responses at once.\",\"In modern web development there is often a need to perform several independent asynchronous operations (for example, API requests) at the same time, to reduce the total waiting time. JavaScript provides several tools for this, the most basic and widespread of which is `Promise.all()`.\\n\\nPromise.all()\\n\\nThis static method takes an iterable collection (most often an array) of promises and returns a single new promise. That new promise is fulfilled only when every promise in the array has been fulfilled successfully. Its result is an array of each promise's result, in the same order as they were passed in. If at least one of the promises is rejected, the promise from `Promise.all()` is immediately rejected with the reason of the first rejected promise.\\n\\nAn example of use with fetch\\n\\nImagine we need to get data about a user and their posts from the JSONPlaceholder API.\\n\\nAlternative methods\\n\\nSometimes more flexible control is required:\\n\\n- Promise.allSettled(): Waits for all the promises to settle, regardless of their status (success or failure). Returns an array of objects with each promise's result, which is useful for analysing partially failed operations.\\n\\n- Promise.race(): Returns a promise that resolves or rejects with the result of the first of the promises passed in to settle.\\n\\n- Promise.any(): Returns a promise that resolves with the result of the first promise to be fulfilled successfully. It rejects only if every promise is rejected.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nParallel requests are critically important for optimising front-end performance. They are used when:\\n\\n- Loading data for different parts of a page (the user's profile, the news feed, notifications).\\n\\n- Sending several analytics events.\\n\\n- Checking the availability of several services (a health check).\\n\\n- Aggregating data from several API sources.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.all()` to run independent asynchronous operations in parallel when you need the results of all of them. It is the standard, effective pattern for reducing waiting time in web applications.\",\"async function fetchUserAndPosts(userId) {\\n  const userPromise = fetch(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/${userId}`);\\n  const postsPromise = fetch(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?userId=${userId}`);\\n\\n  try {\\n    // Both requests start in parallel\\n    const [userResponse, postsResponse] = await Promise.all([userPromise, postsPromise]);\\n\\n    const user = await userResponse.json();\\n    const posts = await postsPromise.json();\\n\\n    console.log('User:', user.name);\\n    console.log('Number of posts:', posts.length);\\n    return { user, posts };\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('One of the requests failed:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfetchUserAndPosts(1);\"],[1619,\"Mutating state directly breaks the principle of immutability, which is the foundation of predictability in modern UI frameworks. When an object is mutated directly, the reference to it stays the same and the system cannot detect that the state has changed. The result is that components do not re-render, caches are not invalidated and the history of changes is lost. Instead you need to create new objects or arrays, for example using the spread operator or methods such as map and filter.\",\"Mutating state directly means changing values inside an existing object or array without creating a new reference. In the context of React, Redux and many other state-management systems this is considered an anti-pattern, because it breaks the principle of immutability.\\n\\nWhy is it a problem?\\n\\n- Broken reactivity: React and similar libraries rely on comparing references (a shallow comparison) to determine whether state has changed. If you mutate an object in place, its reference stays the same and React \\\"does not see\\\" the change, which leads to missed re-renders.\\n\\n- Difficulty of debugging: When state is mutated in different parts of the application, it becomes extremely hard to track where and when the change happened, especially in asynchronous code. That makes reproducing bugs harder.\\n\\n- PureComponent and React.memo stop working: These optimisations depend on a shallow comparison of props. If you pass a mutated object, the comparison does not fire, and the component may fail to update when it should, or update when it should not.\\n\\n- Problems with history and undo: In libraries such as Redux, the devtools and middleware (for example, redux-thunk) rely on every state being a new immutable snapshot. Mutation breaks the ability to \\\"time travel\\\" and to log.\\n\\nPractical example\\n\\nConsider a typical mistake when updating an array in a React component:\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nThe principle of immutable state is a cornerstone not only in React and Redux but also in libraries for global state (MobX, although it uses observable objects, likewise recommends careful mutations), in functional programming, and even in systems like Immer, which provide an API for writing mutable-looking code but generate new immutable structures under the hood.\\n\\nConclusion: Avoid mutating state directly, to ensure predictability, simplify debugging and guarantee that reactive systems and their optimisations work correctly. Always create new objects or arrays when updating.\",\"// ❌ BAD: direct mutation\\nconst [todos, setTodos] = useState([{ id: 1, text: 'Learn React' }]);\\nconst addTodoBad = (newTodo) => {\\n  todos.push(newTodo); // Mutating the existing array!\\n  setTodos(todos); // The reference did not change, React may ignore the update\\n};\\n\\n// ✅ GOOD: creating a new array\\nconst addTodoGood = (newTodo) => {\\n  setTodos([...todos, newTodo]); // A new array, a new reference\\n};\\n\\n// An example with an object\\nconst [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });\\n// ❌ user.age = 31;\\n// ✅ setUser({ ...user, age: 31 });\"],[1620,\"A deep comparison of objects is a recursive check for the equality of all the properties of two objects, including nested objects and arrays. Unlike a simple comparison (`==` or `===`), which for objects checks references, deep equal compares the actual values. The basic algorithm: check the types; if they are primitives, compare with `===`; if they are objects, recursively compare their keys and values. It is important to handle the edge cases: null, Date, RegExp, circular references.\",\"A deep comparison (deep equal) is an operation that determines whether two objects are identical in structure and in the value of all their properties, including nested objects and arrays. This is a fundamental task in JavaScript, since the standard equality operators (==, ===) for objects compare only references, not contents.\\n\\nThe basic algorithm\\n\\nThe algorithm is usually implemented recursively and involves the following steps:\\n\\n- Compare the types of the operands. If the types differ, the objects are not equal.\\n\\n- If both operands are primitives (or null/undefined), use strict equality (===).\\n\\n- If both are objects (including arrays):\\n\\n- Check whether they are the same object (reference equality) — this speeds the check up.\\n\\n- Compare the number of keys (properties). If they differ, the objects are not equal.\\n\\n- For each key, call deep equal recursively on the values.\\n\\nSpecial cases and optimisations\\n\\nA naive implementation can run into problems:\\n\\n- Circular references: an object refers to itself through a chain of properties. You need to track the pairs of objects already visited to avoid infinite recursion (for example, with a WeakMap).\\n\\n- Special objects: Date, RegExp, Map and Set require special comparison (for example, for Date you compare the timestamp).\\n\\n- Performance: for large objects the recursion can be deep; sometimes it is useful to compare the simple properties first.\\n\\nAn example implementation (basic, without handling every edge case)\\n\\nIn real projects ready-made library functions are often used, such as `_.isEqual` from Lodash, which handle all the edge cases thoroughly.\\n\\nConclusion: A deep comparison is needed when working with immutable state (for example, in Redux), for writing tests, or when comparing configuration objects. For simple cases you can write your own function, but in production it is safer to use proven libraries.\",\"function deepEqual(obj1, obj2) {\\n  // Comparing primitives and references\\n  if (obj1 === obj2) return true;\\n  \\n  // Checking the types\\n  if (typeof obj1 !== 'object' || obj1 === null ||\\n      typeof obj2 !== 'object' || obj2 === null) {\\n    return false;\\n  }\\n  \\n  const keys1 = Object.keys(obj1);\\n  const keys2 = Object.keys(obj2);\\n  \\n  if (keys1.length !== keys2.length) return false;\\n  \\n  for (let key of keys1) {\\n    if (!keys2.includes(key) || !deepEqual(obj1[key], obj2[key])) {\\n      return false;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return true;\\n}\\n\\n// Example usage\\nconst a = { x: 1, y: { z: 2 } };\\nconst b = { x: 1, y: { z: 2 } };\\nconsole.log(deepEqual(a, b)); // true\\nconsole.log(deepEqual(a, { x: 1, y: { z: 3 } })); // false\"],[1621,\"A callback function can work incorrectly in an asynchronous context because of closures and race conditions. If the callback uses variables from an outer scope, their value may change before the callback is invoked, since the asynchronous operation completes later. For example, in a loop with asynchronous calls every callback may receive the same value from the last iteration. That happens because the loop variable changes synchronously while the callbacks are invoked asynchronously.\",\"Callback functions are functions passed as arguments to other functions to be executed after some operation, often an asynchronous one, completes. The main problem arises when a callback captures (closes over) variables from an outer lexical scope and those variables change before the moment the callback is invoked.\\n\\nThe problem with closures and loops\\n\\nThe classic example is using a `for` loop with asynchronous operations inside it. The loop variable is declared with `var`, which has function scope. By the time the callback functions are invoked the loop has already finished, and every callback refers to the same final value of the variable.\\n\\nHere `setTimeout` is an asynchronous function, and its callback runs after the loop has finished. By that moment the variable `i` equals 3, and all three callbacks print that value.\\n\\nRace conditions\\n\\nAnother problem is a race condition, where several asynchronous operations try to change shared data and the result depends on the order in which they finish. If a callback reads or changes a shared variable, the final state can be unpredictable.\\n\\nSolving the problems\\n\\nTo avoid problems with closures you can:\\n\\n- Use `let` or `const` for block scope (in modern JavaScript).\\n\\n- Create a new scope for each iteration, for example with an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression).\\n\\n- Pass the value as a function parameter.\\n\\nTo fight race conditions you should use synchronisation mechanisms, such as promises with `async/await`, queues or mutexes, to guarantee the order of execution or the atomicity of operations.\\n\\nConclusion: Callbacks can work incorrectly in an asynchronous context because of changes to the captured variables and because of race conditions. To write reliable asynchronous code it is important to understand closures and to use modern approaches (promises, async/await) or to isolate the data for each callback carefully.\",\"for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(function() {\\n    console.log(i); // Always prints 3\\n  }, 100);\\n}\\n\\nlet balance = 100;\\n\\nfunction asyncAdd(amount, callback) {\\n  setTimeout(() => {\\n    balance += amount;\\n    callback();\\n  }, Math.random() * 100);\\n}\\n\\nasyncAdd(10, () => console.log('Balance after first:', balance));\\nasyncAdd(20, () => console.log('Balance after second:', balance));\\n// The order of the output and the final value of balance are unpredictable.\\n\\n// The solution with let\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(function() {\\n    console.log(i); // Prints 0, 1, 2\\n  }, 100);\\n}\\n\\n// The solution with an IIFE\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  (function(j) {\\n    setTimeout(function() {\\n      console.log(j); // Prints 0, 1, 2\\n    }, 100);\\n  })(i);\\n}\"],[1622,\"Exceptions in JavaScript are an error-handling mechanism that lets you interrupt the normal execution of a program when a problem occurs and transfer control to a special handler. The `try...catch` construct is used to handle exceptions: code that may raise an error is placed in the `try` block, and the code to handle it in the `catch` block. You can raise your own exceptions with the `throw` operator, passing it any object, though usually it is an instance of Error. The `finally` block always runs, regardless of whether there was an exception, and is used to release resources.\",\"Exceptions in JavaScript are special objects that signal an error during a program's execution. When an exception occurs, the normal flow of execution is interrupted and JavaScript looks for the nearest exception handler (a `catch` block). If no handler is found, the program terminates with an error, which can be critical for the user experience. That is why competent exception handling is the key to building reliable applications.\\n\\nThe basic try...catch...finally syntax\\n\\nThe main tool for working with exceptions is the `try...catch...finally` construct.\\n\\nCreating your own exceptions\\n\\nYou can raise exceptions yourself with the `throw` operator. Although you can throw any value (a string, a number), it is recommended to use the built-in or your own error classes, inheriting from `Error`.\\n\\nThe built-in error types\\n\\nJavaScript provides several built-in error types for specific cases:\\n\\n- `SyntaxError` — a syntax error.\\n\\n- `TypeError` — an operation applied to a value of the wrong type.\\n\\n- `ReferenceError` — a reference to a non-existent variable.\\n\\n- `RangeError` — a value outside the permitted range.\\n\\n- `URIError` — an error in the URI handling functions.\\n\\nUsing specific types helps diagnose problems more precisely.\\n\\nWhere and how to apply exception handling\\n\\nExceptions should be used to handle unforeseen but possible runtime errors, such as:\\n\\n- A network error in a request to an API.\\n\\n- A data parsing error (for example, malformed JSON).\\n\\n- Errors in user input that cannot be checked statically.\\n\\nIt is important not to abuse exceptions to control a program's ordinary logic — that is an anti-pattern. Use conditional statements for expected branching.\\n\\nConclusion: The exception mechanism in JavaScript is a powerful tool for handling runtime errors. It is worth applying to isolate and handle unpredictable failures, to prevent the program from crashing and to give the user a comprehensible error message. Always use the `finally` block to release resources (for example, closing file descriptors or connections).\",\"try {\\n  // Code that may throw an exception\\n  const data = JSON.parse(invalidJsonString);\\n} catch (error) {\\n  // Handling the error\\n  console.error('JSON parsing error:', error.message);\\n} finally {\\n  // This block always runs\\n  console.log('The operation has finished.');\\n}\\n\\nfunction divide(a, b) {\\n  if (b === 0) {\\n    throw new Error('Division by zero is not possible.');\\n  }\\n  return a / b;\\n}\\n\\ntry {\\n  const result = divide(10, 0);\\n} catch (err) {\\n  console.log(err.message); // \\\"Division by zero is not possible.\\\"\\n}\"],[1623,\"Effect cleanup in React is organised with the cleanup function returned from the useEffect hook. That function runs before the effect runs again (when the dependencies change) and before the component unmounts. This lets you cancel subscriptions, timers or network requests, preventing memory leaks and errors from updating the state of a component that no longer exists. Always return a cleanup function if the effect creates something that has to be torn down.\",\"The `useEffect` hook in React lets you perform side effects in functional components. Those effects often create resources, such as event subscriptions, timers or connections to a server, which have to be released when the component is removed from the DOM, or before the effect runs again, so as to avoid memory leaks and unexpected behaviour.\\nHow the cleanup function works\\nThe cleanup function is a function you return from the callback passed to `useEffect`. React calls it in two cases:\\n\\n- Before the effect runs again (if the values in the dependency array changed).\\n- Immediately before the component unmounts.\\nThis guarantees that resources created in the previous render are removed correctly before new ones are created.\\nPractical examples\\nConsider an example with an event subscription and a timer:\\n\\nIn this example the cleanup function removes the event handler and stops the interval. Without it, every update of the component would create new subscriptions and timers, which would lead to a memory leak and incorrect behaviour (for example, the counter would speed up).\\nWhen and where this applies\\nCleanup functions are necessary for any effect that:\\n\\n- Creates subscriptions (WebSocket, DOM events, observers).\\n- Starts asynchronous operations that may try to update state after the component unmounts.\\n- Uses timers (`setInterval`, `setTimeout`).\\n- Performs requests to an API that can be cancelled (for example, with `AbortController`).\\nFor asynchronous operations such as fetch requests, `AbortController` is often used inside the cleanup function to cancel the request if the component unmounts before it finishes.\\nConclusion: Always return a cleanup function from `useEffect` if your effect creates resources that have to be released. This is a key practice for writing reliable, leak-free React applications, especially when working with external APIs, events or timers.\",\"import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ExampleComponent() {\\n  const [position, setPosition] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // Effect 1: subscribing to the mousemove event\\n    const handleMouseMove = (e) => {\\n      setPosition({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });\\n    };\\n    window.addEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\\n\\n    // Effect 2: starting an interval\\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The cleanup function for both effects\\n    return () => {\\n      window.removeEventListener('mousemove', handleMouseMove);\\n      clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty dependency array = the effect runs only on mount\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Mouse position: {position.x}, {position.y}</p>\\n      <p>Counter: {count}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1624,\"Callback-based code can be rewritten in ES6 using promises and async/await. First wrap the asynchronous function that takes a callback in a promise. Then use `.then()` and `.catch()` chains to handle success and errors. For maximum readability apply async/await, which lets you write asynchronous code almost like synchronous code. This eliminates \\\"callback hell\\\" and simplifies debugging.\",\"Moving from callbacks to promises and async/await is a key step in modernising JavaScript code. Callbacks lead to nested structures (\\\"callback hell\\\") that are hard to read and maintain. Promises provide a more structured way of handling asynchronous operations, and async/await makes the code linear and intuitive.\\n\\nStep 1: wrapping a callback in a promise\\n\\nAny function that uses a callback in the `(error, result) => {}` style can be wrapped in a promise. That creates the foundation for further transformations.\\n\\nStep 2: using promise chains\\n\\nInstead of nested callbacks, use the `.then()` and `.catch()` methods. That makes the flow of execution more explicit.\\n\\nStep 3: applying async/await\\n\\nThe `async` and `await` keywords let you work with promises as though the code were synchronous. A function declared `async` always returns a promise, and `await` suspends execution until a promise settles.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\nThis approach is used wherever there are asynchronous operations: working with the file system in Node.js, network requests (APIs), interacting with databases, timers. Modern libraries (for example, for HTTP or databases) often already return promises, but legacy code or some native Node.js APIs (for example, `fs.readFile` without promises) require this kind of conversion.\\n\\nConclusion: Rewrite callback-based code in async/await to improve readability, simplify error handling (via try/catch) and make maintenance easier. This is especially useful in large projects, where clarity in the logic of the flow of execution matters.\",\"// The original function with a callback\\nfunction readFileCallback(path, callback) {\\n    // Simulating an asynchronous read\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n        const data = 'file content';\\n        callback(null, data); // The first argument is the error\\n    }, 100);\\n}\\n\\n// Wrapping it in a promise\\nfunction readFilePromise(path) {\\n    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n        readFileCallback(path, (err, data) => {\\n            if (err) reject(err);\\n            else resolve(data);\\n        });\\n    });\\n}\\n\\n// The callback version (bad)\\nreadFileCallback('file1.txt', (err, data1) => {\\n    if (err) console.error(err);\\n    else {\\n        processData(data1, (err, result) => {\\n            if (err) console.error(err);\\n            else console.log(result);\\n        });\\n    }\\n});\\n\\n// The promise version (better)\\nreadFilePromise('file1.txt')\\n    .then(data1 => processDataPromise(data1))\\n    .then(result => console.log(result))\\n    .catch(err => console.error(err));\\n\\n// The async/await version (the most readable)\\nasync function processFiles() {\\n    try {\\n        const data1 = await readFilePromise('file1.txt');\\n        const result = await processDataPromise(data1);\\n        console.log(result);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n        console.error(err);\\n    }\\n}\\nprocessFiles();\"],[1625,\"In JavaScript data types are divided into two main groups: primitive and reference. Primitive types store the value directly, and they are: string, number, boolean, undefined, null, symbol and bigint. Reference types (objects, arrays, functions) store a reference to a value in memory. The key difference is in how they are copied and compared: primitives are copied by value, while reference types are copied by reference.\",\"In JavaScript every value has a particular data type, and understanding their classification is critically important for writing predictable code. The types are divided into two fundamental groups: primitive and reference. This division is based on the way the data is stored in and passed through memory.\\nPrimitive types\\nPrimitive types are indivisible, immutable values stored directly in the variable. When a variable holding a primitive value is copied, an independent copy of that value is created. The primitives are:\\n\\n- String: text, for example `'Hello'`.\\n- Number: whole and fractional numbers, including `Infinity` and `NaN`.\\n- Boolean: the logical values `true` and `false`.\\n- Undefined: the value of an undefined variable.\\n- Null: the special value for \\\"nothing\\\" or \\\"empty\\\".\\n- Symbol (ES6): unique identifiers.\\n- BigInt (ES2020): integers of arbitrary length.\\nReference types\\nReference types are objects (in the broad sense). A variable containing an object, an array or a function stores not the value itself but a reference (an address in memory) to it. When such a variable is copied, only the reference is copied, not the object itself, so both variables start pointing at the same data in memory.\\n\\nPractical application and checking\\nThe `typeof` operator helps determine a type, but for `null` it returns `'object'` (a historical quirk), and for arrays and `null` additional checks are needed. Understanding the difference between the groups prevents errors in comparisons (use strict equality `===`) and in object mutations.\\nConclusion: Knowing the division into primitives and reference types is essential for managing data correctly, especially when working with functions, changing state and comparing values in JavaScript.\",\"// An example with a primitive (copying the value)\\nlet a = 5;\\nlet b = a; // b receives a copy of the value 5\\nb = 10;\\nconsole.log(a); // 5, a has not changed\\n\\n// An example with a reference type (copying the reference)\\nlet obj1 = { name: 'John' };\\nlet obj2 = obj1; // obj2 receives a reference to the same object\\nobj2.name = 'Jane';\\nconsole.log(obj1.name); // 'Jane', the object was changed through both references\"],[1626,\"An application interacts with the backend through various protocols, each of which solves particular problems. A REST API uses the standard HTTP methods (GET, POST) to exchange data in JSON format; this is the classic approach for most operations. WebSocket establishes a persistent two-way connection, ideally suited to chats or stock tickers in real time. SignalR is a library from Microsoft that abstracts the choice of transport (WebSocket, Server-Sent Events, long polling), simplifying the development of real-time features for .NET applications.\",\"Interaction between a client application (the front end) and the server side (the back end) is the foundation of modern web and mobile applications. The choice of technology depends on the data requirements: whether you need a simple download of information, instant updates, or two-way communication.\\nREST API\\nREST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style that uses the standard HTTP methods. Every request is independent and contains all the information needed to process it. Data is usually transferred in JSON or XML format.\\n\\n- Use: CRUD operations (creating, reading, updating, deleting data), loading static content, login forms.\\n- Example: Getting a list of users.\\n\\nWebSocket\\nWebSocket is a communication protocol on top of TCP that provides a full-duplex (two-way) channel between the client and the server. Once the connection (the handshake) is established, data can be transferred in both directions with minimal overhead.\\n\\n- Use: Online chats, real-time notifications, multiplayer games, financial tickers.\\n- Example: A simple chat on the client.\\n\\nSignalR\\nSignalR is a library for ASP.NET that simplifies adding real-time features. It automatically chooses the best available transport (WebSocket, Server-Sent Events, long polling) and provides a high-level API for managing connections and broadcasting messages to groups of clients.\\n\\n- Use: Applications on the .NET stack that require real-time updates: dashboards, collaborative tools, live alerts.\\n- Example: A server hub in C# (simplified).\\n\\nConclusion: Use a REST API for most standard data operations, WebSocket when minimal latency and a constant stream of data are critical, and SignalR for simplified development of real-time features in the .NET ecosystem.\",\"// An example request in JavaScript using fetch\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/users')\\n  .then(response => response.json())\\n  .then(data => console.log(data));\\n// The server returns an array of users in JSON.\\n\\n// The client side in JavaScript\\nconst socket = new WebSocket('wss://echo.websocket.org');\\nsocket.onopen = () => socket.send('Hello!');\\nsocket.onmessage = event => console.log('Message:', event.data);\\n\\n// An example hub on the server (ASP.NET Core)\\npublic class ChatHub : Hub\\n{\\n    public async Task SendMessage(string user, string message)\\n    {\\n        // Broadcasting the message to all clients\\n        await Clients.All.SendAsync(\\\"ReceiveMessage\\\", user, message);\\n    }\\n}\"],[1627,\"The value of `this` in JavaScript can be changed in several ways. The `call` and `apply` methods invoke a function with an explicitly specified `this` context and arguments. The `bind` method creates a new function permanently bound to the specified `this` context. Arrow functions have no `this` of their own and inherit it from the surrounding lexical scope. `this` is also determined when a function is called as a method of an object.\",\"In JavaScript the value of `this` is determined dynamically at the moment a function is called. There are several ways to control that value explicitly, which is critically important for working with object methods, event handlers and asynchronous code.\\nThe call and apply methods\\nThe `call` and `apply` methods let you invoke a function immediately, explicitly specifying what `this` will refer to inside it. The only difference is the way the arguments are passed: `call` takes the arguments as a list, `apply` as an array.\\n\\nThe bind method\\nThe `bind` method creates a new function that will have a firmly bound value of `this`, regardless of how it is called later. This is useful for passing methods as callbacks.\\n\\nArrow functions\\nArrow functions (`=>`) have no `this` of their own. They capture the value of `this` from the surrounding lexical scope (for example, from an outer function). That makes them convenient for use inside class methods or with callbacks, where the context needs to be preserved.\\n\\nCalling as a method of an object\\nWhen a function is called as a method of an object (via a dot or square brackets), `this` is automatically set to that object.\\n\\nConclusion: Controlling `this` explicitly through `call`, `apply` and `bind` is necessary when a function's call context does not match what is expected, for example when passing methods as callbacks or working with libraries. Arrow functions should be used when the lexical context needs to be preserved, avoiding the loss of `this`.\",\"function greet(greeting) {\\n  console.log(greeting + ', ' + this.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice' };\\n\\n// Using call\\n greet.call(user, 'Hello'); // Output: Hello, Alice\\n\\n// Using apply\\n greet.apply(user, ['Hi']); // Output: Hi, Alice\\n\\nconst user = { name: 'Bob' };\\n\\nfunction showName() {\\n  console.log(this.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst boundFunction = showName.bind(user);\\nboundFunction(); // Output: Bob\\n\\n// Even when called in another context\\nconst anotherObj = { name: 'Eve' };\\nanotherObj.method = boundFunction;\\nanotherObj.method(); // Output: Bob (still bound to user)\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  value: 42,\\n  regularFunc: function() {\\n    console.log(this.value); // this refers to obj\\n  },\\n  arrowFunc: () => {\\n    console.log(this.value); // this is inherited from the outer scope (for example, window/undefined)\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nobj.regularFunc(); // 42\\nobj.arrowFunc(); // undefined (if the code runs in the global scope)\\n\\nconst car = {\\n  brand: 'Toyota',\\n  getBrand: function() {\\n    return this.brand;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nconsole.log(car.getBrand()); // Output: Toyota\"],[1628,\"When nested objects are changed after a shallow copy, the changes affect the original object too, since only the references to the nested objects were copied. A deep copy creates a completely independent copy, including every nested level. This matters for immutable updates in React or when working with state. For deep copying use structuredClone, the JSON methods, or libraries such as Lodash.\",\"In JavaScript objects are passed and copied by reference. That means when you create a variable referring to an existing object, or make a shallow copy (for example, with the spread operator `...` or `Object.assign`), only the top-level properties are copied. Nested objects (or arrays) inside the original object are not copied but merely passed by reference.\\n\\nShallow copying\\n\\nConsider an example using the spread operator:\\n\\nAs you can see, changing `shallowCopy.details` affected `original.details`, because both properties hold a reference to the same object in memory.\\n\\nDeep copying\\n\\nTo avoid this problem you have to create a deep copy, where every nested structure is copied recursively. There are several ways:\\n\\n- structuredClone (modern, supports many types):\\n\\n- JSON.stringify/JSON.parse (works with JSON-serialisable data; loses functions, undefined values and cycles):\\n\\n- Libraries (for example, Lodash's `_.cloneDeep`).\\n\\nWhere does this matter?\\n\\nUnderstanding the difference is critical in several scenarios:\\n\\n- Managing state in React/Redux: state must be updated immutably. An unintended mutation can lead to bugs and incorrect re-renders.\\n\\n- Working with configurations or data: when you need to modify a copy of an object without touching the original settings.\\n\\n- Functional programming: functions that must have no side effects (pure functions).\\n\\nConclusion: always analyse the structure of the data you are copying. For simple objects with no nesting, a shallow copy is enough. If an object contains other objects or arrays and you plan to change them, use deep copying to isolate the changes and avoid hard-to-catch errors.\",\"const original = {\\n  name: 'Test',\\n  details: { id: 1, value: 'secret' }\\n};\\n\\nconst shallowCopy = { ...original };\\n// Changing a top-level property - safe\\nshallowCopy.name = 'Changed';\\nconsole.log(original.name); // 'Test' - unchanged\\n\\n// Changing the nested object - DANGEROUS!\\nshallowCopy.details.value = 'exposed';\\nconsole.log(original.details.value); // 'exposed' - changed in original too!\\n\\nconst deepCopy = structuredClone(original);\\ndeepCopy.details.value = 'safe';\\nconsole.log(original.details.value); // 'secret' - the original is untouched\\n\\nconst deepCopy2 = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\"],[1629,\"Redux becomes inefficient when the application is small and simple and using it adds excessive boilerplate. It can also slow things down if the store holds too much frequently changing data, which leads to frequent re-renders of every connected component. For simple local state it is better to use React's built-in facilities, such as useState or the Context API.\",\"Redux is a powerful state-management library, but it is not always the optimal choice. Its effectiveness drops in certain scenarios, and it is important to understand those limits so as not to complicate the architecture unnecessarily.\\nWhen Redux adds redundancy\\nThe main criticism of Redux is the large amount of boilerplate. For every state change you have to create actions, reducers and possibly thunks. In a small application, where the state is minimal and localised in a few components, that complexity is unjustified. For example, React hooks are enough to manage a form's state or toggle a modal window.\\nPerformance problems\\nRedux can become a bottleneck if:\\n\\n- The store holds a huge volume of data that is updated frequently (for example, live sensor data). Every update triggers a re-render of every component connected via `connect` or `useSelector`, even if they use a different part of the state.\\n- You keep non-serialisable data (for example, promises or class instances) in the store, which contradicts Redux's principles and complicates debugging.\\n- The reducer logic becomes too complex and slow because of deep copying of large state objects.\\nA practical example: simple form state\\nConsider the example of managing a login form. With Redux it would take a lot of code:\\n\\nThe same functionality with `useState` takes a few lines inside the component, which is far simpler and more performant for a case like this.\\nAlternatives and modern approaches\\nFor simple state shared between components at the same level, or at a shallow depth, React's Context API works excellently. For more complex scenarios with asynchronous operations you can consider libraries such as React Query (for server data) or Zustand/Recoil, which offer a lighter API and better performance in some respects.\\nConclusion: Redux is worth using in large applications with complex business logic, where centralised, predictable state, convenient debugging tools (such as the Redux DevTools) and a strict architecture are needed. For small projects, simple UI state or high-frequency data updates it often becomes superfluous and inefficient.\",\"// actions.js\\nexport const setEmail = (email) => ({ type: 'SET_EMAIL', payload: email });\\nexport const setPassword = (pw) => ({ type: 'SET_PASSWORD', payload: pw });\\n\\n// reducer.js\\nconst initialState = { email: '', password: '' };\\nfunction formReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch(action.type) {\\n    case 'SET_EMAIL': return { ...state, email: action.payload };\\n    case 'SET_PASSWORD': return { ...state, password: action.payload };\\n    default: return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// In the component: dispatching actions, selectors...\"],[1630,\"A UI kit is a set of reusable interface components, such as buttons, input fields and modal windows, created to a single set of design rules. It lets developers assemble interfaces quickly without reinventing every element, and ensures visual consistency throughout the application. A UI kit usually includes base components, a theming system, documentation and usage examples. Introducing one speeds up development and simplifies maintenance.\",\"A UI kit (or component library) is a systematised collection of ready-made interface elements created for reuse within a project. Its main purpose is to provide a single visual language and speed up the development process, sparing the team from having to create buttons, forms or cards from scratch every time.\\nThe key elements of a UI kit\\n\\n- Base components: Primitives such as Button, Input, Select, Modal, configured through props.\\n- Composite components: More complex blocks assembled from the base ones (for example, a search form or a profile header).\\n- A design-token system: Centralised variables for colours, typography, spacing and shadows, often implemented through CSS variables or a theme in styled-components.\\n- Documentation and a sandbox: An interactive environment (for example, Storybook) to demonstrate the components and their properties.\\nAn example of the structure and code\\nConsider a simple button example in a React project using styled-components for styling.\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\nA UI kit is especially useful in large projects with several front-end development and design teams. It serves as the \\\"single source of truth\\\" for the interface. It is often integrated with tools such as Storybook for isolated development and testing of components. In modern stacks (React, Vue, Angular) a UI kit can be published as a private npm package for use across several of a company's applications.\\nConclusion: A UI kit is worth using in medium and large-scale projects where development speed, design consistency and long-term maintainability matter. It reduces duplication and makes global design changes easier to apply.\",\"// Button.jsx\\nimport styled from 'styled-components';\\n\\n// Using the design tokens from the theme\\nconst StyledButton = styled.button`\\n  padding: 12px 24px;\\n  border-radius: 8px;\\n  border: none;\\n  font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif;\\n  font-weight: 600;\\n  cursor: pointer;\\n  background-color: ${props => props.variant === 'primary' ? '#007bff' : '#6c757d'};\\n  color: white;\\n  &:hover {\\n    opacity: 0.9;\\n  }\\n  &:disabled {\\n    opacity: 0.5;\\n    cursor: not-allowed;\\n  }\\n`;\\n\\nexport const Button = ({ children, variant = 'primary', ...props }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <StyledButton variant={variant} {...props}>\\n      {children}\\n    </StyledButton>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// Usage in another component\\n// import { Button } from './ui-kit/Button';\\n// <Button variant=\\\"primary\\\" onClick={handleSave}>Save</Button>\"],[1631,\"Redux is a library for managing an application's state. An action is a plain object describing what happened (for example, 'USER_LOGGED_IN'). A reducer is a pure function that takes the previous state and an action and returns the new state. The store is a single centralised object that holds the whole application state and provides methods for updating it (dispatch) and subscribing to changes (subscribe). Together they form a predictable cycle of data updates.\",\"The main concepts of Redux\\nRedux is a library for managing state in JavaScript applications, most often used with React. Its architecture is based on three fundamental principles: a single source of truth (the store), state that is read-only, and changes made only through pure functions (reducers).\\nAction\\nAn action is a plain JavaScript object describing the intention to change the state. Every action must have a type, and may also carry additional information (a payload). An action does not perform the change itself; it merely signals that something happened in the application (for example, the user pressed a button).\\n\\nReducer\\nA reducer is a pure function that determines how the application's state changes in response to an action. It takes two arguments: the current state and the action object, and returns the new state. It is important that a reducer does not mutate the original state but returns a new object. Redux can combine several reducers to manage different parts of the state.\\n\\nStore\\nThe store is the object that brings everything together. It holds the application's global state, lets you update it through the dispatch(action) method, registers change listeners through subscribe(listener) and provides access to the state through getState(). A store is created with the createStore(rootReducer) function.\\n\\nTogether, actions, reducers and the store form a unidirectional data flow: the UI dispatches an action, the store passes the action to the reducer, the reducer computes the new state, the store updates itself and notifies its subscribers, and the UI re-renders based on the new state. That makes debugging and testing predictable.\\nConclusion: Redux, with its actions, reducers and store, is especially useful in medium and large applications where complex state has to be managed centrally and must be predictable and easy to debug. It is worth applying when prop drilling in React becomes a problem, or when state has to be synchronised between many independent components.\",\"// An example action creator that returns an action\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n  payload: { text, id: Date.now(), completed: false }\\n});\\n// Calling dispatch(addTodo('Learn Redux')) sends this object into the store.\\n\\n// An example reducer for a list of tasks\\nconst initialState = [];\\nfunction todosReducer(state = initialState, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      // Return a new array with the new task added\\n      return [...state, action.payload];\\n    case 'TOGGLE_TODO':\\n      return state.map(todo =>\\n        todo.id === action.payload.id\\n          ? { ...todo, completed: !todo.completed }\\n          : todo\\n      );\\n    default:\\n      return state; // If the action is not recognised, the state does not change\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nimport rootReducer from './reducers';\\n\\n// Creating the store with the root reducer\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\\n\\n// Dispatching an action to change the state\\nstore.dispatch(addTodo('Understand the store'));\\n\\n// Subscribing to state changes (for example, to update the UI)\\nconst unsubscribe = store.subscribe(() => {\\n  console.log('State updated:', store.getState());\\n});\\n\\n// You can unsubscribe later\\n// unsubscribe();\"],[1632,\"On the first render React calls the class constructor (if the component is a class) or executes the body of the functional component. React then computes the JSX, creates a virtual DOM tree and compares it with the real DOM, applying the necessary changes (the mounting stage). After the component is painted into the DOM, the `useEffect` with an empty dependency array runs, or the `componentDidMount` method for class components, which lets you perform side effects such as loading data.\",\"A React component's first render, also known as mounting, is the process where the component is added to the DOM tree for the first time. Understanding this process is critical for initialising state, making API requests and setting up subscriptions.\\nThe stages of the first render\\n\\n- Initialisation: For a functional component React executes the body of the function, calling every hook (for example, `useState`, `useRef`) in the order of their declaration. For a class component the constructor is called first, where state is usually initialised.\\n- Render: React calls the `render` method (for classes) or the component function itself to obtain the JSX. That JSX is converted into React elements (the virtual DOM).\\n- Reconciliation: React compares the resulting virtual DOM tree with the current real DOM (which is initially empty for this component) and computes the minimal set of changes.\\n- Commit: React applies the computed changes to the real DOM, actually creating the HTML elements on the page.\\n- Running the mount effects: Once the component is displayed in the DOM, React schedules the side effects. For functional components that is the call of the function inside `useEffect` with the empty dependency array `[]`. For class components the `componentDidMount` method is called.\\nPractical example\\nConsider a functional component that loads data on mount:\\n\\nIn this example the order on the first render is: declaring the state, executing the component's body (returning `Loading...`), mounting into the DOM, and then the asynchronous `fetch` call inside `useEffect`.\\nWhere this is used\\nKnowledge of the mounting stages is used to:\\n\\n- Optimise performance (avoiding unnecessary renders).\\n- Initialise third-party libraries correctly (for example, maps or charts), which need access to the DOM.\\n- Manage subscriptions to events or external data, to avoid memory leaks.\\nConclusion: Understanding the first render lets you place initialisation code correctly (in `useEffect` or `componentDidMount`), avoiding common mistakes such as performing side effects during the render or trying to access the DOM before it is ready.\",\"import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction UserProfile({ userId }) {\\n  // 1. Initialising the state on the first render\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n\\n  // 3. The effect that runs after mounting\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`)\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        setUser(data);\\n        setLoading(false);\\n      });\\n    // The empty dependency array guarantees it runs once\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  // 2. Returning the JSX to render\\n  if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{user.name}</h1>\\n      <p>{user.email}</p>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1633,\"The main alternatives to Redux include the built-in Context API, MobX, Zustand, Recoil and Jotai. The Context API suits simple global state without middleware. MobX uses reactive programming and is simpler to set up. Zustand is a minimalist hook-based library, popular for its simplicity. Recoil and Jotai offer atomic state management and integrate well with React. The choice depends on the complexity of the project and the team's preferences.\",\"Redux was long the standard for state management in React applications, but its complexity and boilerplate led to the appearance of lighter, more convenient alternatives. These alternatives solve the same problems — predictable state changes and distributing state across components — but with different approaches and trade-offs.\\n\\nBuilt-in solutions: the Context API\\n\\nThe Context API is React's native facility for passing data through the component tree without props. It is excellent for simple global state, for example a theme or user data. It is not, however, optimised for frequent updates and can cause unnecessary re-renders unless combined with useMemo or useReducer.\\n\\nLibraries with a reactive approach: MobX\\n\\nMobX uses the principles of reactive programming: you declare observable state, and the library automatically tracks and updates the components that depend on it. This requires less boilerplate than Redux and is often considered more intuitive.\\n\\nMinimalist solutions: Zustand\\n\\nZustand is a small library built on React hooks. It lets you create stores with minimal configuration, with no need for providers. State is updated directly, and subscription to changes happens automatically.\\n\\nAtomic state managers: Recoil and Jotai\\n\\nRecoil (from Facebook) and Jotai (a lighter counterpart) represent state as atoms — independent pieces that can be combined. That lets you manage derived state efficiently and avoid unnecessary re-renders.\\n\\nSummary: The choice of an alternative to Redux depends on the scale of the project. For small applications the Context API or Zustand is enough. For complex ones requiring fine-grained optimisation, MobX or the atomic libraries (Recoil/Jotai) are suitable. Redux remains a good choice for large projects with clear discipline and a need for powerful middleware (for example, for logging or asynchronous actions).\",\"// Creating a context\\nconst ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\n\\n// The provider at the root of the application\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// Use in a child component\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  const { theme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return <div>Current theme: {theme}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { makeAutoObservable } from 'mobx';\\nimport { observer } from 'mobx-react-lite';\\n\\n// Creating a store\\nclass CounterStore {\\n  count = 0;\\n  constructor() {\\n    makeAutoObservable(this);\\n  }\\n  increment() {\\n    this.count += 1;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst store = new CounterStore();\\n\\n// A component that reacts to changes\\nconst Counter = observer(() => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <span>{store.count}</span>\\n      <button onClick={() => store.increment()}>+</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nimport create from 'zustand';\\n\\n// Creating a store\\nconst useStore = create((set) => ({\\n  bears: 0,\\n  increasePopulation: () => set((state) => ({ bears: state.bears + 1 })),\\n  removeAllBears: () => set({ bears: 0 }),\\n}));\\n\\n// Use in a component\\nfunction BearCounter() {\\n  const bears = useStore((state) => state.bears);\\n  const increase = useStore((state) => state.increasePopulation);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{bears} bears</h1>\\n      <button onClick={increase}>Add a bear</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1634,\"With infinite recursion a function keeps calling itself without ever reaching an exit condition. Every call adds a new frame to the call stack. When the stack overflows, the runtime (for example, JavaScript) raises a \\\"Maximum call stack size exceeded\\\" error and the program's execution terminates abnormally. This is a critical error that must be prevented with a correct base case in the recursive function.\",\"Recursion is a technique where a function calls itself to solve a task, breaking it down into smaller subtasks. Infinite recursion arises when there is no correct base case (a stopping condition) or when the recursive calls never converge towards that case.\\n\\nThe call-stack mechanism\\n\\nEvery function call stores its execution context on the call stack: local variables, arguments and the return address. With infinite recursion new frames are added continuously until the memory allocated for the stack is exhausted.\\n\\nAn example of the error\\n\\nConsider the classic example in JavaScript:\\n\\nPractical consequences\\n\\n- Abnormal termination: The program crashes with an error, which is unacceptable in production.\\n\\n- Debugging: The stack trace will be very long, but it will show the repeating pattern of calls.\\n\\n- Prevention: Always define a clear base case and make sure the recursive step moves towards it.\\n\\nA correct example of recursion\\n\\nHere is a function to compute a factorial with a stopping condition:\\n\\nConclusion: Infinite recursion must be avoided, since it leads to a stack overflow and a crash. Recursive algorithms should be designed so that the base case is guaranteed to be reached, and for deep recursion it is sometimes worth considering iterative solutions or optimisations (tail recursion, where it is supported).\",\"function infiniteRecursion() {\\n    // No stopping condition - the function always calls itself\\n    return infiniteRecursion();\\n}\\n\\n// Attempting to call it leads to an error:\\n// \\\"Uncaught RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded\\\"\\ninfiniteRecursion();\\n\\nfunction factorial(n) {\\n    // The base case: the factorial of 0 or 1 is 1\\n    if (n <= 1) {\\n        return 1;\\n    }\\n    // The recursive step: n * factorial(n-1)\\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);\\n}\\nconsole.log(factorial(5)); // 120\"],[1635,\"For parallel HTTP requests in JavaScript, use `Promise.all()`. Create an array of promises, each performing a fetch request. `Promise.all()` starts them simultaneously and waits for all of them to complete. This is significantly faster than sequential requests. If any of the promises fails, the whole `Promise.all` rejects, which is why `Promise.allSettled()` is sometimes used instead.\",\"Modern web applications often need to get data from several independent sources (for example, a user API, a news API and a weather API). Performing those requests sequentially creates unnecessary delays, since every request waits for the previous one to finish. Running them in parallel lets you send all the requests almost simultaneously and process the results once they are all ready, which reduces the total waiting time.\\n\\nThe main approach: Promise.all\\n\\nThe standard way in modern JavaScript is to use `Promise.all()`. This method takes an iterable collection of promises (for example, an array) and returns a new promise that is fulfilled when all the promises passed in have completed, or rejected if at least one of the promises is rejected.\\n\\nAlternatives for handling errors\\n\\nIf it is important to get the results even for partially failed requests, use `Promise.allSettled()`. It always completes successfully, returning an array of objects with a status (`fulfilled` or `rejected`) and the value or the reason for the error.\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\n\\n- Loading data for a dashboard from several microservices.\\n\\n- Preloading resources (images, scripts) to speed up page rendering.\\n\\n- Validating data through several external services at once.\\n\\n- Collecting metrics or logs from several instances of an application.\\n\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.all()` to run independent HTTP requests in parallel when you need the results of every operation and an error in one of them should abort the whole process. If you need more resilient handling that preserves partial results, choose `Promise.allSettled()`. This approach is fundamental to building responsive, fast web applications.\",\"async function fetchMultipleUrls(urls) {\\n  // Create an array of request promises\\n  const fetchPromises = urls.map(url => fetch(url));\\n\\n  try {\\n    // Start all the requests in parallel and wait for the results\\n    const responses = await Promise.all(fetchPromises);\\n    // Decode the response bodies (also in parallel)\\n    const data = await Promise.all(\\n      responses.map(response => response.json())\\n    );\\n    return data;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('One of the requests failed:', error);\\n    throw error;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst apiEndpoints = [\\n  'https://api.example.com/users/1',\\n  'https://api.example.com/posts',\\n  'https://api.example.com/weather'\\n];\\n\\nfetchMultipleUrls(apiEndpoints).then(results => {\\n  console.log('All the data has arrived:', results);\\n});\\n\\nconst promises = [\\n  fetch('https://api.example.com/good'),\\n  fetch('https://api.example.com/bad-endpoint'), // May return 404\\n  fetch('https://api.example.com/another')\\n];\\n\\nconst results = await Promise.allSettled(promises);\\nconst successfulData = results\\n  .filter(result => result.status === 'fulfilled')\\n  .map(result => result.value);\"],[1636,\"To combine the results of several asynchronous requests in JavaScript, `Promise.all()` is most often used. This method takes an array of promises and returns a new promise that is fulfilled when all the promises passed in have completed successfully. If at least one promise fails, the whole `Promise.all` fails too. This lets the requests run in parallel rather than sequentially, which significantly speeds up loading the data. An alternative is `Promise.allSettled()`, which waits for every promise to settle regardless of its status.\",\"In modern web development there is often a need to perform several independent requests to an API, a database or the file system and to process their results together. Sequential execution (one after another) leads to accumulating delays and a poor user experience. The solution is parallel execution followed by aggregation of the results.\\nThe main approach: Promise.all\\nThe standard method `Promise.all(iterable)` takes an iterable collection (most often an array) of promises and returns a single promise. That resulting promise becomes fulfilled only when every promise passed in has completed successfully. Its value is an array of the results, in the same order as the promises were passed. If any of the promises is rejected, `Promise.all` is immediately rejected with that same error, ignoring the results of the others (even those already fulfilled).\\n\\nAlternative methods\\nSometimes more flexible behaviour is required:\\n\\n- Promise.allSettled() – waits for every promise to settle and returns an array of objects describing each result (the status `fulfilled` or `rejected`, and the value/reason). Useful when you need to handle all the results even if some requests failed.\\n- Promise.race() – returns the result of the first promise to settle (whether successfully or with an error). Used to implement timeouts.\\n- Promise.any() – returns the result of the first promise to be fulfilled successfully. It ignores errors as long as at least one promise succeeds.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nThis approach is widely used when loading data to initialise a page (for example, the user's profile, their settings and their notifications), aggregating information from several sources (different microservices, third-party APIs) or performing independent tasks in parallel (validating several form fields, loading several images).\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.all` to run independent asynchronous operations in parallel when you need all the results at once and are prepared to handle a possible error in any of the requests. For more resilient handling, where errors in individual operations are acceptable, choose `Promise.allSettled`.\",\"async function fetchUserData(userId) {\\n  const [profile, orders, messages] = await Promise.all([\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}/profile`).then(r => r.json()),\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}/orders`).then(r => r.json()),\\n    fetch(`/api/users/${userId}/messages`).then(r => r.json())\\n  ]);\\n  return { profile, orders, messages };\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nfetchUserData(123)\\n  .then(data => console.log('All the data:', data))\\n  .catch(err => console.error('One of the requests failed:', err));\\n\\n// An example with allSettled\\nconst promises = [\\n  fetch('/api/data1'),\\n  fetch('/api/data2'),\\n  Promise.reject(new Error('An artificial error'))\\n];\\n\\nconst results = await Promise.allSettled(promises);\\nconst successfulData = results\\n  .filter(r => r.status === 'fulfilled')\\n  .map(r => r.value);\"],[1637,\"String is made immutable to provide security, performance and predictability. For example, it lets strings be used safely in a multithreaded environment and lets their hash codes be cached. Recursion is preferable when a task is naturally recursive (traversing a tree), while loops are better for simple iteration and for saving memory, since recursion can lead to a stack overflow.\",\"The immutability of strings in Java is a fundamental design decision that affects security, performance and ease of use. An immutable object is an object whose state cannot be changed after it is created. For strings that means any operation that seemingly changes a string (for example, concatenation) in fact creates a new string.\\n\\nThe reasons for String's immutability\\n\\n- Security: Strings are often used to hold sensitive data (passwords, URLs). If they were mutable, malicious code could change their contents after they were passed along, which would lead to vulnerabilities.\\n\\n- Thread safety: Immutable objects are thread-safe by their very nature, since their state is constant. That lets strings be used safely in multithreaded applications without synchronisation.\\n\\n- Caching the hash code: The String class caches its hash code after the first computation, which significantly speeds up work with collections such as HashMap, where the hash code is used frequently.\\n\\n- The possibility of a string pool: The JVM uses a string pool to store unique string literals. That saves memory and lets literals be compared quickly with `==`.\\n\\nRecursion vs a loop\\n\\nThe choice between recursion and a loop depends on the specific task, the readability of the code and performance constraints.\\n\\n- Recursion is a function calling itself. It is ideally suited to tasks that are naturally recursive in structure, for example traversing trees (the DOM, a file system), divide-and-conquer algorithms (quicksort, binary search) or computing a factorial.\\n\\n- Loops (iteration) use explicit control structures (for, while) to repeat operations. They are usually more memory-efficient, since they do not create new stack frames.\\n\\nThe key criteria for choosing\\n\\n- Readability: For tasks that are recursive in nature, a recursive solution is often simpler to understand and maintain.\\n\\n- Performance and memory: Recursion can lead to a stack overflow (StackOverflowError) at deep levels of nesting. Loops are usually more memory-efficient and faster.\\n\\n- Tail recursion: Some languages (not Java) optimise tail recursion, turning it into a loop, which removes the drawbacks. Java has no such optimisation.\\n\\nCode example: factorial\\n\\nConclusion: String is made immutable to provide security, thread safety and optimisation. Recursion is worth using when a task is recursive in nature and the depth of the calls is guaranteed to be small, while loops are preferable for simple iteration, for performance and to avoid a stack overflow.\",\"// The recursive solution\\npublic static int factorialRecursive(int n) {\\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\\n    return n * factorialRecursive(n - 1);\\n}\\n\\n// The iterative solution (a loop)\\npublic static int factorialIterative(int n) {\\n    int result = 1;\\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\\n        result *= i;\\n    }\\n    return result;\\n}\"],[1638,\"The garbage collector (GC) automatically frees the memory occupied by objects that are no longer referenced from the active part of the program. With circular dependencies, two or more objects refer to each other, but there may be no reference to the whole group from outside. Simple algorithms based purely on reference counting cannot remove such cycles, which leads to a memory leak. Modern GCs (for example, in JavaScript or Java) use algorithms such as \\\"Mark-and-Sweep\\\", which start from the root objects (global variables, the call stack), mark every reachable object and then delete everything unmarked, which lets unreachable cycles be collected safely.\",\"Garbage collection is an automatic memory-management process that frees the resources occupied by objects the program no longer needs. The key problem arises when objects form a closed loop of references but none of them is reachable from the program's \\\"roots\\\" (for example, from the global scope or the call stack).\\nThe problem of circular references\\nConsider a simple pseudocode example: object A holds a reference to object B, and object B holds a reference back to object A. If the external reference to A or B is removed, both objects become unreachable for the program but still refer to each other. An algorithm based purely on reference counting will see that each object's reference count is 1 (coming from the other object in the cycle) and will not free the memory, which is the classic leak.\\nModern GC algorithms\\nMost modern runtimes (the JVM for Java, V8 for JavaScript, the .NET CLR) use algorithms that determine the reachability of objects rather than simply counting references. The most widespread approach is Mark-and-Sweep:\\n\\n- The mark phase: The GC starts traversing from the root set — global variables, local variables in active function calls, static fields and so on. Every object that can be reached from the roots is marked as live.\\n- The sweep phase: Memory is scanned, and every unmarked object is considered garbage and freed. Unreachable circular structures will not be marked in the mark phase, since there is no path to them from the roots, so they are correctly removed in the sweep phase.\\nAn example in JavaScript\\nJavaScript (in engines like V8) uses an advanced garbage collector that handles cycles efficiently. Consider code that creates a circular reference:\\n\\nAfter the `createCycle` function finishes, the local variables `objA` and `objB` go out of scope. Although the objects refer to each other, there are no longer any references to them from the root set (global variables or the stack). So in the mark phase they will not be marked as live and will subsequently be freed.\\nWhere this is used\\nUnderstanding how the GC handles cycles matters when developing in languages with automatic memory management (Java, C#, Python, JavaScript, Go) in order to write reliable applications. It is especially critical in long-lived processes (server applications, mobile applications), where an accumulation of circular references that cannot be removed would lead to memory being gradually exhausted. A developer usually does not need to break cycles manually, but should avoid creating global or long-lived references to objects that may take part in cycles, if those objects are meant to be collected.\\nConclusion: Modern garbage collectors, using reachability algorithms (such as Mark-and-Sweep), handle circular dependencies correctly, automatically freeing the memory of unreachable cycles. That spares the developer from manual memory management in most scenarios, but requires an understanding of which references keep objects in memory, so as not to create unintended long-lived references.\",\"function createCycle() {\\n  let objA = { name: 'A' };\\n  let objB = { name: 'B' };\\n  // Create a circular reference\\n  objA.ref = objB;\\n  objB.ref = objA;\\n  // Once the function exits, the local variables objA and objB\\n  // cease to exist, but the objects still refer to each other.\\n  // The GC (Mark-and-Sweep), however, works out that they are unreachable from the global scope,\\n  // and deletes both.\\n}\\ncreateCycle();\\n// Here the GC can safely collect objA and objB.\"],[1639,\"String concatenation is the operation of joining two or more strings into one new string. In JavaScript the `+` operator or the `concat()` method is used for it. When concatenating with non-string values, they are automatically converted to strings (implicit type coercion happens). Strings in JavaScript are immutable, so every concatenation creates a new string in memory, which can affect performance when the operation is frequent.\",\"String concatenation is a fundamental operation in programming that lets you combine several string values into one resulting string. In JavaScript it is often used to build dynamic text, paths, SQL queries (although for the latter parameterised queries are better) or HTML markup.\\n\\nHow concatenation works in JavaScript\\n\\nThe main operator for concatenation is plus (+). When one of the operands is a string, the other operand (if it is not a string) is converted to a string through implicit type coercion. This rule also works in complex expressions: evaluation proceeds left to right, and as soon as a string is encountered, the subsequent operands are converted to strings.\\n\\nAlternative methods\\n\\nBesides the '+' operator, you can use the `String.prototype.concat()` method, but it is less popular. In modern versions of JavaScript, template literals are also convenient for building complex strings with variables, letting you embed expressions via `${expression}`.\\n\\nPerformance and immutability\\n\\nStrings in JavaScript are primitive, immutable values. That means any operation that changes a string (including concatenation) in fact creates a new string in memory. With frequent concatenation in loops that can lead to many intermediate strings being created and to a drop in performance. For such cases it is better to use an array and the `join()` method.\\n\\nConclusion: String concatenation is a simple but important operation. Use the '+' operator for simple cases, template literals for the convenience of inserting variables, and for mass concatenation in loops prefer an array with the join() method to optimise performance.\",\"let result = 'Hello, ' + 'World!'; // 'Hello, World!'\\nlet numAndStr = 5 + ' apples';    // '5 apples' (the number 5 converted to '5')\\nlet mixed = 2 + 2 + ' apples';    // '4 apples' (first 2+2=4, then 4 + ' apples')\\nlet reversed = 'apples: ' + 2 + 2; // 'apples: 22' (after the string, both numbers are converted to strings)\\n\\n// Using concat\\nlet str = 'Hello'.concat(', ', 'World', '!'); // 'Hello, World!'\\n\\n// Using template literals (ES6+)\\nlet name = 'Alice';\\nlet greeting = `Hello, ${name}!`; // 'Hello, Alice!'\\n\\n// Inefficient in a loop\\nlet str = '';\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    str += 'x'; // A new string is created every time\\n}\\n\\n// More efficient\\nlet parts = [];\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    parts.push('x');\\n}\\nlet result = parts.join(''); // The concatenation happens once\"],[1640,\"If there is a return in the try block, the value is computed and remembered, but execution moves on to the finally block (if there is one), and it is the value returned from finally (if it has its own return) that becomes the function's result. If finally has no return, the remembered value from try is returned. If an exception was thrown in try, it is caught by catch, and the logic is the same: finally can override the return value.\",\"The try-catch-finally construct in JavaScript (and in many other languages) is used to handle exceptions. The key aspect is how a return statement inside those blocks interacts with the control flow and the function's return value.\\nThe order of execution and return\\nWhen a return statement is encountered in the try block, the following happens:\\n\\n- The value of the expression after return is computed.\\n- That value is \\\"remembered\\\" as the function's result.\\n- Control then passes to the finally block (if one is present). The finally block always runs, regardless of whether there was an exception or a return.\\n- If the finally block has its own return statement, that value becomes the function's final result, overwriting the value remembered earlier from try (or catch).\\n- If finally has no return, the function returns the value remembered from try (or from catch, if there was an exception).\\nCode examples\\nConsider several scenarios:\\n\\nIn this case finally runs but does not return a value of its own, so the result is 'from try'.\\n\\nHere the return in finally overrides the return value.\\n\\nWhen an exception occurs in try, control passes to catch, which may also have a return. Finally runs after catch, and if finally has no return, the value from catch is returned.\\nWhere this is used\\nUnderstanding this behaviour is critical when working with resources that must be released (for example, closing file descriptors or database connections). The finally block guarantees that the cleanup code runs, even if a return occurred in try or catch. You do need to be careful with a return inside finally, however, since it can \\\"wipe out\\\" an important value or hide a thrown exception.\\nConclusion: Use finally for mandatory cleanup operations, but avoid putting a return inside finally unless you explicitly want to override the function's return value, since that can lead to non-obvious behaviour and make debugging harder.\",\"function example1() {\\n  try {\\n    console.log('try');\\n    return 'from try';\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('catch');\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('finally');\\n  }\\n}\\nconsole.log(example1()); // Output: try, finally, 'from try'\\n\\nfunction example2() {\\n  try {\\n    console.log('try');\\n    return 'from try';\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('catch');\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('finally');\\n    return 'from finally';\\n  }\\n}\\nconsole.log(example2()); // Output: try, finally, 'from finally'\\n\\nfunction example3() {\\n  try {\\n    console.log('try');\\n    throw new Error('Oops!');\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.log('catch');\\n    return 'from catch';\\n  } finally {\\n    console.log('finally');\\n  }\\n}\\nconsole.log(example3()); // Output: try, catch, finally, 'from catch'\"],[1641,\"The choice depends on the task. Loops are usually more memory-efficient and faster, since they do not create new function calls. Recursion makes code cleaner for tasks with a naturally recursive structure, for example traversing trees. Deep recursion, however, can cause a stack overflow. In most cases iteration is preferable for performance, and recursion for clarity of code when working with recursive data structures.\",\"Recursion and loops are the two fundamental approaches to organising repeated operations in programming. Each has its strengths and weaknesses, and the choice between them affects the readability, performance and reliability of the code.\\nThe main differences\\nRecursion is when a function calls itself to solve subtasks. It suits tasks that naturally break down into identical subtasks, for example traversing trees, computing a factorial or Fibonacci numbers. A loop (iteration) executes a block of code repeatedly while a condition is true, and is usually controlled by a counter or a condition.\\nPerformance and memory\\nRecursion can be less efficient: every function call creates a new stack frame, which consumes memory. With deep recursion that leads to a stack overflow. Loops create no new calls, so they are generally faster and use less memory. For example, computing a factorial with a loop is more efficient.\\nCode examples\\nConsider computing the factorial of a number n.\\n\\nThe recursive version is shorter and may be more intuitive, but for large n it can cause a stack overflow. The iterative version is safer and faster.\\nWhere to use which\\n\\n- Recursion: tasks with recursive data structures (trees, graphs), divide-and-conquer algorithms (quicksort, binary search), when readability matters more than micro-optimisations.\\n- Loops: simple repeated operations, processing arrays or lists, when performance and avoiding a stack overflow matter.\\nConclusion: use loops for most iterative tasks because of their efficiency and reliability. Apply recursion where it significantly simplifies the code and the task is naturally recursive, but always bear the recursion-depth limits in mind.\",\"// The recursive approach\\nfunction factorialRecursive(n) {\\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;\\n    return n * factorialRecursive(n - 1);\\n}\\n\\n// The iterative approach (a loop)\\nfunction factorialIterative(n) {\\n    let result = 1;\\n    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\\n        result *= i;\\n    }\\n    return result;\\n}\"],[1642,\"When you get errors from an external service, first determine their type: transient (network failures, timeouts) or permanent (invalid data, unavailability). For transient errors it is worth implementing retries with exponential backoff. If the service is unavailable for a long time, use the Circuit Breaker pattern so as not to overload it. Always provide a fallback, for example cached data or simplified logic, so that the application's main function does not break.\",\"Interaction with external services is an inseparable part of modern applications, and errors in that interaction are inevitable. Handling such failures competently increases the system's fault tolerance and improves the user experience.\\nClassifying errors\\nThe first step is to analyse the code and the type of the error. Transient errors (for example, network timeouts, 5xx errors) can often be resolved by repeating the request. Permanent errors (4xx, for example 404 or 400 caused by invalid input) require fixing the logic on the client side.\\nHandling strategies\\n\\n- Retries: For transient failures, implement retry logic with exponential backoff and jitter, to avoid the thundering-herd effect.\\n- Circuit Breaker: This pattern prevents constant requests to a broken service. After a certain number of failures the \\\"breaker\\\" opens and requests return an error immediately, while periodically checking whether the service has recovered.\\n- Fallback: Provide an alternative way of working, for example showing cached data, using default values or switching to another, less critical service.\\nCode example (Node.js with exponential backoff)\\n\\nConclusion: A strategy for handling errors from external services should include classifying the failures, retry mechanisms for transient problems, and mandatory fallback scenarios to maintain the application's basic functionality during prolonged outages.\",\"async function callWithRetry(apiCall, maxRetries = 3) {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < maxRetries; i++) {\\n    try {\\n      return await apiCall();\\n    } catch (error) {\\n      // Retry only for transient errors\\n      if (error.statusCode && error.statusCode >= 500 && i < maxRetries - 1) {\\n        const delay = Math.pow(2, i) * 1000 + Math.random() * 1000;\\n        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      throw error; // If the error is permanent or the retry limit is exhausted\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\ncallWithRetry(() => fetch('https://api.example.com/data'))\\n  .then(data => console.log(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error('Final error:', error));\"],[1643,\"To avoid duplicating operations, debouncing and throttling techniques are used for events, as well as idempotency checks for requests. Debouncing postpones execution until the events stop, while throttling limits the frequency of calls. For network operations, unique idempotency keys are used so that the server can ignore repeated requests. These approaches prevent unnecessary load and errors.\",\"Processing an operation twice can lead to errors, unnecessary load on the server and an incorrect application state. For example, clicking a \\\"Submit\\\" button repeatedly can create several identical orders. Several approaches are used to solve this problem, depending on the context.\\nDebounce\\nDebouncing postpones the execution of a function until a certain amount of time has passed with no new calls. This is useful for handling events that happen frequently, for example typing text into a search field. Instead of sending a request for every character, the request is sent only after a pause in the typing.\\n\\nThrottle\\nThrottling guarantees that a function runs no more often than once per given interval of time. This is useful for handling scroll or window-resize events, where it matters that the interface updates, but not too often.\\n\\nIdempotency\\nFor network requests, especially in financial operations, avoiding duplication is critical. Idempotency means a repeated request with the same data does not change the result. On the client you can disable a button after it is pressed, and on the server you can use unique idempotency keys to ignore repeated requests with the same key.\\n\\nThe choice of approach depends on the task: debouncing for input events, throttling for frequent UI events, and idempotency for critical data operations. Applying these techniques correctly improves an application's performance and reliability.\",\"function debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeoutId;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId);\\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => func.apply(this, args), delay);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById('search');\\nconst fetchResults = debounce((query) => {\\n  console.log('Fetching results for:', query);\\n}, 300);\\nsearchInput.addEventListener('input', (e) => fetchResults(e.target.value));\\n\\nfunction throttle(func, limit) {\\n  let inThrottle;\\n  return function(...args) {\\n    if (!inThrottle) {\\n      func.apply(this, args);\\n      inThrottle = true;\\n      setTimeout(() => inThrottle = false, limit);\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nwindow.addEventListener('scroll', throttle(() => {\\n  console.log('Scroll event handled');\\n}, 200));\\n\\n// An example of sending a request with an idempotency key\\nasync function createOrder(orderData) {\\n  const idempotencyKey = generateUniqueId(); // For example, a UUID\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/orders', {\\n    method: 'POST',\\n    headers: {\\n      'Content-Type': 'application/json',\\n      'Idempotency-Key': idempotencyKey\\n    },\\n    body: JSON.stringify(orderData)\\n  });\\n  return response.json();\\n}\\n// The server checks the key and, if it has already been processed, returns the previous result.\"],[1644,\"To run several REST requests in parallel in JavaScript, the `Promise.all()` method is used. It takes an array of promises (for example, `fetch()` calls) and returns a new promise that resolves when all the promises passed in have completed. This lets you send all the requests at once rather than sequentially, which significantly speeds up getting the data. If at least one promise is rejected, the whole `Promise.all` is rejected too, so `Promise.allSettled()` can be used to handle errors.\",\"Modern web applications often need to get data from several independent sources (for example, the user's profile, a list of orders and notifications). Performing those requests sequentially leads to accumulating delays, since each subsequent request waits for the previous one to finish. Running them in parallel lets you send all the requests at once and process the results when they are all ready, which reduces the total waiting time.\\nThe main approach: Promise.all\\nThe standard way is to use the built-in `Promise.all()` method. It takes an iterable collection (most often an array) of promises and returns a single promise. That resulting promise is fulfilled when all the promises passed in have completed successfully, and returns an array of their results in the same order. If any of the promises is rejected, `Promise.all` is immediately rejected with the reason of that first error.\\n\\nAlternatives for handling errors\\nIf it is important to get the results even for partially failed requests, use `Promise.allSettled()`. It waits for every promise to settle and returns an array of objects with a status (`fulfilled` or `rejected`) and the value or the reason for the error.\\nLimiting the number of simultaneous requests\\nWhen working with a large number of requests, or a rate-limited API, you may need to limit the concurrency so as not to exceed the server's limits. Various patterns can be used for this, for example splitting the array into chunks or using ready-made libraries (p-limit).\\n\\nConclusion: Use `Promise.all()` to run independent REST requests in parallel when maximum speed is needed and every request is critical. If you need more flexible error handling or want to limit the load, use `Promise.allSettled()` or concurrency-limiting patterns.\",\"async function fetchMultipleUrls(urls) {\\n  try {\\n    // Create an array of request promises\\n    const promises = urls.map(url => fetch(url).then(res => res.json()));\\n    // Wait for all the promises to settle in parallel\\n    const results = await Promise.all(promises);\\n    console.log('All the data has arrived:', results);\\n    return results;\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('One of the requests failed:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst apiEndpoints = [\\n  'https://api.example.com/users/1',\\n  'https://api.example.com/posts?userId=1'\\n];\\nfetchMultipleUrls(apiEndpoints);\\n\\nconst promises = [\\n  fetch('/api/data1'),\\n  fetch('/api/data2'),\\n  fetch('/api/invalid-endpoint') // May fail\\n];\\n\\nconst results = await Promise.allSettled(promises);\\nconst successfulData = results\\n  .filter(result => result.status === 'fulfilled')\\n  .map(result => result.value);\\n\\n// A simple example limited to 2 simultaneous requests\\nasync function fetchWithConcurrency(urls, limit) {\\n  const results = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < urls.length; i += limit) {\\n    const chunk = urls.slice(i, i + limit);\\n    const chunkResults = await Promise.all(chunk.map(url => fetch(url)));\\n    results.push(...chunkResults);\\n  }\\n  return results;\\n}\"],[1645,\"Hard-coded values are numbers or strings embedded directly in the code with no explanatory name. They make the code less readable, since their purpose is not obvious. Changing such a value requires finding and editing every place it is used, which is error-prone. They also complicate testing and configuring the application for different environments. Moving the values into named constants or configuration files solves these problems.\",\"Hard-coded values, often called \\\"magic numbers\\\" or \\\"magic strings\\\", are literals (for example, 100, \\\"admin\\\", 3.14) that appear in code with no contextual explanation. Using them directly in a program's logic creates several serious problems for development and maintenance.\\n\\nThe main problems with hard-coded values\\n\\n- Poor readability and comprehensibility: The number 86400 in code says nothing about its purpose, whereas a constant `SECONDS_IN_DAY` makes the developer's intention immediately clear.\\n\\n- Difficulty of change: If the same value is used in several places, changing it requires editing each of those places. That is laborious and increases the risk of missing an instance, which leads to bugs.\\n\\n- Difficulty of testing: Values hard-wired into the code (for example, an API URL or a server port) make it hard to isolate modules for unit testing or to substitute test values.\\n\\n- No configuration flexibility: Parameters that may change depending on the environment (development, staging, production), such as API keys or database connection settings, should be externalised.\\n\\n- An increased likelihood of errors: When copying code it is easy to make a mistake and paste the wrong value, and strings with the same meaning but different spelling (for example, \\\"Admin\\\" and \\\"admin\\\") can lead to hard-to-catch bugs.\\n\\nA practical example and the solution\\n\\nConsider a fragment of code with hard-coded values:\\n\\nRefactoring by moving the values into constants or configuration:\\n\\nThis approach not only makes the code self-documenting but also centralises the management of critical values. For environment-dependent configuration, configuration files (.env, config.json) or configuration-management systems are used.\\n\\nConclusion: Avoid hard-coded values in business logic and in code that may change. Move them into named constants to improve readability, and into configuration files/environment variables to provide flexibility and security when deploying to different environments.\",\"// Bad: hard-coded values\\nfunction calculateDiscount(price) {\\n    if (price > 1000) {\\n        return price * 0.1; // What are 1000 and 0.1?\\n    }\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\\nfunction getUserRole() {\\n    return \\\"admin\\\"; // The role is hard-wired into the code\\n}\\n\\n// Good: the values moved into named constants\\nconst DISCOUNT_THRESHOLD = 1000;\\nconst DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.1;\\nconst USER_ROLES = {\\n    ADMIN: 'admin',\\n    USER: 'user'\\n};\\n\\nfunction calculateDiscount(price) {\\n    if (price > DISCOUNT_THRESHOLD) {\\n        return price * DISCOUNT_RATE;\\n    }\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\\nfunction getUserRole() {\\n    return USER_ROLES.ADMIN; // Now it is clear that a role is returned\\n}\\n\\n// For configuration parameters (for example, from environment variables)\\nconst API_BASE_URL = process.env.API_URL || 'https://api.example.com';\"],[1646,\"When hashCode is poorly distributed in a HashMap, many keys land in the same buckets. That leads to frequent collisions. Instead of fast O(1) access, lookup degrades to O(n), since within a bucket the elements are stored as a list (or a tree). As a result the put, get and remove operations become slow, which sharply reduces the collection's performance.\",\"HashMap in Java uses a key's hash code to determine the index of the bucket in which the key-value pair will be stored. An ideal hashCode distributes the keys evenly across all the available buckets, minimising collisions.\\nWhat is a poor distribution?\\nA poor distribution arises when the hashCode method returns identical or very similar values for different keys. For example, if hashCode always returns the constant 1, every key lands in a single bucket.\\nConsequences\\n\\n- Collisions: Many keys map to a single bucket.\\n- Degradation of the structure: A bucket may turn from a linked list into a balanced tree (in Java 8+, with a large number of collisions), but lookup will still be slower than O(1).\\n- Reduced performance: The get(), put() and remove() operations run in O(n) in the worst case instead of the expected O(1), where n is the number of elements in a single bucket.\\nCode example\\nConsider a class with a poor hashCode:\\n\\nHow to avoid the problem\\n\\n- Use the standard classes (String, Integer) as keys — they have good hashCodes.\\n- When implementing your own hashCode, try to generate unique values using the object's different fields (for example, via 31 * field1.hashCode() + field2.hashCode()).\\n- Make sure hashCode is consistent with equals.\\nConclusion: A good hashCode implementation is critically important for a HashMap to work efficiently. A poor distribution turns a fast hash table into a slow list, which is especially noticeable with large volumes of data. Always test the distribution of hashCode for typical sets of keys.\",\"class BadKey {\\n    private final int id;\\n    BadKey(int id) { this.id = id; }\\n    @Override\\n    public int hashCode() {\\n        // Always returns 1 - the worst case\\n        return 1;\\n    }\\n    @Override\\n    public boolean equals(Object o) { /* a correct implementation */ }\\n}\\n\\npublic class Main {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        HashMap<BadKey, String> map = new HashMap<>();\\n        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n            map.put(new BadKey(i), \\\"value\\\" + i);\\n        }\\n        // The lookup will be very slow, since every key is in one bucket\\n        String value = map.get(new BadKey(5000));\\n    }\\n}\"],[1647,\"Copying a file is the operation of reading data from a source file and writing it to a new file. In Node.js the `fs` module is used for this, with the `fs.copyFile` method or `fs.createReadStream`/`fs.createWriteStream`. In Python the standard way is the `shutil` module with the `shutil.copy` function. These methods handle binary data and work correctly with large files, without loading them into memory in their entirety.\",\"Copying a file is a fundamental operation in programming, used for backups, data processing or moving information between directories. The implementation depends on the language and the runtime, but the general principle is to open the source file for reading, read its contents (often as a stream, to avoid overloading memory) and write the data read into the target file.\\nImplementation in Node.js\\nIn Node.js the built-in `fs` (File System) module is used to work with files. The simplest way is to use the asynchronous `fs.copyFile` method.\\n\\nFor large files, or for finer control, you can use streams, which lets you copy a file in pieces without loading it into RAM in its entirety.\\nImplementation in Python\\nIn Python the standard library provides the `shutil` module, which contains high-level functions for file operations, including copying.\\n\\nThe `shutil.copy` function automatically handles both text and binary files. To copy metadata (for example, access permissions) there is the `shutil.copy2` function.\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Task automation: Scripts to back up logs or configuration files.\\n- Data processing: Creating copies of source data before transforming it.\\n- Application deployment: Copying assets (images, templates) into the build directory.\\nConclusion: Use the built-in high-level functions (`fs.copyFile` in Node.js, `shutil.copy` in Python) for most tasks, since they are efficient and simple. When working with very large files, or when you need to monitor progress, a streaming approach is worth considering.\",\"const fs = require('fs').promises;\\n\\nasync function copyFile(source, destination) {\\n  try {\\n    await fs.copyFile(source, destination);\\n    console.log('The file was copied successfully');\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error('Error while copying:', error);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ncopyFile('source.txt', 'destination.txt');\\n\\nimport shutil\\n\\ntry:\\n    shutil.copy('source.txt', 'destination.txt')\\n    print(\\\"The file was copied successfully\\\")\\nexcept IOError as e:\\n    print(f\\\"I/O error: {e}\\\")\"],[1648,\"An Error usually means a serious problem the program cannot handle, for example running out of memory. An Exception is an exceptional situation that can be foreseen and handled, for example division by zero. Errors are often fatal and should not be caught, while exceptions are intended to be handled with try-catch. The distinction helps you write reliable code, where fatal failures stop the program and expected problems are handled.\",\"In programming the terms Error and Exception are often used to denote different kinds of abnormal situation. Understanding their differences is critical for writing resilient code that reacts correctly to failures.\\nWhat is an Error?\\nAn Error usually represents a serious problem arising at the level of the system or the virtual machine, which the application should not attempt to handle. These are fatal conditions, such as running out of memory (OutOfMemoryError), a stack overflow (StackOverflowError) or class-loading errors (ClassFormatError). In languages such as Java, Error is a subclass of Throwable but not of Exception. A program should generally not catch an Error, because they signal deep problems after which continuing to run may be unsafe.\\nWhat is an Exception?\\nAn Exception is an event that disrupts the normal flow of a program's execution but which can be foreseen and handled. Exceptions divide into checked and unchecked (runtime) ones. For example, FileNotFoundException (checked) or NullPointerException (unchecked). They inherit from Exception in Java. A developer should use try-catch blocks to handle exceptions and restore the program's operation or terminate the operation gracefully.\\nA code example in Java\\n\\nWhere is this distinction used?\\nThe division into Error and Exception is used in languages with exceptions, such as Java, C# and Python (although there the clear division is less strict). It helps to:\\n\\n- Clearly separate fatal system failures (Error) from a program's logical errors (Exception).\\n- Apply different handling strategies: exceptions are handled in the business logic, while errors often lead to an abnormal shutdown.\\n- Write more reliable code, where exception handling does not mask critical problems.\\nConclusion: Use exception handling for expected I/O problems, data validation or network failures. Errors usually indicate irrecoverable system problems — they should not be caught; instead you should log them and restart the application.\",\"public class ErrorVsException {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        // An example of handling an Exception\\n        try {\\n            int result = 10 / 0; // Throws ArithmeticException\\n        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {\\n            System.out.println(\\\"Exception caught: \\\" + e.getMessage());\\n        }\\n        \\n        // An Error is normally not caught, but technically it can be\\n        try {\\n            causeStackOverflow(); // May throw StackOverflowError\\n        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {\\n            // Not recommended!\\n            System.out.println(\\\"The error was caught, but continuing is unsafe\\\");\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    static void causeStackOverflow() {\\n        causeStackOverflow(); // Infinite recursion\\n    }\\n}\"],[1649,\"A standard Set (for example, HashSet in Java) does not guarantee the order of the elements. LinkedHashSet, however, preserves the insertion order, since it uses a linked list alongside the hash table. In JavaScript the Set object guarantees insertion order according to the ECMAScript 2015 specification. In Python 3.7+ an ordinary dict (and therefore a set, which uses a dict internally) also preserves insertion order as an implementation detail, and from Python 3.7 this is guaranteed.\",\"The Set data structure is a collection of unique elements. Its key property is the absence of duplicates, but the question of the elements' order depends on the specific implementation.\\nImplementations that guarantee order\\nIn various programming languages there are Set implementations that preserve the insertion order of the elements:\\n\\n- Java: LinkedHashSet — extends HashSet, adding a doubly linked list that remembers the order in which the elements were added. That lets you iterate over the elements in the order they were inserted.\\n- JavaScript: Set — according to the ECMAScript 2015 (ES6) specification and all subsequent ones, a Set object must iterate over its elements in insertion order. This is guaranteed by the standard.\\n- Python: set (from Python 3.7) — dictionaries in Python 3.7+ preserve insertion order as a guaranteed property of the language. Since set is implemented using a dict (to store the keys), it also inherits this behaviour. Insertion order in a set is therefore guaranteed.\\nCode examples in Java and Python\\nAn example in Java using LinkedHashSet:\\n\\nAn example in Python demonstrating the order in a set (Python 3.7+):\\n\\nWhere is this used?\\nOrdered Sets are useful in scenarios where sequence matters, for example:\\n\\n- Removing duplicates from a list while preserving the original order.\\n- Caching with an LRU (Least Recently Used) algorithm, where the order of access matters.\\n- Processing events or data where the chronological order is significant.\\nConclusion: Use LinkedHashSet in Java, the standard Set in JavaScript and set in Python 3.7+ when you need a collection of unique elements with a guaranteed insertion order. That simplifies the logic if the order matters for subsequent processing or display of the data.\",\"import java.util.LinkedHashSet;\\nimport java.util.Set;\\n\\npublic class OrderedSetExample {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        Set<String> orderedSet = new LinkedHashSet<>();\\n        orderedSet.add(\\\"zebra\\\");\\n        orderedSet.add(\\\"apple\\\");\\n        orderedSet.add(\\\"banana\\\");\\n        orderedSet.add(\\\"zebra\\\"); // The duplicate will not be added\\n        \\n        // The elements are printed in insertion order: zebra, apple, banana\\n        for (String item : orderedSet) {\\n            System.out.println(item);\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n# Create a set and add elements in a particular order\\nmy_set = set()\\nmy_set.add('zebra')\\nmy_set.add('apple')\\nmy_set.add('banana')\\nmy_set.add('zebra')  # The duplicate is ignored\\n\\n# When iterating, the order matches the insertion order (guaranteed from Python 3.7)\\nfor item in my_set:\\n    print(item)\\n# Output: zebra apple banana (the order may differ in Python < 3.7)\"],[1650,\"A stack frame is a block of memory on the call stack, allocated when a function is called. It holds the function's local variables, its arguments, the return address and other bookkeeping information. When the function finishes, its frame is removed from the stack, freeing the memory. This is what makes nested function calls and recursion possible, providing a clear order of execution.\",\"A stack frame is a fundamental concept in memory management and program execution. Every time a program calls a function, the runtime (for example, the interpreter or the compiler) creates a new stack frame and places it in the area of memory called the call stack. That frame serves as an isolated working space for that particular function call.\\n\\nWhat is stored in a stack frame?\\n\\nA stack frame usually contains the following elements:\\n\\n- The function's arguments (parameters): the values passed into the function.\\n\\n- Local variables: the variables declared inside the function.\\n\\n- The return address: a pointer to the place in the code to return to once the function finishes.\\n\\n- A pointer to the previous frame: a reference to the stack frame of the calling function, which lets the context be restored.\\n\\n- Other bookkeeping data: for example, the values of processor registers that need to be saved.\\n\\nHow does it work in practice?\\n\\nConsider a simple example in C. The stack works on the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle — the last frame added is removed first.\\n\\nWhen `multiply(x, y)` is called, a new stack frame is created. It holds copies of the arguments `a` and `b`, the local variable `result` and the return address into the `main` function. After `return result;` is evaluated, the `multiply` frame is destroyed, control returns to `main`, and the value is placed in the variable `product`.\\n\\nWhere is this used and why does it matter?\\n\\nUnderstanding stack frames is critical for:\\n\\n- Debugging: a stack trace shows the chain of functions that led to the error.\\n\\n- Performance analysis: deep chains of calls, or recursion, can lead to a stack overflow if there become too many frames.\\n\\n- Understanding scope: local variables live only in their own frame, which explains their isolation.\\n\\n- Working with recursion: every recursive call creates its own frame, holding its own state.\\n\\nConclusion: Knowledge of stack frames is essential for writing and debugging code deliberately, especially when working with recursive algorithms, deeply nested calls, or when optimising memory use. It is a basic concept that helps you understand how programs actually execute.\",\"#include <stdio.h>\\n\\nint multiply(int a, int b) {\\n    int result = a * b; // A local variable in multiply's frame\\n    return result;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int x = 5;\\n    int y = 3;\\n    int product = multiply(x, y); // A frame is created for multiply\\n    printf(\\\"%d\\\", product);\\n    return 0;\\n}\"],[1651,\"A JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler works while the program is running. It translates frequently used sections of bytecode or intermediate code into machine code for the particular processor. That avoids interpreting the same code over and over, speeding up execution. A JIT can apply optimisations based on data about the program's actual execution, which a static compiler cannot see.\",\"JIT compilation is a hybrid approach between interpretation and static compilation. A program is initially compiled into intermediate bytecode, which is platform-independent. While the program runs, a virtual machine (for example, the JVM for Java or V8 for JavaScript) interprets that bytecode. When certain sections of code (frequently called methods or \\\"hot\\\" loops) are executed many times, however, the JIT compiler kicks in.\\n\\nThe main stages of a JIT's work\\n\\n- Interpretation and profiling: At first the code is executed by the interpreter. The system gathers statistics: which methods are called most often, which conditional branches are taken, what the data types are.\\n\\n- Compiling the \\\"hot\\\" code: Based on the profile gathered, the JIT picks the hottest sections (hotspots) and compiles them into highly optimised machine code for the current hardware platform.\\n\\n- Runtime optimisations: A JIT can apply runtime-specific optimisations that are impossible with static compilation, for example inlining virtual calls based on the known types of the objects, or removing unused branches of code.\\n\\n- Caching the compiled code: The compiled machine code is kept in memory and used on subsequent calls, which removes the overhead of interpretation.\\n\\nAn example in the context of JavaScript (V8)\\n\\nThe V8 engine uses two JIT compilers: a baseline one (Sparkplug) and an optimising one (TurboFan). At first the code is compiled quickly into machine code with minimal optimisations. If a function is called many times, it is passed to the optimising compiler, which generates faster code based on assumptions about the data types. If an assumption is violated (for example, a variable receives a different type), the optimised code is discarded (deoptimisation happens) and execution falls back to the less optimised version.\\n\\nWhere JIT is used\\n\\nJIT compilation is a key technology for high-performance virtual machines and runtimes. It is widely used in:\\n\\n- The Java Virtual Machine (JVM) — HotSpot.\\n\\n- JavaScript engines (V8 in Chrome/Node.js, SpiderMonkey in Firefox).\\n\\n- The .NET runtime (the CLR).\\n\\n- Some implementations of Python (PyPy).\\n\\nConclusion: JIT compilation is worth using in environments where both the portability of bytecode and high performance matter. It lets you reach speeds close to statically compiled languages while keeping the advantages of interpreted languages, such as cross-platform support and dynamic capabilities.\",\"// A simplified illustration: a function that becomes \\\"hot\\\"\\nfunction calculateSum(array) {\\n    let sum = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n        sum += array[i]; // The JIT can optimise this loop,\\n                         // assuming array contains only numbers\\n    }\\n    return sum;\\n}\\n\\n// Calling it many times makes the JIT compile the function\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\\n    calculateSum([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);\\n}\\n// After many calls TurboFan will produce optimised\\n// machine code for this loop.\"],[1652,\"A garbage collector frees memory automatically, but it can cause problems. The main one is pauses (stop-the-world), when the application halts completely for the duration of a collection, which is critical for real-time systems. Memory leaks are also possible if objects are mistakenly retained by references, and the unpredictable timing of the GC makes it harder to ensure stable performance. In some cases the GC can consume significant CPU resources, competing with the application's main logic.\",\"A garbage collector (GC) is an automatic memory-management mechanism that frees objects no longer used by the program. Although it spares the developer from manual memory management and prevents many errors, its work can affect an application's behaviour negatively.\\nThe main problems with a garbage collector\\n\\n- Pauses (stop-the-world): Most GC algorithms require the application's execution to be halted completely for the duration of the collection. That leads to latency that may be unacceptable for real-time systems, high-load services or interactive applications (for example, games or financial trading platforms).\\n- Unpredictable timing: When the GC starts and how long it runs often depend on the current state of the heap and on the algorithm. That makes it harder to ensure a stable, predictable response time.\\n- Memory leaks: Despite the automation, leaks are still possible if references to objects are implicitly retained (for example, in caches, global variables or closures). The GC cannot collect such objects, which leads to growing memory consumption.\\n- CPU consumption: The process of finding and clearing garbage requires computing resources. At peak moments, or with suboptimal tuning, the GC can consume a significant share of the CPU, leaving fewer resources for the application's useful work.\\n- Memory fragmentation: After many cycles of allocation and freeing, the heap can become fragmented, which reduces the efficiency of allocating new objects and can increase pauses during compaction.\\nA code example illustrating a potential problem\\nConsider an example in Java, where careless use of collections can lead to a leak despite the GC's work.\\n\\nIn this example the static `cache` collection constantly retains references to large arrays, preventing them from being collected. This is the classic memory leak in a GC environment.\\nWhere this matters and how it is applied\\nUnderstanding the GC's problems is critical when developing high-performance systems (back-end services, game servers, embedded systems) where pauses are unacceptable. Various strategies are used to minimise the impact: choosing a GC algorithm (for example, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah in the JVM, or the generational GC in .NET), managing object life cycles manually, using object pooling and profiling memory.\\nConclusion: A garbage collector is a powerful tool, but it is not a silver bullet. It is worth using in most applications to improve reliability, but in systems with strict requirements for latency and predictability you need to understand its workings deeply, tune it carefully and account for its limitations when designing the architecture.\",\"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\npublic class GCDemo {\\n    private static List<byte[]> cache = new ArrayList<>();\\n\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        // Simulating work: constantly adding data to the \\\"cache\\\"\\n        while (true) {\\n            // Create a large object\\n            byte[] data = new byte[1024 * 1024]; // 1 MB\\n            cache.add(data);\\n            // ... some logic\\n            // The problem: we never clear cache,\\n            // so the objects never become garbage.\\n            // The GC cannot free them -> a memory leak.\\n            // The fix: clear cache periodically, or use WeakReference.\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\"],[1653,\"JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation is an approach where code is compiled not in advance but while the program is running. An interpreter or virtual machine first executes the code in interpreted mode, and then compiles the \\\"hot\\\" (frequently used) sections into machine code. That lets you combine the flexibility of interpretation (for example, cross-platform support) with the high execution speed of compiled code. JIT is used in environments such as the JVM (Java) and V8 (JavaScript).\",\"JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation is a hybrid approach to executing code that combines features of interpretation and of traditional (Ahead-Of-Time, AOT) compilation. The main idea is to postpone compiling the source code (for example, bytecode or an intermediate representation) into machine code until the moment the program actually executes it.\\n\\nHow JIT compilation works\\n\\nA typical process involves several stages:\\n\\n- Interpretation or initial execution: The program starts working, executing the code through an interpreter or a virtual machine. That lets it start quickly without a long initial compilation.\\n\\n- Profiling and analysis: The system tracks which functions or loops execute most often (the \\\"hot\\\" sections of the code).\\n\\n- Compilation on the fly: Those hot sections are compiled into optimised machine code specific to the current platform (CPU, OS).\\n\\n- Executing the compiled code: From then on, when those sections are called, the fast machine code runs rather than interpretation.\\n\\nWhere and why JIT is used\\n\\nJIT compilation has become a key technology for languages that aim for a balance between performance and portability.\\n\\n- Virtual machines (the JVM, the .NET CLR): Java and C# are compiled into portable bytecode. The virtual machine's JIT compiler converts that bytecode into machine code for the particular processor, which gives high performance, close to native languages.\\n\\n- JavaScript engines (V8, SpiderMonkey): Modern browsers use complex multi-tier JIT compilers. They analyse data types at runtime and generate highly optimised code for repeated operations.\\n\\n- Languages like Python (PyPy): Alternative implementations, such as PyPy for Python, use JIT to speed up the execution of an interpreted language significantly.\\n\\nAn example of how it works (conceptual)\\n\\nConsider a simplified pseudocode example illustrating the idea of a \\\"hot\\\" function.\\n\\nAdvantages and disadvantages\\n\\n- Advantages: High performance for hot code, cross-platform support (one bytecode for all), the possibility of optimisations based on runtime data (for example, inlining, specialisation by type).\\n\\n- Disadvantages: The overhead of compiling at runtime (the \\\"warm-up\\\"), increased memory consumption to hold the compiled code, and the complexity of implementing the JIT compiler itself.\\n\\nSummary: JIT compilation is worth using in environments where both portability (running the same code on different platforms) and high performance matter. It is indispensable for the virtual machines of general-purpose languages (Java, C#) and is the heart of modern high-speed JavaScript engines.\",\"// Source code in some language\\nfunction calculateSum(limit) {\\n    let total = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < limit; i++) {\\n        total += i;\\n    }\\n    return total;\\n}\\n\\n// The virtual machine first interprets this loop.\\n// After many calls (for example, 10000 times) the JIT compiler marks the function as \\\"hot\\\".\\n// It compiles it into something like native machine code:\\n// mov eax, 0; loop: add eax, ecx; dec ecx; jnz loop; ...\\n// Every subsequent call runs this fast machine code.\"],[1654,\"A circular dependency arises when two or more modules (classes, functions, files) depend on each other directly or indirectly, creating a closed loop. That leads to problems with compilation, testing and understanding the code. The main ways to solve it are: refactoring to remove the cycle (for example, moving shared logic into a third module), using the Mediator pattern, or injecting dependencies through interfaces/abstractions. In languages with lazy loading (such as Python) a cycle may work, but it is bad practice.\",\"A circular dependency is a situation in software design where two or more components (modules, classes, packages) refer to each other, forming a closed loop. For example, module A imports module B, and module B in turn imports module A. That creates an architectural problem, since it breaks the principle of a unidirectional flow of dependencies, complicates testing, can cause runtime errors (for example, an `ImportError` in Python) or compilation errors, and generally reduces the maintainability of the code.\\n\\nWhy is it a problem?\\n\\n- Difficulty of comprehension: The code becomes tangled, with the logic scattered around a loop.\\n\\n- Initialisation problems: With static module loading, the compiler/interpreter cannot work out which module to load first.\\n\\n- Difficulty of testing: It is impossible to test one module in isolation without loading the other.\\n\\n- The risk of infinite recursion: In some scenarios it can lead to infinite loops or a stack overflow.\\n\\nThe main solution strategies\\n\\nThere are several proven approaches to removing circular dependencies.\\n\\n1. Refactoring and extracting shared logic\\n\\nThe cleanest way is to revisit the design and break the cycle. A circular dependency often indicates that the modules have a shared responsibility that can be moved into a third, independent module.\\n\\n2. Using lazy loading or inversion of control (IoC)\\n\\nIn some frameworks (for example, Angular with its Dependency Injection, or Python thanks to its dynamic nature) you can resolve a cycle by injecting the dependency not in the constructor but later, via a setter or a method. This often masks the problem, however, rather than solving it.\\n\\n3. Applying the Mediator or Observer pattern\\n\\nIf modules have to communicate but should not know about each other directly, you can introduce an intermediary. The modules will then depend on a central event or bus rather than on each other.\\n\\nWhere does this apply?\\n\\nThe problem is relevant in any large project split into modules: a Node.js back end with many services, React/Redux front-end applications with interdependent actions and reducers, microservice architectures (where cycles between services are an anti-pattern). Solving it through interfaces and dependency inversion is the foundation of many modern frameworks (NestJS, Spring).\\n\\nConclusion: Circular dependencies are a sign of poor design that should be removed by refactoring at an early stage. Use extraction of shared logic, dependence on abstractions (interfaces) and patterns such as Mediator to create a clean, maintainable, testable architecture. Lazy loading is a temporary solution that may hide the problem rather than solve it.\",\"// THE PROBLEM: UserService depends on AuthService, and vice versa.\\n// class UserService {\\n//   constructor(private authService: AuthService) {}\\n//   getUser() { /* uses authService */ }\\n// }\\n// class AuthService {\\n//   constructor(private userService: UserService) {}\\n//   login() { /* uses userService */ }\\n// }\\n\\n// THE SOLUTION: Move the shared entity or interface into a separate module.\\n// Create the IUserRepository interface.\\ninterface IUserRepository {\\n  getUser(id: string): User;\\n}\\n\\n// UserService implements IUserRepository.\\nclass UserService implements IUserRepository {\\n  getUser(id: string) { /* the logic */ }\\n}\\n\\n// AuthService now depends only on the IUserRepository interface, not on the concrete UserService.\\nclass AuthService {\\n  constructor(private userRepo: IUserRepository) {}\\n  login() { /* uses userRepo.getUser() */ }\\n}\\n// The cycle is broken. The dependency has become unidirectional: AuthService -> IUserRepository <- UserService.\\n\\n# A Python example: breaking the cycle with an import inside a function (lazy loading).\\n# File a.py\\n# from b import B  # A direct import would create a cycle.\\nclass A:\\n    def do_something(self):\\n        from b import B  # The import happens only when the method is called.\\n        b_instance = B()\\n        return b_instance.process()\\n\\n# File b.py\\nfrom a import A  # This is now safe, as long as A does not import B at the top level.\\nclass B:\\n    def process(self):\\n        a_instance = A()\\n        return \\\"done\\\"\\n\\n// An example with a simple event bus.\\nclass EventBus {\\n  static emit(eventName, data) { /* ... */ }\\n  static on(eventName, callback) { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n\\n// Module A only emits an event.\\nclass ModuleA {\\n  action() {\\n    EventBus.emit('userAction', { userId: 123 });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Module B only subscribes to the event.\\nclass ModuleB {\\n  constructor() {\\n    EventBus.on('userAction', (data) => this.handleAction(data));\\n  }\\n  handleAction(data) { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n// A and B do not import each other. Depending on EventBus creates no cycle.\"],[1655,\"An immutable object is an object whose state cannot be changed after it is created. To implement one you need to: 1) make all the fields private and final/readonly, 2) provide no setters, 3) if the object holds references to mutable objects, return defensive copies of them. This provides thread safety and predictable state, and makes debugging easier.\",\"An immutable object is an object whose internal state is fixed at the moment of creation and cannot be changed by any external method afterwards. This is a fundamental concept in functional programming that is also widely used in object-oriented languages to create safe, predictable data structures.\\n\\nThe key requirements for an implementation\\n\\nFor an object to be genuinely immutable, several strict rules must be observed:\\n\\n- Declare the class `final` (in Java, C#) or use other mechanisms to prevent inheritance and the overriding of methods.\\n\\n- Make all the fields private and read-only (for example, `final` in Java, `readonly` in C#).\\n\\n- Provide no setter methods that change the object's state.\\n\\n- If the constructor takes references to mutable objects, you must create defensive copies of them to hold inside the object.\\n\\n- When returning references to internal mutable objects (through getters), you must likewise return defensive copies, so that external code cannot change the internal state.\\n\\nAn example in Java\\n\\nWhere and why it is used\\n\\nImmutable objects are critically important in multithreaded environments, since they are thread-safe by their very nature — several threads can safely read the same object without locks. They are also key components in state-management systems such as Redux, where the application's state must change only by creating new objects. That simplifies tracking changes, debugging and testing, since the object's state at any moment in time is known and fixed.\\n\\nConclusion: Immutable objects are worth using to represent values that should not change (for example, configurations, DTOs, pieces of state), especially in multithreaded applications and in architectures where predictability and the absence of side effects matter.\",\"import java.util.ArrayList;\\nimport java.util.Collections;\\nimport java.util.List;\\n\\npublic final class ImmutablePerson {\\n    private final String name;\\n    private final int age;\\n    private final List<String> hobbies; // A mutable object inside\\n\\n    // The constructor makes a copy of the list passed in\\n    public ImmutablePerson(String name, int age, List<String> hobbies) {\\n        this.name = name;\\n        this.age = age;\\n        this.hobbies = new ArrayList<>(hobbies); // Defensive copying\\n    }\\n\\n    public String getName() { return name; }\\n    public int getAge() { return age; }\\n    // The getter returns an unmodifiable view of the list\\n    public List<String> getHobbies() {\\n        return Collections.unmodifiableList(hobbies);\\n    }\\n\\n    // A method for \\\"changing\\\" it - returns a new object\\n    public ImmutablePerson withAge(int newAge) {\\n        return new ImmutablePerson(this.name, newAge, this.hobbies);\\n    }\\n}\"],[1656,\"Refactoring is needed when code becomes hard to understand and change. The key signals are: duplicated code, long methods or classes, a high level of coupling between modules, frequent bugs in one area, and difficulty adding new features. These signs indicate accumulating technical debt that slows development down. Timely refactoring reduces the risks and makes maintenance easier.\",\"Refactoring is the process of improving the internal structure of code without changing its external behaviour. Understanding when to start is critically important for keeping a project healthy. There are several categories of signal that indicate such work is needed.\\nKey metrics and \\\"code smells\\\"\\n\\n- Duplicated code: The same or very similar code appears in several places. That breaks the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle and makes changes harder to apply.\\n- The complexity of methods and classes: Methods that are too long (more than 20-30 lines) or giant classes performing many unrelated tasks. Cyclomatic complexity is a formal metric that helps measure this.\\n- High coupling and low cohesion: Modules depend heavily on each other (coupling), which makes changing one of them risky. Inside a module, meanwhile, the code may be poorly organised around a single responsibility (low cohesion).\\n- Difficulty testing: If it is hard to write unit tests for a module because of its many dependencies or tangled logic, that is a clear sign of design problems.\\nPractical signals during development\\n\\n- Developers spend a disproportionate amount of time understanding and changing a particular section of code.\\n- New features constantly require modifications to the same central module, which increases the risk of breakage.\\n- New bugs frequently appear in the same module or class (\\\"fragile\\\" code).\\n- The team starts to be afraid of making changes to certain parts of the system.\\nCode example: from a \\\"smell\\\" to refactoring\\nConsider a method that does too much:\\n\\nThis method breaks the single-responsibility principle. It can be split into several smaller methods or classes responsible for validation, calculation, saving and notification. That will simplify testing and let you change the logic in one area without touching the others.\\nConclusion: Refactoring is worth starting at the first signs of development slowing down and of a growing number of errors in specific modules. Proactively eliminating code smells prevents the accumulation of unmanageable technical debt, which costs far more in the future.\",\"// BAD: A long method with high coupling\\nfunction processOrder(order) {\\n    // Validation\\n    if (!order.id || !order.items.length) {\\n        throw new Error('Invalid order');\\n    }\\n    // The calculation logic\\n    let total = 0;\\n    for (let item of order.items) {\\n        total += item.price * item.quantity;\\n    }\\n    // Applying a discount\\n    if (order.customer.isPremium) {\\n        total *= 0.9;\\n    }\\n    // Saving to the database\\n    db.orders.save({...order, total: total});\\n    // Sending an email\\n    emailService.send(order.customer.email, `Your order total is ${total}`);\\n    // Logging\\n    console.log(`Order ${order.id} processed`);\\n    return total;\\n}\"],[1657,\"Refactoring is needed when code becomes hard to understand, change or test. The main signs are: duplicated code, long methods, large classes, tangled conditional statements. It is also worth considering refactoring when adding new functionality to a problematic module, or when code smells are discovered. Regular refactoring helps reduce technical debt and increases a team's long-term effectiveness.\",\"Refactoring is the process of improving the internal structure of code without changing its external behaviour. The decision about whether it is needed is based on a range of objective and subjective criteria, whose main goal is to preserve or restore such qualities of the code as readability, maintainability and extensibility.\\n\\nThe key signs that refactoring is needed\\n\\n- Code smells: These are indicators of potential problems. For example, duplicated code, long methods (more than 10-15 lines), large classes, chains of object calls (a.getB().getC().doSomething()).\\n\\n- Difficulty making changes: If adding a simple feature requires edits in many places, or if every change breaks non-obvious parts of the system.\\n\\n- Low test coverage or none at all: Code that is hard or impossible to cover with unit tests often has high coupling and needs refactoring to make it testable.\\n\\n- Violations of the SOLID principles and other best practices: For example, a class with many responsibilities (a breach of SRP), or modules with a strong circular dependency.\\n\\nPractical triggers for starting a refactor\\n\\nRefactoring is often initiated in specific working situations:\\n\\n- Before adding new functionality: If the code in the target area is already tangled, it is worth improving first, so that the new addition fits in cleanly.\\n\\n- When fixing a bug: If the cause of the bug lies in a poor structure, it makes sense not only to fix the error but to improve the surrounding code, to prevent the same thing in the future.\\n\\n- As part of the \\\"boy scout rule\\\": Leave the code a little better than you found it. Small improvements every time you touch a module.\\n\\nExample: refactoring a long method\\n\\nConsider a fragment of code that computes an order's total and produces a report. Initially it is all in one method.\\n\\nThis method does too much: it adds up the sum, applies taxes and discounts, and prints a report. It can be split into several small methods with clear names.\\n\\nSuch code is easier to read, test and change. For example, changing the tax calculation logic would affect only one small method.\\n\\nWhere and how to apply it\\n\\nRefactoring is not a one-off event but a constant practice. It is worth building into the workflow: setting aside time in sprints, making it part of code review (\\\"can this be simplified?\\\") and not being afraid to improve code that still \\\"works\\\" but already smells.\\n\\nConclusion: Refactoring is needed when the cost of maintaining and changing code starts to exceed the benefit of its current structure. It is worth doing when clear code smells are found, before adding functionality to a complex module, or as part of regular code hygiene to fight technical debt.\",\"function processOrder(order) {\\n    let total = 0;\\n    for (let item of order.items) {\\n        total += item.price * item.quantity;\\n        if (item.isTaxable) {\\n            total += item.price * item.quantity * 0.1; // 10% tax\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if (order.customer.isVIP) {\\n        total *= 0.9; // a 10% discount for VIPs\\n    }\\n    // ... another 20 lines of report-building logic\\n    console.log(`Order total: ${total}`);\\n    return total;\\n}\\n\\nfunction calculateSubtotal(items) { /* ... */ }\\nfunction applyTax(subtotal, items) { /* ... */ }\\nfunction applyDiscount(total, customer) { /* ... */ }\\nfunction generateReport(total) { /* ... */ }\\n\\nfunction processOrder(order) {\\n    let subtotal = calculateSubtotal(order.items);\\n    let totalWithTax = applyTax(subtotal, order.items);\\n    let finalTotal = applyDiscount(totalWithTax, order.customer);\\n    generateReport(finalTotal);\\n    return finalTotal;\\n}\"],[1658,\"Parameters in a GET request are passed as a query string appended to the URL after a question mark '?'. The query string consists of 'key=value' pairs separated by ampersands '&'. For example, in the URL 'https://api.example.com/search?q=term&limit=10' the parameters are: q=term and limit=10. The values must be encoded for safe transmission (for example, a space becomes %20). This format is easy to read and debug, but is not intended for transferring large volumes of data.\",\"GET requests in the HTTP protocol are used to retrieve data from a server. Since they should not contain a body, all the additional parameters needed to qualify the request are passed directly in the URL itself, in a special format called the query string.\\nThe structure of a query string\\nA query string begins with the '?' character after the URL's main path. It consists of one or more parameter pairs in the `key=value` format. The pairs are separated by the '&' character.\\n\\nIn this example three parameters are passed: `name` with the value \\\"John\\\", `age` with the value \\\"30\\\" and `city` with the value \\\"New York\\\" (the space encoded as %20).\\nEncoding parameters\\nSince URLs have a strict set of permitted characters (letters, digits and some special characters), every disallowed character in a parameter's value must be encoded using percent-encoding. For example, a space becomes %20, the '&' character becomes %26, and '=' becomes %3D. In JavaScript the `encodeURIComponent()` function is used for this.\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\nPassing parameters in a query string is the standard way to:\\n\\n- Filter, sort and paginate data in a RESTful API (for example, `?page=2&limit=20&sort=name`).\\n- Handle search queries.\\n- Pass session identifiers or tokens (although for sensitive data that is insecure).\\n- Create shareable links with preset parameters.\\nAn important limitation: the length of a URL is limited by the browser and the server (usually a few thousand characters), so to transfer large volumes of data you should use a POST request with a body.\\nSummary: The query-string format is a simple, universal mechanism for passing small, non-secret parameters in GET requests, and is fundamental to building readable, debuggable web interfaces and APIs.\",\"https://example.com/api/users?name=John&age=30&city=New%20York\\n\\n// An example of encoding a parameter in JavaScript\\nconst searchTerm = \\\"coffee & tea\\\";\\nconst encodedTerm = encodeURIComponent(searchTerm); // \\\"coffee%20%26%20tea\\\"\\nconst url = `https://api.com/search?q=${encodedTerm}`;\"],[1659,\"The body of an HTTP request can carry data in various formats, determined by the Content-Type header. The main types are: application/json for JSON data structures, application/x-www-form-urlencoded for simple key-value pairs (as in HTML forms), multipart/form-data for uploading files together with text fields, as well as text/plain, application/xml or even binary data (for example, image/jpeg). The server uses the Content-Type header to work out how to interpret the bytes it receives.\",\"The body of an HTTP request is used to transfer data from the client to the server, for example when submitting a form, creating a resource through an API or uploading a file. The specific format of that data is stated in the mandatory `Content-Type` header, which tells the server how to parse the stream of bytes it receives correctly.\\nThe main body formats\\n\\n- application/x-www-form-urlencoded: The standard format for HTML form data. The data is encoded as key=value pairs joined by the & character, with special characters replaced by percent-encoding. For example: `name=John+Doe&age=30`.\\n- multipart/form-data: Used when you need to send files together with text fields. The body is divided into parts by a boundary, and each part has its own headers and data. That lets binary data be transferred without loss.\\n- application/json: The most popular format for REST APIs. The data is sent as a JSON string, which is convenient for complex nested structures. For example: `{\\\"title\\\": \\\"Post\\\", \\\"published\\\": true}`.\\n- text/plain, application/xml, application/octet-stream: Also possible: plain text, XML or arbitrary binary data (for example, to upload an image or a PDF).\\nA code example sending different types\\nBelow is how to send requests with different content types using JavaScript (the Fetch API).\\n\\nThe choice of format depends on the task: JSON for APIs, form-urlencoded for simple forms, multipart for file uploads. Always state the correct `Content-Type`, or the server may fail to recognise the data.\",\"// 1. JSON\\nfetch('/api/users', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'Alice', age: 25 })\\n});\\n\\n// 2. Form-encoded data\\nconst formData = new URLSearchParams();\\nformData.append('username', 'john');\\nformData.append('password', 'secret');\\n\\nfetch('/login', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },\\n  body: formData\\n});\\n\\n// 3. Multipart (with a file)\\nconst multiPartForm = new FormData();\\nmultiPartForm.append('document', fileInput.files[0]);\\nmultiPartForm.append('comment', 'Please review');\\n\\nfetch('/upload', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  // The Content-Type header is NOT set explicitly; the browser sets it itself, with the boundary\\n  body: multiPartForm\\n});\"],[1660,\"Parameters in a POST request can be passed in several formats, determined by the Content-Type header. The main formats are: application/x-www-form-urlencoded (form data as key=value pairs separated by &), multipart/form-data (for uploading files; it separates the parts with a boundary) and application/json (structured data in JSON format). The choice of format depends on the type of data being sent and on the server's requirements.\",\"POST requests in HTTP are used to send data to a server, for example when submitting forms or calling an API. The way that data is encoded is determined by the `Content-Type` header in the request. Different formats suit different tasks.\\nThe main transfer formats\\n\\n- application/x-www-form-urlencoded: This is the standard format for HTML forms. The data is encoded into a string where key-value pairs are separated by the `&` character, and spaces and special characters are replaced by percent-encoding (for example, a space becomes `%20`).\\n- multipart/form-data: Used when you need to send files along with other form data. Each piece of data (a text field or a file) is separated by a unique boundary stated in the header. That prevents confusion with files' binary data.\\n- application/json: A popular format for REST APIs. The data is sent as a string in JSON format, which lets you send complex nested structures (objects, arrays).\\n- text/plain and application/xml: Used more rarely, to send plain text or XML documents respectively.\\nCode examples\\nSending data in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` format using the JavaScript Fetch API:\\n\\nSending data in `application/json` format:\\n\\nSending a form with a file (used automatically by the browser for forms with `enctype=\\\"multipart/form-data\\\"`).\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- application/x-www-form-urlencoded: Traditional web forms (login, registration).\\n- multipart/form-data: File-upload forms (avatars, documents).\\n- application/json: Modern RESTful APIs, mobile and SPA applications.\\nConclusion: The `application/json` format is preferable for APIs because of how structured and readable it is, while `multipart/form-data` is necessary for file uploads and `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` remains the standard for simple HTML forms.\",\"fetch('https://api.example.com/submit', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded',\\n  },\\n  body: 'username=johndoe&password=secret123'\\n});\\n\\nfetch('https://api.example.com/data', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: {\\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json',\\n  },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({\\n    title: 'Post Title',\\n    body: 'This is the content.',\\n    userId: 1\\n  })\\n});\"],[1661,\"Serialisation is the process of converting an object or data structure into a format that can be stored (for example, in a file) or transferred over a network (for example, as a JSON string or a binary stream). Deserialisation is the reverse process of restoring the object from that format. This lets data be exchanged between different systems, programming languages or application sessions. For example, a web server serialises an object into JSON to send to a client, and the client deserialises the JSON back into a JavaScript object.\",\"Serialisation and deserialisation are fundamental concepts in programming to do with converting data. They let you save an object's state or transfer it between different parts of a system, which may run in different languages or different processes.\\nThe main goal\\nThe main goal is to turn complex data structures (objects, arrays, graphs of objects) into a flat sequence of bytes or a string. This is necessary because data in memory exists in a format specific to the language and environment, which cannot be written to a file or sent over a network directly.\\nCommon formats\\n\\n- Textual (human-readable): JSON, XML, YAML. Widely used in web APIs and configuration files.\\n- Binary (compact and fast): Protocol Buffers (protobuf), MessagePack, Apache Avro, languages' own formats (for example, pickle in Python). Used in high-load systems where performance and data size matter.\\nAn example in Python with JSON\\nJSON is one of the most popular formats thanks to its simplicity and its support in many languages.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Web development: Exchanging data between the client and the server (REST/GraphQL APIs).\\n- Databases and caching: Storing objects in key-value stores such as Redis.\\n- Microservices and IPC: Passing messages between services through brokers (RabbitMQ, Kafka).\\n- Saving state: Writing an application's settings or a user's session to a file.\\nIt is important to keep security in mind, especially when deserialising data from untrusted sources, since that can lead to arbitrary code execution (for example, the vulnerabilities in Python's pickle).\\nConclusion: Serialisation is worth using whenever you need to save data outside RAM or transfer it across the boundaries of processes, languages or networks. The choice of format (JSON, protobuf and so on) depends on the requirements for readability, speed and data size.\",\"import json\\n\\n# The original Python object (a dictionary)\\ndata = {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice\\\",\\n    \\\"age\\\": 30,\\n    \\\"skills\\\": [\\\"Python\\\", \\\"JavaScript\\\"]\\n}\\n\\n# Serialisation: object -> JSON string\\njson_string = json.dumps(data)\\nprint(\\\"Serialised:\\\", json_string)\\n# Output: {\\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice\\\", \\\"age\\\": 30, \\\"skills\\\": [\\\"Python\\\", \\\"JavaScript\\\"]}\\n\\n# Deserialisation: JSON string -> Python object\\nrestored_data = json.loads(json_string)\\nprint(\\\"Deserialised, the name:\\\", restored_data[\\\"name\\\"]) # Alice\"],[1662,\"In a REST API the main HTTP methods correspond to the CRUD operations. GET is used to retrieve data, POST to create a new resource, PUT to fully update an existing resource, and DELETE to remove it. The PATCH method is used for a partial update. These methods provide a uniform interface, which is a key principle of REST.\",\"A REST API uses the standard HTTP methods to perform operations on resources, which makes the interface predictable and conforms to the principle of a uniform interface. Every method has strictly defined semantics that clients and servers must observe for things to work correctly.\\nThe main methods and their purpose\\n\\n- GET: Requests a representation of a resource. It must be safe (not change the server's state) and idempotent (repeated requests give the same result). Used to read data.\\n- POST: Used to create a new resource. It is neither safe nor idempotent. The server usually assigns a new URI for the created resource.\\n- PUT: Used to fully update a resource at a known URI. If the resource does not exist, it may be created. The method is idempotent.\\n- DELETE: Deletes the resource at the specified URI. The method is idempotent.\\n- PATCH: Used for a partial update of a resource. It is not idempotent by default, but should be implemented so that repeated requests with the same data give the same result.\\nUsage examples in code\\nConsider a simple API for managing a to-do list.\\n\\nWhere and how they are used\\nUsing the HTTP methods correctly is critical for building comprehensible, maintainable, scalable APIs. It lets developers and tools (for example, Swagger/OpenAPI) generate documentation and client libraries automatically. Observing the methods' semantics also matters for caching (especially of GET requests) and for security.\\nConclusion: Use the standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH) in accordance with their semantics to design clean, predictable RESTful APIs. That simplifies integration, improves other developers' understanding of the API and lets you take advantage of HTTP's built-in capabilities, such as caching.\",\"// Get the list of all tasks\\nfetch('/api/tasks', { method: 'GET' });\\n\\n// Create a new task\\nfetch('/api/tasks', {\\n  method: 'POST',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ title: 'A new task' })\\n});\\n\\n// Fully update the task with id=1\\nfetch('/api/tasks/1', {\\n  method: 'PUT',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ title: 'An updated task', completed: true })\\n});\\n\\n// Delete the task with id=1\\nfetch('/api/tasks/1', { method: 'DELETE' });\\n\\n// Partially update the task with id=1 (only the completed field)\\nfetch('/api/tasks/1', {\\n  method: 'PATCH',\\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ completed: true })\\n});\"],[1663,\"Content-Type is an HTTP header that tells the client or the server what type of data the body of a request or response contains. It uses MIME types, for example application/json for JSON or text/html for HTML pages. Without the correct Content-Type a browser may display a page incorrectly, and a server may fail to parse the data from a request. Setting this header is a mandatory practice for web applications to work correctly.\",\"The `Content-Type` HTTP header is key to determining the format of the data carried in the body of an HTTP request or response. It tells the recipient (for example, a browser or a server application) how to interpret the bytes received. Without this header the client and the server are forced to guess the content type, which often leads to errors.\\n\\nWhat are MIME types?\\n\\nThe value of the `Content-Type` header is given as a MIME type (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions). A MIME type consists of a type and a subtype separated by a slash. For example:\\n\\n- `text/html` — for HTML documents.\\n\\n- `application/json` — for data in JSON format.\\n\\n- `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` — for form data sent from a browser.\\n\\n- `multipart/form-data` — for sending files through forms.\\n\\nSometimes a character encoding is added to the type, for example: `text/html; charset=utf-8`.\\n\\nIts role in requests and responses\\n\\nIn an HTTP response the server uses `Content-Type` to tell the browser how to handle the content it receives. If the server sends HTML with the type `text/html`, the browser renders the page. If the type is `text/plain`, however, the browser simply displays the HTML source as text.\\n\\nIn an HTTP request (for example, POST or PUT) the client (a browser or another client) states this header so that the server knows what format the data arrived in and can parse it correctly.\\n\\nPractical examples\\n\\nAn example HTTP response from a server with JSON data:\\n\\nAn example of sending a POST request with JSON from JavaScript using the Fetch API:\\n\\nIf you forget to state `Content-Type: 'application/json'` in that request, many server frameworks (for example, Express.js) will be unable to recognise the JSON in the request body automatically, and `req.body` will turn out to be empty.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nSetting the `Content-Type` header correctly is critically important for interoperability between client and server in web development. It lets you avoid data-parsing errors and guarantees that the content is handled exactly as the developer intended.\",\"HTTP/1.1 200 OK\\nContent-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8\\n\\n{\\\"status\\\": \\\"success\\\", \\\"data\\\": {\\\"id\\\": 123}}\\n\\nfetch('/api/users', {\\n    method: 'POST',\\n    headers: {\\n        'Content-Type': 'application/json'\\n    },\\n    body: JSON.stringify({ name: 'John', age: 30 })\\n});\"],[1664,\"The Provider pattern is a way of passing data (for example, state or functions) deep into a component tree, bypassing the intermediate levels. It solves the problem of \\\"prop drilling\\\", where props have to be passed through many components that do not use them themselves. In React the Context API is often used for this, where a Provider wraps part of the application and supplies a value to all its child components. That makes the code cleaner and simplifies managing global state.\",\"The Provider pattern is an architectural approach that lets you \\\"provide\\\" particular data or services to components in a hierarchy without passing them explicitly through props at every level. The main goal is to avoid cumbersome prop drilling, where data passes through many intermediate components, which complicates maintenance and the reuse of code.\\n\\nHow a Provider works in React\\n\\nIn the React ecosystem this pattern is most often implemented through the built-in Context API. You create a context with `React.createContext()`, which returns an object with two components: `Provider` and `Consumer` (or the `useContext` hook is used). The `Provider` component takes a `value` prop and wraps part of the component tree. Any child component inside that wrapper can gain access to the `value` by subscribing to the context.\\n\\nA code example with React Context\\n\\nWhere else is the Provider pattern used?\\n\\nBesides React Context, the Provider idea underpins many state-management libraries (for example, Redux, MobX, Zustand). In Redux the `<Provider store={store}>` component makes the Redux store available to all connected components. The pattern is also widespread in other frameworks and languages as a form of dependency injection, where a \\\"provider\\\" registers a service and then \\\"injects\\\" it into client classes.\\n\\nConclusion: The Provider pattern is worth using when you need to share data (a theme, authentication, a locale, global state) between many components at different levels of nesting. It reduces coupling in the code, frees you from passing props manually and centralises the data-management logic.\",\"// 1. Create the context\\nexport const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\n\\n// 2. The application component, which provides the value\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('dark');\\n\\n  return (\\n    // The Provider wraps the child components\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{ theme, setTheme }}>\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// 3. An intermediate component (it receives no props explicitly)\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  return <ThemedButton />;\\n}\\n\\n// 4. The child component, which consumes the value\\nfunction ThemedButton() {\\n  // The useContext hook subscribes to the nearest Provider up the tree\\n  const { theme, setTheme } = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button\\n      onClick={() => setTheme(prev => prev === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark')}\\n    >\\n      Toggle Theme\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\"],[1665,\"A REST API is an architectural style for building web services, based on the principles of stateless client-server interaction. It models data as resources accessed through the standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE). Every resource has a unique identifier (a URI), and the data exchange usually happens in JSON or XML format. This approach makes an API simple, scalable and independent of client technologies.\",\"REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style, not a protocol or a standard. It describes a set of constraints for building distributed systems in which clients interact with servers through the standard HTTP methods, addressing resources represented as unique URIs.\\n\\nThe key principles of REST\\n\\n- Client-server architecture: a separation of responsibilities: the client manages the interface, the server the data and business logic.\\n\\n- Stateless: every request from the client must contain all the information needed to process it. The server stores no session state between requests.\\n\\n- Caching: responses should state explicitly whether they can be cached, to improve performance.\\n\\n- A uniform interface: a unified way of interacting through resources, representations and self-describing messages.\\n\\n- A layered system: the architecture may consist of several layers (proxies, load balancers), which improves scalability.\\n\\nHow a REST API is used\\n\\nREST APIs are widely used in web development and mobile applications to exchange data between the front end and the back end. For example, an online shop may provide an API to get a list of products, create an order or update a user's profile.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConsider a simple REST API for managing a to-do list. The resource is a task, available at the URI `/tasks`.\\n\\nThis example shows how the HTTP methods correspond to the CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations on the \\\"task\\\" resource.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nA REST API is worth using when you need a simple, standardised, scalable way for different systems to interact, especially in web and mobile development. It is ideal for scenarios where the client's independence from the server and the possibility of caching matter.\",\"// An example in Node.js with Express\\nconst express = require('express');\\nconst app = express();\\napp.use(express.json());\\n\\nlet tasks = [{ id: 1, title: \\\"Learn REST\\\", done: false }];\\n\\n// GET /tasks - get all the tasks\\napp.get('/tasks', (req, res) => {\\n  res.json(tasks);\\n});\\n\\n// POST /tasks - create a new task\\napp.post('/tasks', (req, res) => {\\n  const newTask = { id: tasks.length + 1, ...req.body };\\n  tasks.push(newTask);\\n  res.status(201).json(newTask);\\n});\\n\\n// PUT /tasks/:id - update a task\\napp.put('/tasks/:id', (req, res) => {\\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\\n  const taskIndex = tasks.findIndex(t => t.id === id);\\n  if (taskIndex === -1) return res.status(404).send('Task not found');\\n  tasks[taskIndex] = { ...tasks[taskIndex], ...req.body };\\n  res.json(tasks[taskIndex]);\\n});\\n\\n// DELETE /tasks/:id - delete a task\\napp.delete('/tasks/:id', (req, res) => {\\n  tasks = tasks.filter(t => t.id !== parseInt(req.params.id));\\n  res.status(204).send();\\n});\\n\\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('The server is running on port 3000'));\"],[1666,\"A REST API is an architectural style for building web services, based on several key principles. It uses the standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) for operations on resources, which are identified by URIs. An important characteristic is statelessness: every request from the client contains all the information needed to process it. That distinguishes REST from approaches that store session state on the server, and makes it more scalable and easier to cache.\",\"REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style, not a protocol or a standard. It defines a set of constraints which, when applied to the design of a system, result in scalable, performant, easily maintainable web services.\\n\\nThe key principles (constraints) of REST\\n\\n- A uniform interface: This is the fundamental principle. Resources are identified by URIs, and interaction with them happens through the standard HTTP methods. Resources are separate from their representations (for example, JSON or XML), and clients can manipulate resources through those representations.\\n\\n- Statelessness: The server stores no client session state between requests. Every request must contain all the information needed to understand and execute it. That improves reliability and simplifies horizontal scaling.\\n\\n- Cacheability: The server's responses must state explicitly whether they can be cached and for how long. That significantly reduces the load on the server and improves performance.\\n\\n- Client-server architecture: A clear separation of responsibilities lets the client and server components evolve independently.\\n\\n- A layered system: The architecture may consist of several layers (proxies, load balancers, gateways), with the client not knowing which layer exactly it is interacting with.\\n\\n- Code on demand (optional): The server may temporarily extend the client's functionality by sending executable code (for example, JavaScript).\\n\\nDifferences from other approaches\\n\\nThe main differences from, say, SOAP or RPC-like APIs (including GraphQL) lie in the philosophy. REST is oriented around resources and the standard HTTP methods, whereas SOAP is a protocol oriented around operations (actions) and strict contracts (WSDL). GraphQL, for its part, gives the client flexibility in the data it requests, but does not necessarily follow all of REST's constraints, such as caching at the HTTP level.\\n\\nAn example of RESTful interaction\\n\\nThis example shows how a URI (`/api/users`) identifies the \\\"users\\\" resource, while the HTTP method determines the action. Responses may have different status codes (200 OK, 201 Created, 404 Not Found), which is likewise part of the uniform interface.\\n\\nConclusion: A REST API should be used when you need a simple, standardised, scalable and easily cacheable way for distributed systems to interact, especially in public web services and microservice architectures. Its principles make an API predictable and comprehensible to developers.\",\"// Getting the list of users (resource: /users)\\nGET /api/users HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nAccept: application/json\\n\\n// Creating a new user\\nPOST /api/users HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice\\\",\\n  \\\"email\\\": \\\"alice@example.com\\\"\\n}\\n\\n// Updating the user with id=123\\nPUT /api/users/123 HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice Smith\\\",\\n  \\\"email\\\": \\\"alice.smith@example.com\\\"\\n}\\n\\n// Deleting the user with id=123\\nDELETE /api/users/123 HTTP/1.1\\nHost: example.com\"],[1667,\"A custom alert on top of every screen is implemented by creating an element outside the main DOM tree, to avoid the influence of the parent's styles. In React a React Portal is used for this, mounting the component into a separate div, for example in the body. You need to manage the z-index so that the alert sits above other elements, and handle events (clicks outside the alert, the Escape key) to close it. It is also important to provide accessibility (ARIA attributes) and to block scrolling of the background page.\",\"Creating a custom alert that has to be displayed on top of every interface element is a common task in web development. The main difficulty is making sure the component is not constrained by the styles (for example, `overflow: hidden`) of its parent container in the DOM tree and has priority on the Z axis (sits above other elements).\\nThe key implementation principles\\n\\n- Leaving the document flow: The alert must render outside the main component tree, usually directly into `<body>`. That solves the problem with the ancestors' CSS properties.\\n- Managing the z-index: The alert element and its overlay (the semi-transparent background) need a high `z-index` value, for example 9999 or 10000.\\n- Accessibility (a11y): It is important to add ARIA attributes (`role=\\\"alertdialog\\\"`, `aria-labelledby`, `aria-modal=\\\"true\\\"`) and to manage focus (focus should move inside the alert and be \\\"trapped\\\" there).\\n- Managing state: You need to handle closing on a click on the overlay, on the Escape key and on the \\\"OK\\\" button inside the alert.\\nAn implementation example with a React Portal\\nReact provides the `ReactDOM.createPortal` API to render children into a DOM node that exists outside the parent component's hierarchy.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is used not only for alerts but also for modal windows, toasts (notifications), sidebars and action-confirmation dialogs. In large applications this logic is often moved into a global context or a state manager (for example, Redux) so that notifications can be shown from anywhere in the code under central control.\\nConclusion: Custom alerts on top of the screen are worth implementing through portals (or their equivalents in other frameworks) to guarantee they appear above the UI. That provides isolation from the parents' styles, simplifies z-index management and makes the component a reusable global interface element.\",\"import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\nimport ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\n\\nconst CustomAlert = ({ message, isOpen, onClose }) => {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const handleEscape = (e) => {\\n      if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();\\n    };\\n    if (isOpen) {\\n      document.addEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n      document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden'; // Block scrolling\\n    }\\n    return () => {\\n      document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);\\n      document.body.style.overflow = 'unlocked';\\n    };\\n  }, [isOpen, onClose]);\\n\\n  if (!isOpen) return null;\\n\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <>\\n      <div className=\\\"alert-overlay\\\" onClick={onClose} />\\n      <div className=\\\"alert-container\\\" role=\\\"alertdialog\\\" aria-modal=\\\"true\\\">\\n        <p>{message}</p>\\n        <button onClick={onClose}>OK</button>\\n      </div>\\n    </>,\\n    document.body\\n  );\\n};\\n\\n// The styles (CSS)\\n// .alert-overlay {\\n//   position: fixed;\\n//   top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0;\\n//   background: rgba(0,0,0,0.5);\\n//   z-index: 9999;\\n// }\\n// .alert-container {\\n//   position: fixed;\\n//   top: 50%; left: 50%;\\n//   transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\\n//   background: white;\\n//   padding: 2rem;\\n//   border-radius: 8px;\\n//   z-index: 10000;\\n// }\"],[1668,\"To implement Equatable for a generic type, the generic type itself must also conform to the Equatable protocol. That lets the compiler guarantee that comparison operators are defined for the inner type. In Swift this is done with a type constraint in the generic's declaration: `struct Box<T: Equatable>: Equatable`. Then, in the implementation of the `==` method, we can compare values of type T using the standard operator.\",\"Implementing the Equatable protocol for a generic struct or class requires that the inner (parameterised) type also support comparison. That is necessary because the compiler has to know how to compare values of that type for the equality operator to work correctly.\\nThe main approach\\nIn Swift you declare conformance to the Equatable protocol for your generic type by adding a constraint to the type parameter. That guarantees that any concrete type substituted for the generic parameter will also conform to Equatable.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\nSuch an implementation is often used in:\\n\\n- Custom collections (for example, queues, stacks) that have to be compared by their contents.\\n- Data models where one of the fields is a generic type (for example, Result<Value, Error>).\\n- Testing, to check the equality of objects in assertions.\\nAn example with an enum\\nConsider a more complex case with an enum that has associated values.\\n\\nThe Swift compiler is capable of synthesising the Equatable implementation for an enum if all its associated values also conform to Equatable. That keeps the code cleaner.\\nConclusion\\nUse the type constraint `T: Equatable` when declaring a generic struct, to implement comparison safely. This approach provides type safety and lets the code be reused for any types that support equality.\",\"struct Container<T: Equatable>: Equatable {\\n    let value: T\\n    let id: Int\\n\\n    static func == (lhs: Container, rhs: Container) -> Bool {\\n        // We can use == on T, because T: Equatable\\n        return lhs.id == rhs.id && lhs.value == rhs.value\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nenum Result<Success: Equatable, Failure: Equatable>: Equatable {\\n    case success(Success)\\n    case failure(Failure)\\n\\n    // The == implementation is generated automatically by the Swift compiler,\\n    // because every associated type conforms to Equatable.\\n}\"],[1669,\"A vtable (virtual table) is an internal data structure that the C++ compiler creates for every class containing virtual functions. It is an array of pointers to those functions. When a virtual method is called through a pointer or reference to the base class, the program looks up the required function address in the object's vtable and performs an indirect call. That is what makes polymorphism possible, where the same code can work with objects of different derived classes.\",\"A vtable (virtual table) is the key mechanism for implementing dynamic polymorphism in languages like C++. When a class declares one or more virtual functions, the compiler implicitly adds a hidden pointer (often called the vptr) to every object of that class, referring to the table of virtual functions unique to that class.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nFor every class with virtual functions the compiler creates a static table in memory. That table contains the addresses of the implementations of all that class's virtual functions, in the order they were declared. When an object is created, its hidden vptr is initialised with the address of its class's vtable.\\n\\nA code example and the internal structure\\n\\nIn memory, an object of Derived will have roughly this structure:\\n\\n- The vptr pointer → points at the Derived class's vtable.\\n\\n- The data member from Base.\\n\\n- Possibly additional members of Derived.\\n\\nDerived's vtable will contain: [the address of Derived::func1, the address of Base::func2].\\n\\nWhere and how it is used\\n\\nThis mechanism is used wherever polymorphic behaviour is required: in libraries with class hierarchies, in frameworks, in design patterns (for example, Strategy, Observer). A call through the vtable adds a small overhead (one extra pointer dereference and an array access) but provides flexibility and conformance with the Liskov substitution principle.\\n\\nConclusion: The vtable is the fundamental implementation of polymorphism in C++. It is worth understanding in order to write efficient object-oriented code and to debug complex class hierarchies, although in everyday development it is hidden from the programmer.\",\"class Base {\\npublic:\\n    virtual void func1() { /* the Base::func1 implementation */ }\\n    virtual void func2() { /* the Base::func2 implementation */ }\\n    int data;\\n};\\n\\nclass Derived : public Base {\\npublic:\\n    void func1() override { /* the overridden implementation */ }\\n    // func2 is not overridden; the version from Base will be used\\n};\\n\\n// An example of use\\nBase* ptr = new Derived();\\nptr->func1(); // A dynamic call to Derived::func1 through the vtable\"],[1670,\"When `sync` is called inside a serial queue, the task is added to that same queue and runs immediately, blocking the current thread until it finishes. If the `sync` call is made from a task already running on that same serial queue, and we try to submit a new task synchronously to the same queue, that leads to a deadlock, because the queue will wait for the current task to finish while the current task waits for the new one to start.\",\"A serial queue in GCD guarantees that tasks run strictly one after another. The `dispatch_sync` method (or `sync` in Swift) submits a task to the queue and blocks the current thread until it completes.\\nThe ordinary case\\nIf the `sync` call happens from outside the serial queue (for example, from the main thread or another queue), the task is placed at the end of the serial queue and runs as soon as the queue is free. The current thread is blocked for the duration of that task.\\n\\nThe deadlock case\\nThe problem arises when the `sync` call is made from a task already running on that same serial queue. Since the queue is serial, it cannot start a new task until it has finished the current one. But the current task is waiting for the new task to start and finish. That mutual waiting leads to a deadlock.\\n\\nIn this example the outer asynchronous task occupies the serial queue. Inside it we try to submit another task synchronously to the same queue. The queue waits for the outer task to finish, but the outer task waits for the inner one to start — a deadlock.\\nPractical application and conclusion\\nUnderstanding this behaviour is critical for avoiding deadlocks in multithreaded code. Serial queues are often used to synchronise access to shared resources. Synchronous calls on them are useful when you need to wait for a result, but they must be used carefully, avoiding calls into the same queue from inside it.\\nSummary: Calling `sync` inside a serial queue is safe if it is not made from a task running on that same queue. Otherwise it leads to a deadlock. Use `async`, or make sure the call comes from a different thread/queue.\",\"let serialQueue = DispatchQueue(label: \\\"com.example.serial\\\")\\nserialQueue.async {\\n    print(\\\"Task 1\\\")\\n}\\n// A call from the main thread\\nserialQueue.sync {\\n    print(\\\"Task 2\\\") // Runs after Task 1\\n}\\nprint(\\\"After sync\\\") // Printed after Task 2 finishes\\n\\nlet serialQueue = DispatchQueue(label: \\\"com.example.serial\\\")\\nserialQueue.async {\\n    // This task is already running on serialQueue\\n    serialQueue.sync {\\n        print(\\\"This code will never run\\\")\\n    }\\n    print(\\\"This will not run either\\\")\\n}\"],[1671,\"ForEach in SwiftUI is not a loop but a view structure that dynamically creates child views for every element in a collection. It requires the data to conform to the Identifiable protocol, or for you to specify the key path to the identifier explicitly. ForEach is often used inside a List, a VStack or other containers to display repeating content, for example a list of messages. It updates the interface automatically when the underlying collection changes, providing reactivity.\",\"ForEach in SwiftUI is not a traditional for-in loop but a special structure that is itself a View, used to build a view hierarchy dynamically from a collection of data. Its main job is to turn data into an interface, following the principles of declarative UI.\\n\\nHow ForEach works\\n\\nForEach takes a collection of data (for example, an array) and a closure that returns a View for each element. The key requirement is that every element of the collection must be uniquely identifiable. That is necessary so that SwiftUI can update the interface efficiently when the data changes, without rebuilding every view from scratch.\\n\\nThe Identifiable requirement\\n\\nThere are two main ways to provide identification:\\n\\n- Use a collection of elements conforming to the `Identifiable` protocol, which requires an `id` property.\\n\\n- Specify the key path to the property that serves as the identifier explicitly, using the `id:` parameter.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nAn example with a model conforming to `Identifiable`:\\n\\nAn example with the identifier specified explicitly (for example, for strings):\\n\\nUse and particular features\\n\\nForEach is most often used inside container views such as `List`, `VStack`, `HStack` or `LazyVGrid`. It is integrated with SwiftUI's reactive update system: when the underlying array changes (for example, when elements are added, removed or moved), the interface is animated accordingly, automatically. It is important to remember that ForEach is not a container in itself — it has to be placed inside another View.\\n\\nConclusion: Use ForEach in SwiftUI whenever you need to display a list or grid of elements from a dynamic collection of data. It is a fundamental tool for creating repeating, reactive interfaces that delivers performance thanks to the unique identification of every element.\",\"struct Item: Identifiable {\\n    let id = UUID()\\n    let name: String\\n}\\n\\nstruct ContentView: View {\\n    let items = [Item(name: \\\"Apple\\\"), Item(name: \\\"Banana\\\")]\\n    \\n    var body: some View {\\n        List {\\n            ForEach(items) { item in\\n                Text(item.name)\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nlet colors = [\\\"Red\\\", \\\"Green\\\", \\\"Blue\\\"]\\n\\nForEach(colors, id: \\\\.self) { color in\\n    Text(color)\\n}\"],[1672,\"A RunLoop is an event-processing loop that manages the execution of tasks on a thread. In every RunLoop cycle an autorelease pool is created and released. Objects placed in that pool with autorelease are freed at the end of the RunLoop iteration, when the pool is drained. That prevents temporary objects accumulating in memory and helps ARC free memory promptly. The RunLoop therefore organises periodic memory cleanup through autorelease pools.\",\"A RunLoop is a fundamental pattern in event-driven programming systems, especially on iOS/macOS. It continuously processes incoming events (touches, timers, network data) and schedules tasks. Every iteration of the RunLoop is tied to the draining (releasing) phase of an autorelease pool, which directly affects memory management in an environment with automatic reference counting (ARC).\\n\\nHow the RunLoop's relationship with memory works\\n\\nIn Objective-C and Swift (with Cocoa support) objects can be marked `autorelease`. That means their lifetime is extended to the end of the current autorelease pool. The RunLoop for the main thread creates and drains such a pool on every iteration. For example, when a touch event is handled, temporary objects are created (for example, strings, arrays). If they end up in the autorelease pool, they are freed after the handling of that event finishes, rather than immediately on leaving the local scope.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nConsider code that runs in a button-press event handler:\\n\\nIn this example the `temporaryArray` array may be placed in the autorelease pool automatically by the compiler. At the end of the RunLoop cycle the pool is drained and the memory is freed. Without that, temporary objects could accumulate if the loop did not provide regular cleanup.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- The main UI thread: The main thread's RunLoop manages the pools automatically, which is critical for the interface's responsiveness and for preventing memory leaks.\\n\\n- Background threads: When creating your own threads, the developer has to add a RunLoop and autorelease pools manually to ensure memory is managed correctly.\\n\\n- Optimisation: In loops that create many temporary objects you can create nested autorelease pools to free memory more often.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nThe RunLoop provides structured memory management by draining autorelease pools regularly, which lets ARC free temporary objects efficiently and prevents memory consumption growing prematurely. This matters especially in mobile development, where resources are limited and the interface must stay smooth.\",\"// An example in Swift\\n@IBAction func buttonTapped(_ sender: UIButton) {\\n    // A temporary array is created, which may be placed in the autorelease pool\\n    let temporaryArray = (0..<10000).map { _ in \\\"temp\\\" }\\n    // Using the array\\n    print(temporaryArray.count)\\n    // After the method finishes, if the object was autoreleased,\\n    // it will be freed at the end of the current RunLoop iteration.\\n}\"],[1673,\"An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache is a data structure that holds a limited number of elements. When the cache fills up, it removes the element that has gone longest without being accessed. This is useful for speeding up access to frequently used data, for example in web caches or databases. The implementation often uses a combination of a hash table for fast lookup and a doubly linked list to track the order of use.\",\"An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache is a popular cache-management strategy that limits the number of elements stored. When the cache is full and a new element has to be added, the one accessed longest ago is removed. This is based on the assumption that recently used data is more likely to be needed again in the near future.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nThe main idea is to maintain the order of the elements by the time they were last used. On every access (a read or a write) the element is moved to the front of the list as \\\"the most recent\\\". When the cache overflows, the element at the end of the list (\\\"the oldest\\\") is removed.\\n\\nA typical implementation\\n\\nFor efficient operation (O(1) per operation) a combination is used:\\n\\n- A hash table (Map): for fast access to an element by its key.\\n\\n- A doubly linked list: to maintain the order of the elements from the most recent to the oldest.\\n\\nA code example in Python\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nLRU caches are widely used in systems where it matters to limit memory use and to predict which data will be needed:\\n\\n- Caching web pages and DNS records.\\n\\n- Databases (for example, the InnoDB buffer pool).\\n\\n- Operating systems, to cache memory pages.\\n\\n- Frameworks (for example, in Django for caching).\\n\\nConclusion: An LRU cache is worth using when you need to manage a limited cache efficiently with a simple, predictable eviction policy based on the time of last access. It is especially useful in scenarios where data access has temporal locality (recently used data is often requested again).\",\"from collections import OrderedDict\\n\\nclass LRUCache:\\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\\n        self.capacity = capacity\\n\\n    def get(self, key: int) -> int:\\n        if key not in self.cache:\\n            return -1\\n        # Move the key to the end (as the most recent)\\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\\n        return self.cache[key]\\n\\n    def put(self, key: int, value: int) -> None:\\n        if key in self.cache:\\n            # Update the value and move it\\n            self.cache.move_to_end(key)\\n        self.cache[key] = value\\n        if len(self.cache) > self.capacity:\\n            # Remove the first (oldest) element\\n            self.cache.popitem(last=False)\\n\\n# Usage\\ncache = LRUCache(2)\\ncache.put(1, 1)\\ncache.put(2, 2)\\nprint(cache.get(1))  # 1\\ncache.put(3, 3)      # Evicts the key 2\\nprint(cache.get(2))  # -1 (not found)\"],[1674,\"An object is freed from memory when the garbage collector determines that there are no longer any references to it from the active part of the program. In languages such as Java or C# this happens automatically, but not instantly. The garbage collector periodically scans memory, finds unreachable objects and frees the area they occupy. The programmer usually does not control the exact moment of release.\",\"Freeing an object from memory is a process managed by the garbage collector (GC) in languages with automatic memory management, such as Java, C#, Python, Go or JavaScript. The main idea is that memory is freed not when the programmer explicitly deletes it, but when the system determines that the object is no longer needed by the program.\\n\\nKey concepts\\n\\nAn object is considered reachable if there is a chain of references to it starting from the so-called roots. The roots are usually global variables, local variables in active call stacks and static fields. If no such chain exists, the object becomes unreachable and a candidate for removal.\\n\\nGarbage collection algorithms\\n\\n- Reference counting: Every object keeps a count of the references to it. When the count reaches zero, the memory is freed immediately. Used in CPython (for some objects) and in old versions of PHP. The drawback is that it cannot handle circular references.\\n\\n- Mark and Sweep: The algorithm walks all the root references, \\\"marking\\\" the reachable objects, and then \\\"sweeps\\\" (removes) every unmarked object. Used in JavaScript and Java.\\n\\n- Generational GC: Memory is divided into generations (young and old objects). Young objects are checked more often, since most of them become garbage quickly. That improves efficiency. Used in Java's HotSpot JVM and the .NET CLR.\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nWhen does it happen?\\n\\nThe exact moment memory is freed is not deterministic and depends on the garbage collector's implementation. The GC usually runs:\\n\\n- When free memory in the heap runs out.\\n\\n- When a certain threshold of allocated memory is reached.\\n\\n- In specially allocated pauses (stop-the-world) or concurrently with the program.\\n\\nIn languages without automatic memory management (C, C++) the release happens strictly when `free()` or `delete` is called.\\n\\nConclusion: An object is freed asynchronously and automatically, when the garbage collector discovers that it is unreachable. Understanding this mechanism helps you avoid memory leaks (for example, from circular references) and write more efficient applications, especially ones with large volumes of data or long uptimes.\",\"import sys\\n\\n# Create an object\\nobj = [1, 2, 3]\\nprint(f\\\"The reference count: {sys.getrefcount(obj) - 1}\\\")  # Roughly 1\\n\\n# Create one more reference\\nanother_ref = obj\\nprint(f\\\"The reference count after the new reference: {sys.getrefcount(obj) - 1}\\\")  # 2\\n\\n# Remove the references\\ndel another_ref  # The count decreases by 1\\ndel obj          # The count becomes 0, the object may be removed\\n# The exact moment of removal is determined by Python's garbage collector.\"],[1675,\"A class can refer to itself because this is a fundamental capability of object-oriented programming for creating recursive data structures. An instance of a class is an object in memory, and it can hold a reference to another object even if that object is of the same type. This is necessary to implement structures such as linked lists, trees or graphs, where a node contains a pointer to the next node or to its children. The compiler or interpreter permits it, since the type of the reference is known at compile time while the memory is allocated dynamically at runtime.\",\"In object-oriented programming a class is a template for creating objects. When we define a field or property inside a class, the type of that field can be another class, including the very same class. This is possible because declaring a class defines its structure but does not create an instance. The compiler or interpreter knows what \\\"class A\\\" is and permits it to have a field of type \\\"class A\\\" inside, since that is simply a statement of the data type that will be used later.\\n\\nHow it works technically\\n\\nA reference (or pointer) inside an object is simply an address in memory. When an object is created, its fields are initialised. A field that is a reference to the same class may initially be `null` or point at another, already existing object. The key point: an object does not contain a full copy of another object of the same type inside itself, only a reference to it. That prevents infinite recursion in the object's size.\\n\\nPractical application\\n\\nThis capability is critically important for implementing many fundamental data structures:\\n\\n- Linked lists: Every node contains data and a `next` reference to the following node.\\n\\n- Trees: A tree node may contain references to its children (for example, `left` and `right` in a binary tree).\\n\\n- Graphs: A graph vertex may hold a list of adjacent vertices.\\n\\n- Recursive structures in domain models: For example, an employee in an organisational hierarchy may have a reference to their manager, who is also an employee.\\n\\nA code example (Java)\\n\\nIn this example the `Node` class has a `next` field of type `Node`. That lets you create chains of objects without knowing their number in advance. Without the ability to refer to itself you would have to use less efficient structures, such as fixed-size arrays.\\n\\nConclusion: A class's ability to refer to itself is a powerful mechanism for creating dynamic, recursive data structures that underpin many algorithms and systems. It is worth using when you need to model part-whole relationships, hierarchies, or any links where elements can refer to each other.\",\"// A Node class for a singly linked list\\nclass Node {\\n    int data;          // The payload\\n    Node next;         // A reference to the next node (of the same class!)\\n\\n    Node(int data) {\\n        this.data = data;\\n        this.next = null; // Initially the reference leads nowhere\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\npublic class Main {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        Node first = new Node(10);\\n        Node second = new Node(20);\\n        Node third = new Node(30);\\n\\n        // Link the nodes: first -> second -> third\\n        first.next = second;\\n        second.next = third;\\n        // third.next stays null, marking the end of the list\\n\\n        // Traversing the list\\n        Node current = first;\\n        while (current != null) {\\n            System.out.println(current.data);\\n            current = current.next;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\"],[1676,\"A cache's size can be limited by setting a maximum number of entries or a total amount of memory. To remove old entries, eviction policies are used, such as LRU (Least Recently Used), which removes data that has gone unused for a long time. You can also apply a TTL (Time To Live), automatically removing entries once they expire. These approaches are often implemented in libraries (for example, lru-cache for Node.js) or in built-in structures.\",\"Caching is a technique for storing temporary data to speed up access, but an unbounded cache can grow until memory is exhausted. That is why its size has to be managed by applying eviction policies.\\nThe main size-limiting strategies\\n\\n- A fixed number of entries: the cache holds no more than N elements, and when a new one is added an old one is removed according to the chosen policy.\\n- A memory limit: the amount of memory occupied is tracked (for example, in bytes), and elements are removed when the limit is exceeded.\\n- TTL (Time To Live): every entry is given a lifetime, after which it is considered stale and removed on the next access or by a background cleanup.\\nPopular eviction policies\\n\\n- LRU (Least Recently Used): removes the elements that have gone longest without being accessed. Effective for access patterns with temporal locality.\\n- LFU (Least Frequently Used): removes the least frequently used elements. Suits scenarios where some data is requested constantly.\\n- FIFO (First-In-First-Out): removes elements in the order they were added; simple, but not always optimal.\\nAn example LRU cache implementation in JavaScript\\n\\nIn real applications ready-made libraries are often used, for example `lru-cache` for Node.js, or mechanisms built into frameworks (Redis with eviction policies).\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Web applications: caching API responses, the results of database queries.\\n- Database systems: a buffer pool for frequently read pages.\\n- Operating systems: caching files in memory.\\n- Network proxies: storing copies of web pages to reduce load.\\nConclusion: Limiting a cache's size with an eviction policy (especially LRU) is critically important for the long-term stability of applications working with large volumes of temporary data, since it prevents memory leaks and ensures resources are used efficiently.\",\"class LRUCache {\\n  constructor(capacity) {\\n    this.capacity = capacity;\\n    this.cache = new Map(); // Holds key-value pairs\\n  }\\n\\n  get(key) {\\n    if (!this.cache.has(key)) return -1;\\n    const value = this.cache.get(key);\\n    // Update the order: delete and insert again\\n    this.cache.delete(key);\\n    this.cache.set(key, value);\\n    return value;\\n  }\\n\\n  put(key, value) {\\n    if (this.cache.has(key)) {\\n      this.cache.delete(key);\\n    } else if (this.cache.size >= this.capacity) {\\n      // Remove the oldest element (the first in the Map)\\n      const oldestKey = this.cache.keys().next().value;\\n      this.cache.delete(oldestKey);\\n    }\\n    this.cache.set(key, value);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst cache = new LRUCache(2);\\ncache.put('a', 1);\\ncache.put('b', 2);\\nconsole.log(cache.get('a')); // 1 (updates the order)\\ncache.put('c', 3); // Evicts 'b', since it has become the oldest\\nconsole.log(cache.get('b')); // -1 (not found)\"],[1677,\"To guarantee the order of results when loading asynchronously, you need to perform the operations sequentially rather than in parallel. The simplest way is to use async/await in a `for...of` loop, which waits for each previous operation to finish before starting the next. Alternatively you can build a chain of promises with `.then()`, but that is less readable. The key thing is to avoid Promise.all, which runs the promises in parallel and returns the results in the order they completed rather than the order they were started.\",\"When loading data asynchronously, for example from several API endpoints, the operations run independently and may finish at different times. If the order of the results matters (for example, to display a list in a particular sequence), you have to control the flow of execution.\\nThe problem with parallel execution\\nMethods such as `Promise.all` start all the promises at once and return an array of results in the order the promises settled, which may not match the order they were created. That is unsuitable when a strict sequence is needed.\\nThe solution: sequential execution\\nTo guarantee the order you need to perform the asynchronous operations one after another. Here are the main approaches:\\n\\n- Using async/await with a `for...of` loop: this is the most readable and modern way.\\n- A chain of promises with reduce: lets you accumulate the results sequentially.\\n- Recursion: suits a dynamic list of operations.\\nA code example with async/await\\n\\nThis code guarantees that the second request starts only after the first has completed successfully, preserving the order. The total execution time, however, will be the sum of the times of all the requests, which may be slower than the parallel approach.\\nWhere this is used\\nSequential loading is useful when:\\n\\n- The data depends on itself (for example, loading page by page).\\n- The server has limits on the number of simultaneous requests.\\n- A strict processing order is required, as in a task queue.\\nConclusion: use sequential execution of asynchronous operations through async/await in a loop when the order of the results is critically important and you are prepared to sacrifice speed for predictability.\",\"async function fetchSequentially(urls) {\\n  const results = [];\\n  for (const url of urls) {\\n    const response = await fetch(url); // Wait for each request to finish\\n    const data = await response.json();\\n    results.push(data);\\n  }\\n  return results; // The order matches the order in the urls array\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst urls = ['/api/item/1', '/api/item/2', '/api/item/3'];\\nfetchSequentially(urls).then(orderedResults => {\\n  console.log(orderedResults); // The data in the right order\\n});\"],[1678,\"A class that depends on a singleton is hard to test because of the global state. The main approach is to refactor the code to inject the dependency. Instead of accessing the singleton directly, the dependency should be passed through the constructor or a method. That lets you substitute a mock or stub for the singleton in tests. If refactoring is impossible, you can reset the singleton's state before every test or use the framework's tools to substitute it.\",\"A dependency on a singleton creates a problem for unit testing, because a singleton is global, shared state that can persist between tests, making them non-isolated and unpredictable.\\nThe main approach: refactoring and dependency injection\\nThe best solution is to change the class's design so that it does not depend on the singleton directly. Instead, the dependency (the interface or abstraction the singleton provides) should be injected from outside.\\n\\nIn production you will pass the real singleton (for example, `new OrderProcessor(Logger.Instance)`), and in a test a stub.\\nAlternatives if refactoring is impossible\\n\\n- Resetting the state: Add a `Reset()` method to the singleton to clear its internal state and call it in your test framework's `[SetUp]` or `[TearDown]`.\\n- Substitution through reflection: Use reflection to replace the private static instance field in the singleton with a mock object before the test.\\n- Framework tools: Some frameworks, such as Microsoft Fakes or PowerMock, let you substitute static methods and singletons.\\nConclusion: To create reliable, maintainable tests it is preferable to refactor the code, removing the hidden dependency on the singleton through dependency injection. That makes the code more flexible and testable. Last resorts such as resetting state are worth using only for legacy code that cannot be changed.\",\"// The problematic code\\nclass OrderProcessor {\\n    public void Process(Order order) {\\n        var logger = Logger.Instance; // Direct access to the singleton\\n        logger.Log(\\\"Processing order...\\\");\\n        // ... the logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Refactored: dependency injection\\nclass OrderProcessor {\\n    private readonly ILogger _logger;\\n    \\n    // The dependency is passed through the constructor\\n    public OrderProcessor(ILogger logger) {\\n        _logger = logger;\\n    }\\n    \\n    public void Process(Order order) {\\n        _logger.Log(\\\"Processing order...\\\");\\n        // ... the logic\\n    }\\n}\"],[1679,\"Usually not; a collection does not need to be cleared on every insertion. Collections (for example, arrays, lists) are intended to hold many elements, and clearing them on every addition would negate their main function. It is important, however, to manage memory: if you are replacing the whole collection with new data, the old one is worth clearing to avoid leaks. In languages with automatic garbage collection (Java, C#, Python) the memory is freed automatically once there are no more references to the collection.\",\"Collections (arrays, lists, dictionaries) are data structures intended to store and manage many elements. Their main purpose is to accumulate data, not to be cleared every time a new element is added. If we cleared a collection before every insertion, it would never contain more than one element, which contradicts the very idea of a collection.\\n\\nWhen might clearing be needed?\\n\\nClearing a collection (for example, with a `clear()` method) is usually required in the following scenarios:\\n\\n- When you need to replace a collection's contents entirely with new data (for example, refreshing a list of products in an interface).\\n\\n- To prevent memory leaks, if the collection holds large objects and is no longer needed.\\n\\n- In loops or long-lived processes where the collection is reused, to avoid accumulating old data.\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nMemory management\\n\\nIn languages with automatic garbage collection (Java, C#, Python, JavaScript) memory is freed when no references to the collection object remain. If the collection is part of a long-lived object (for example, a cache), however, it is important to clear it explicitly so as not to accumulate unnecessary data. In languages without garbage collection (C, C++) the responsibility for cleanup lies with the developer — the elements have to be removed manually to avoid leaks.\\n\\nConclusion: There is no need to clear a collection on every insertion — that would break its logic. Clearing is used when the contents are being replaced entirely, or to free memory when the old data is no longer required. Managing collections properly improves an application's performance and stability.\",\"# Bad: clearing on every insertion\\nitems = []\\nfor i in range(5):\\n    items.clear()  # Pointless - the collection is always empty\\n    items.append(i)\\n    print(items)  # Output: [0], [1], [2]...\\n\\n# Good: accumulating the elements\\nitems = []\\nfor i in range(5):\\n    items.append(i)\\nprint(items)  # Output: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\n# Clearing when the data is replaced entirely\\ndata = fetch_old_data()  # The old data\\n# ... working with data\\ndata.clear()  # Clear it if it is no longer needed\\nnew_data = fetch_new_data()\\ndata.extend(new_data)  # Fill it again\"],[1680,\"DispatchGroup lets you track the completion of a group of asynchronous tasks. Create a group with DispatchGroup(), call group.enter() before each download and group.leave() after it finishes (whether successfully or with an error). Use group.notify(queue:) to run code after all the images have loaded. That guarantees you are notified when every task has finished, even if they run on different queues.\",\"In iOS development there is often a need to load several images from different URLs and process them all together once every download has finished. DispatchGroup is a GCD (Grand Central Dispatch) mechanism that lets you synchronise the execution of a group of tasks.\\n\\nHow DispatchGroup works\\n\\nDispatchGroup tracks the number of \\\"entries\\\" and \\\"exits\\\". Every asynchronous task must tell the group when it starts (by calling `enter()`) and when it finishes (by calling `leave()`). When the number of `leave()` calls equals the number of `enter()` calls, the group is considered complete.\\n\\nA practical implementation\\n\\nHere is an example function that loads an array of images:\\n\\nKey points of the implementation\\n\\n- Use `defer { group.leave() }` inside the task's closure, to guarantee that the group is notified of completion even if an error occurs or there is an early return.\\n\\n- Store the results in an array using the index from `enumerated()`, to keep the order of the images matching the URLs.\\n\\n- The `group.notify(queue: .main)` method sets a handler that will be called on the main queue once every task has finished — convenient for updating the UI.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nThis approach is useful in scenarios where several resources have to be loaded before a screen is displayed (for example, an image gallery, the avatars of chat participants, product thumbnails). It can also be extended to limit the number of simultaneous downloads using semaphores (DispatchSemaphore).\\n\\nConclusion: Use DispatchGroup when you need to synchronise several independent asynchronous operations and perform an action once they have all completed, especially in a UI context where the interface has to be updated after all the data has loaded.\",\"func loadImages(from urls: [URL], completion: @escaping ([UIImage?]) -> Void) {\\n    let group = DispatchGroup()\\n    var images: [UIImage?] = Array(repeating: nil, count: urls.count)\\n    \\n    for (index, url) in urls.enumerated() {\\n        group.enter()\\n        URLSession.shared.dataTask(with: url) { data, _, error in\\n            defer { group.leave() } // Guarantees leave() is called even on an error\\n            \\n            if let data = data, error == nil {\\n                images[index] = UIImage(data: data)\\n            }\\n        }.resume()\\n    }\\n    \\n    group.notify(queue: .main) {\\n        completion(images)\\n    }\\n}\"],[1681,\"Weak references let you hold objects in a collection without preventing them from being garbage-collected. This is useful for caches, where the data may be removed when memory runs short, or for holding metadata about DOM elements that may be removed from the page. Weak references therefore help avoid memory leaks by automatically clearing references to objects that are no longer used anywhere.\",\"Weak references (WeakRef) are a special type of reference that does not keep an object alive for the garbage collector. If only weak references to an object remain, the garbage collector may remove that object, and the weak references automatically become invalid (undefined or null, depending on the implementation).\\n\\nThe main uses\\n\\nWeak references are most often used in two scenarios:\\n\\n- Caching: you can store the results of heavy computations or data fetched from the network, but let the garbage collector clear the cache if the memory is needed for other tasks. That prevents uncontrolled growth in memory consumption.\\n\\n- Holding metadata: for example, associating additional information with DOM elements or other objects whose lifetime is managed by external code. If an element is removed from the DOM, the weak reference to it is automatically \\\"cleared\\\", and the metadata can be removed too.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nIn JavaScript, WeakRef and the WeakMap/WeakSet collections are used to work with weak references. Consider an example of a cache using WeakRef:\\n\\nImportant nuances\\n\\nGarbage collection in JavaScript is non-deterministic, so the moment weak references are cleared is unpredictable. You should not rely on weak references for critical resource-release logic (explicit methods such as close() or dispose() are better for that). It is also worth remembering that WeakMap and WeakSet accept only objects as keys (WeakMap) or values (WeakSet) and automatically remove entries when there are no strong references to the key object left.\\n\\nConclusion: Weak references are worth using when you need to associate data temporarily with objects whose lifetime you do not control, or to implement caches that should free memory automatically under pressure from the garbage collector, avoiding leaks.\",\"// Create a cache that does not get in the way of garbage collection\\nconst cache = new Map(); // The keys are ordinary strings, the values WeakRefs to objects\\n\\nfunction getCachedData(key) {\\n    const ref = cache.get(key);\\n    if (ref) {\\n        const obj = ref.deref(); // Try to obtain the object\\n        if (obj !== undefined) {\\n            console.log('Cache hit!');\\n            return obj;\\n        } else {\\n            // The object was collected by the garbage collector\\n            cache.delete(key);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    // Compute the data again\\n    const newData = expensiveCalculation(key);\\n    cache.set(key, new WeakRef(newData));\\n    return newData;\\n}\\n\\nfunction expensiveCalculation(key) {\\n    // Heavy computations...\\n    return { result: key.toUpperCase() };\\n}\"],[1682,\"To preserve the order you need to process the data sequentially, or use Promise.all while saving the indices in advance. The simplest way is to create an array of promises and wait for them with Promise.all, but then the order of the results may not match the order of the input data. To match them, you can pass the index along with the data and, once all the promises have settled, sort the results by that index. Alternatively you can use async/await in a loop for sequential processing, which guarantees the order but is slower.\",\"When working with asynchronous operations such as network requests or reading files, the results often come back in an arbitrary order because they take different amounts of time. In many scenarios, however — for example, processing a list of items where the original order matters (such as displaying data in a UI in the same order it was requested) — the results have to be matched to the original sequence.\\nThe main approaches\\nThere are two main approaches: parallel execution followed by sorting, and sequential execution.\\n\\n- Parallel execution while preserving the index: The fastest, since the operations run simultaneously. You need to attach an index to every promise and sort the results once they have all settled.\\n- Sequential execution: Guarantees the order but is slower, since each subsequent operation waits for the previous one to finish. Suitable when the order is critically important and the number of operations is small.\\nCode example: the parallel approach\\nHere is how you can use Promise.all while preserving the original order:\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis pattern is often used in front-end development when loading data for lists, and on the back end when processing database or external-API requests in parallel where the order of the responses has to be preserved. It is also useful in data-stream processing tasks, for example when working with files or messages from a queue.\\nConclusion: Use the parallel approach with indexing and sorting when both performance and order matter. Choose the sequential approach if the operations have to run strictly one after another because of dependencies or resource limits.\",\"async function processInOrder(inputs) {\\n  // Create an array of promises, each carrying an index and the data\\n  const promises = inputs.map((input, index) => \\n    asyncOperation(input).then(result => ({ index, result }))\\n  );\\n\\n  // Wait for all the promises to settle\\n  const results = await Promise.all(promises);\\n\\n  // Sort the results by the original index\\n  results.sort((a, b) => a.index - b.index);\\n\\n  // Extract just the results, in the right order\\n  return results.map(item => item.result);\\n}\\n\\n// An example asynchronous function\\nasync function asyncOperation(data) {\\n  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, Math.random() * 1000));\\n  return `processed_${data}`;\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\n(async () => {\\n  const inputs = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\\n  const orderedResults = await processInOrder(inputs);\\n  console.log(orderedResults); // ['processed_a', 'processed_b', 'processed_c']\\n})();\"],[1683,\"A hard dependency on a concrete implementation, such as a Singleton, can be removed by applying the dependency inversion principle (DIP) and dependency injection (DI). Instead of the class creating the dependency's instance itself, it should receive it from outside through its constructor, a method or a property. That lets you substitute a mock for the real implementation in testing, or another implementation depending on the context. The code thus becomes less coupled and more flexible.\",\"A hard dependency on a concrete class, especially on a Singleton, creates problems with testing and with the system's flexibility, since the code becomes tightly coupled to one implementation. The solution is to apply the dependency inversion principle (DIP), which states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\\n\\nThe main approach: dependency injection\\n\\nInstead of a class creating a dependency inside itself, it should receive it from outside. That can be done through the constructor (the most preferable way), through a setter or through an interface. Consider an example in TypeScript.\\n\\nWhere and how this applies\\n\\nThis approach is widely used in modern frameworks (Angular, NestJS, Spring). Dependency-injection containers (IoC containers) manage the creation and wiring of objects automatically. In the example given, `OrderService` now depends on the `ILogger` abstraction rather than on the concrete `Logger`. That lets you:\\n\\n- Test the class easily by substituting mock objects for its dependencies.\\n\\n- Change the implementation (for example, replace the console logger with a file-based one or with Sentry) without changing `OrderService`'s code.\\n\\n- Observe the single-responsibility principle (SRP) — the class is not in the business of creating its own dependencies.\\n\\nConclusion: Apply dependency injection through abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes) wherever the implementation may change or isolated testing is required. This matters especially for services that work with external systems (databases, APIs, logging).\",\"// BAD: a hard dependency on a Singleton\\nclass OrderService {\\n    private logger = Logger.getInstance(); // Direct use of the Singleton\\n\\n    processOrder() {\\n        this.logger.log('Processing order...');\\n        // ... the logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: the dependency is injected through the constructor\\ninterface ILogger {\\n    log(message: string): void;\\n}\\n\\nclass ConsoleLogger implements ILogger {\\n    log(message: string) {\\n        console.log(`[LOG]: ${message}`);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass OrderService {\\n    constructor(private logger: ILogger) {} // The dependency is supplied from outside\\n\\n    processOrder() {\\n        this.logger.log('Processing order...');\\n        // ... the logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage\\nconst logger = new ConsoleLogger();\\nconst orderService = new OrderService(logger);\\norderService.processOrder();\\n\\n// For testing it is easy to substitute the implementation\\nclass MockLogger implements ILogger {\\n    log(message: string) {\\n        // Do nothing, or record into an array for checking\\n    }\\n}\\nconst testService = new OrderService(new MockLogger());\"],[1684,\"Weak references let objects refer to each other without creating strong cycles that could lead to memory leaks. They do, however, impose constraints: a weak reference can become nil (or None) when the target object is destroyed by the garbage collector. That means weak references cannot be used for data types that do not support optionality or are not objects (for example, primitive types such as int, or structs). In languages like Swift, weak references apply only to class instances, not to structs or enums.\",\"Weak references are a mechanism that lets one object refer to another without increasing the target object's reference count. This is critically important for preventing strong reference cycles, which can lead to memory leaks, since the garbage collector cannot free the objects even when the program no longer uses them.\\n\\nConstraints on data types\\n\\nUsing weak references imposes several key constraints related to data types:\\n\\n- Object types only: In most languages (for example, Swift, Python, Java) weak references apply only to objects (class instances), not to primitive types (int, float) or value types (structs in Swift, value types). That is because weak references require the garbage collector to track the object's state, which is possible only for objects allocated on the heap.\\n\\n- Optionality: Since a weak reference may become invalid (when the target object is destroyed), access to it must be safe. In Swift weak references automatically become nil, so they have to be declared as optional types. In Python, weak references through the weakref module return None when a dead object is accessed.\\n\\n- Immutability and hashability: In some contexts (for example, weak dictionaries in Python) the objects that weak references are created to must be hashable, which can limit their use for certain data types.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nConsider an example in Swift, where weak references are used to break strong reference cycles between classes:\\n\\nIn this example the weak `apartment` reference lets you avoid a strong reference cycle. Note that `apartment` is declared as `weak var`, which means it has to be optional (`Apartment?`) and applies only to a class.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nWeak references are a powerful tool for managing memory, but they are worth using only in cases where there is a risk of creating strong reference cycles between objects (for example, in parent-child or delegate relationships). They are unsuitable for primitive types or structs, and always require the optional state to be handled, which adds complexity to the code.\",\"class Person {\\n    let name: String\\n    weak var apartment: Apartment? // A weak reference\\n    init(name: String) { self.name = name }\\n}\\n\\nclass Apartment {\\n    let unit: String\\n    var tenant: Person?\\n    init(unit: String) { self.unit = unit }\\n}\\n\\nvar john: Person? = Person(name: \\\"John\\\")\\nvar unit4A: Apartment? = Apartment(unit: \\\"4A\\\")\\njohn?.apartment = unit4A\\nunit4A?.tenant = john\\n\\n// When john and unit4A are set to nil, the memory is freed correctly\\njohn = nil\\nunit4A = nil // The weak reference in Person does not retain the Apartment\"],[1685,\"The algorithmic complexity of clearing nil elements from a collection depends on the data structure and the algorithm used. For an array (Array/List) the naive approach of removing each nil in place has a complexity of O(n²), since every removal requires shifting all the subsequent elements. The optimal approach is to build a new array without the nil elements, in O(n) time and O(n) additional memory. For a linked list, removing the nil elements can be done in O(n) with no additional memory, by changing the references between the nodes.\",\"Clearing nil elements (or null values) from a collection is a filtering operation that removes every element equal to nil. Its algorithmic (time) complexity is determined by how the collection is organised and which algorithm is used for the removal.\\n\\nData structures and their effect on the complexity\\n\\nConsider two widespread structures:\\n\\n- An array (Array, List, Slice): the elements are stored in a contiguous region of memory. Removing an element from the middle directly requires shifting all the subsequent elements to fill the \\\"hole\\\" that appears. If you remove each nil as you traverse, that leads to a quadratic complexity of O(n²), because for each of the k nil elements (where k ≤ n) we shift O(n) elements on average.\\n\\n- A linked list: every element holds a reference to the next one (and possibly the previous). Removing an element requires only updating the references of the neighbouring nodes, which is done in O(1) if we have a pointer to the node being removed. A full traversal of the list removing nils therefore runs in O(n).\\n\\nOptimisation for arrays\\n\\nFor arrays you can apply a two-pointer (or two-index) algorithm that works in O(n) and in place, if modifying the original array is acceptable. One pointer (write) marks the position where the next non-nil element will be written, the other (read) traverses every element. At the end the array's length is truncated to the write position.\\n\\nThis approach uses O(1) additional memory (not counting the memory for the array itself) and runs in O(n).\\n\\nAn example in Python\\n\\nIn Python, lists are dynamic arrays. The simple way is to use a list comprehension, which creates a new list:\\n\\nThis method has a time complexity of O(n) and consumes O(n) additional memory for the new list. To work in place you can use the two-pointer approach, but in Python that is less idiomatic.\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\nClearing nils is often needed when processing data obtained from external sources (for example, from an API or files), where some fields may be missing or empty. It is also useful when preparing data for subsequent operations that do not tolerate nil (for example, mathematical computations, building charts).\\n\\nConclusion: Clearing nil elements from a collection can generally be done in linear time, O(n). For arrays it is important to avoid naive removal in a loop, so as not to end up with O(n²). Use the two-pointer algorithm to work in place, or build a new filtered array if the additional memory consumption is acceptable.\",\"// Pseudocode for the array arr\\nwrite = 0\\nfor read = 0 to arr.length - 1:\\n    if arr[read] != nil:\\n        arr[write] = arr[read]\\n        write++\\narr.resize(write) // truncate the array\\n\\noriginal = [1, None, 2, None, 3]\\nfiltered = [x for x in original if x is not None]\\n# filtered = [1, 2, 3]\"],[1686,\"Updating the UI from outside the main thread leads to an unstable interface and, most likely, to the application crashing. On Android it raises a CalledFromWrongThreadException, and on iOS it causes undefined behaviour or a crash, since UIKit is not thread-safe. The main thread (or main queue) is responsible for handling events and drawing, and any interference from another thread corrupts the internal state of the UI components. For safe updates you need to use mechanisms such as runOnUiThread on Android or DispatchQueue.main.async on iOS.\",\"The user interface in modern operating systems and frameworks, such as Android and iOS, is designed as a single-threaded model. That means every operation involved in creating, changing and drawing visual elements must be performed on a specially designated thread, called the main thread or the UI thread.\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\n\\nUI components (buttons, text fields, lists) are not thread-safe. Their internal state (for example, text, colour, coordinates) may be changed from several threads at once, which leads to a race condition. That can cause visual artefacts (for example, flickering, incorrect rendering), interface freezes or an immediate crash.\\n\\nWhat happens on different platforms?\\n\\n- Android: The system actively checks which thread a View update is called from. If you try to change a TextView from outside the main thread, an `android.view.ViewRootImpl$CalledFromWrongThreadException` is thrown with the message \\\"Only the original thread that created a view hierarchy can touch its views.\\\"\\n\\n- iOS (UIKit): UIKit is not thread-safe. Updating the UI from a background queue leads to undefined behaviour — the interface may update partially, freeze, or the application may crash with an error that is hard to debug.\\n\\n- Desktop (for example, Java Swing): Likewise, Swing has an Event Dispatch Thread (EDT), and updating from outside it leads to the same race-condition problems.\\n\\nHow do you update the UI from background tasks properly?\\n\\nAfter a long operation (network, computation) runs on a background thread, the result has to be passed to the main thread to update the interface. Here are examples for two platforms:\\n\\nAndroid (Kotlin)\\n\\niOS (Swift)\\n\\nThese mechanisms guarantee that the UI-updating code is placed in the main thread's task queue and executed safely.\\n\\nConclusion\\n\\nUpdating the UI from outside the main thread is a critical error that leads to an unstable application. Always use the APIs the platform provides (runOnUiThread, DispatchQueue.main, Handler and so on) to pass data from background threads to the UI thread. This is a fundamental rule for building responsive, reliable mobile and desktop applications.\",\"// A background thread (for example, a coroutine or a Thread)\\nthread {\\n    val result = fetchDataFromNetwork() // A long operation\\n    // Update the UI on the main thread\\n    runOnUiThread {\\n        textView.text = result\\n    }\\n}\\n// Or with coroutines and Dispatchers.Main\\nlifecycleScope.launch(Dispatchers.IO) {\\n    val result = fetchData()\\n    withContext(Dispatchers.Main) {\\n        updateUI(result)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A background queue\\nDispatchQueue.global(qos: .background).async {\\n    let result = fetchData() // A long operation\\n    // Return to the main queue to update the UI\\n    DispatchQueue.main.async {\\n        self.label.text = result\\n    }\\n}\"],[1687,\"UI updates must happen on the main thread because most UI frameworks, such as UIKit, the Android UI or Windows Forms, are not thread-safe. That means accessing interface elements from several threads at once can lead to undefined behaviour, failures or visual artefacts. The main thread processes the event queue (for example, clicks, drawing), ensuring that changes are sequential and predictable. If you update the UI from a background thread, the application may crash or freeze. Background tasks should therefore compute data and then pass the result to the main thread for display.\",\"The user interface in modern operating systems and frameworks is built around a model where one special thread, often called the main thread, the UI thread or the Event Dispatch Thread (EDT), is responsible for all drawing and for handling user input. This architectural decision arose from the need to guarantee the interface's responsiveness and stability.\\n\\nWhy is the UI not thread-safe?\\n\\nUI components (buttons, text fields, drawing canvases) often hold internal state that may change asynchronously. If two threads tried to change a button's colour or recompute its geometry at the same time, the internal data structures could end up in an inconsistent state, which would lead to the application crashing or to visual distortions (artefacts). To avoid complex and expensive synchronisation mechanisms (for example, a lock for every widget), framework developers simply forbid access to the UI from other threads, making the API thread-unsafe by design.\\n\\nHow does it work in practice?\\n\\nThe main thread runs in an event loop. It constantly checks the message queue: mouse clicks, key presses, timers, requests to redraw. All these events are handled sequentially. When a background thread (for example, a data loader) finishes its work, it must not update a TextView or a UITableView directly. Instead it should post a task (a message) into the main thread's queue.\\n\\nConsider an example on Android with Kotlin:\\n\\nThe analogous approach in iOS/Swift:\\n\\nExceptions and modern approaches\\n\\nSome frameworks permit certain \\\"safe\\\" operations from other threads, but that is more the exception. Modern tools, such as reactive libraries (RxJava, Combine) or mechanisms like LiveData on Android, automatically ensure a switch to the main thread on subscription, hiding this complexity from the developer.\\n\\nConclusion: Updating the UI on the main thread is a fundamental rule that ensures an application is stable and responsive. It should always be followed when working with any UI framework that requires it. Breaking this rule is a frequent cause of elusive bugs in multithreaded applications.\",\"// A background thread (for example, a coroutine or a Thread)\\nfun loadUserData() {\\n    val data = fetchFromNetwork() // A long operation\\n    // Wrong: updating the UI directly from the background\\n    // textView.text = data.name // May cause a crash\\n\\n    // Right: using runOnUiThread\\n    runOnUiThread {\\n        textView.text = data.name // The update on the main thread\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A background thread\\nDispatchQueue.global(qos: .background).async {\\n    let data = fetchData()\\n    // Update the UI on the main thread\\n    DispatchQueue.main.async {\\n        self.label.text = data\\n    }\\n}\"],[1688,\"An object's life cycle determines when it is created, used and destroyed by the garbage collector. If the object that initiated a network request is destroyed before the request finishes, that can lead to memory leaks or errors, since the callback may try to access an object that no longer exists. In languages with manual memory management the requests must be cancelled explicitly. In JavaScript, with its automatic garbage collector, it is important to avoid keeping references to unneeded objects in callbacks, so that they can be removed correctly.\",\"An object's life cycle is the period from its creation in memory to its removal by the garbage collector (or explicit release). Network requests are asynchronous operations that run independently of the main thread. The connection between them is that the object initiating a request often passes a callback or a promise to handle the response. If that object is destroyed before the request finishes, the response handler may try to update the state or properties of an object that no longer exists, which leads to errors (for example, accessing null) or to memory leaks if the handler retains a reference to the object, preventing it from being collected.\\n\\nProblems and examples\\n\\nConsider a typical scenario in a web application. A UI component (for example, a React component) sends a request on mount, but the user quickly navigates to another page and the component unmounts. If the request finishes after the unmount and tries to update the component's state, an error occurs.\\n\\nHow to manage the life cycle\\n\\nTo avoid problems you have to manage the cancellation of requests explicitly when the object is destroyed:\\n\\n- In modern browsers use `AbortController` to cancel fetch requests.\\n\\n- In libraries (for example, Axios) use cancellation tokens.\\n\\n- In React applications clear subscriptions in `componentWillUnmount` or use hooks with effects.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nThis approach is critical in single-page applications (SPAs), mobile applications and any system with a dynamic interface, where components are frequently created and removed. It also matters in Node.js server applications when working with long HTTP requests or WebSocket connections, in order to release resources.\\n\\nConclusion: Explicitly managing an object's life cycle with respect to network requests is essential to prevent memory leaks and runtime errors. Always cancel or clean up pending requests when an object is destroyed, especially in UI frameworks and asynchronous environments.\",\"// An example in JavaScript with React and fetch\\nclass UserProfile extends React.Component {\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    fetch('/api/user')\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => {\\n        // The danger: if the component has already unmounted,\\n        // this.setState will raise an error.\\n        this.setState({ user: data });\\n      });\\n  }\\n\\n  render() { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n\\n// An example with AbortController and a React hook\\nfunction UserProfile() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const controller = new AbortController();\\n    const signal = controller.signal;\\n\\n    fetch('/api/user', { signal })\\n      .then(response => response.json())\\n      .then(data => setUser(data))\\n      .catch(err => {\\n        if (err.name === 'AbortError') {\\n          console.log('The request was cancelled');\\n        }\\n      });\\n\\n    // The cleanup function: cancels the request on unmount\\n    return () => controller.abort();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return /* JSX */;\\n}\"],[1689,\"A singleton creates global state that persists between tests, breaking their isolation. That leads to non-deterministic results, where one test affects another. A dependency on the concrete Singleton class makes it harder to substitute a mock or stub for the real implementation. To solve the problem it is recommended to use dependency injection, passing the instance as a parameter rather than obtaining it globally.\",\"The Singleton pattern guarantees that only one instance of a class exists for the whole life cycle of an application. Although that is useful for managing shared resources, it creates hidden dependencies and global state, which conflicts with the principles of unit testing.\\nThe main problems in testing\\n\\n- Broken test isolation: A Singleton's state persists between test runs. If one test changes that state, the next test receives unexpected data, which leads to fragile, non-deterministic tests.\\n- Difficulty substituting dependencies (mocking): Code that accesses Singleton.getInstance() directly is rigidly tied to the concrete class. In a test it is impossible to replace the real logic with a stub or mock object easily without modifying the Singleton class itself.\\n- Hidden dependencies: A Singleton hides a class's dependencies, making them implicit. That makes it harder to understand what a module needs in order to work and how to test it properly.\\nA code example illustrating the problem\\nConsider a simple Singleton managing settings, and a class that uses it.\\n\\nTesting `UserService` in isolation is impossible, since it depends on `ConfigManager`'s global state. If another test set the 'adminEmail' value, our test may fail.\\nThe solution: dependency injection\\nInstead of accessing the Singleton directly, the dependency should be passed through the constructor or a method.\\n\\nThis approach makes the dependencies explicit and lets you use stubs in tests easily.\\nConclusion: Singletons are worth using with caution, mainly for genuinely global, stateless utilities (for example, a logger). For objects with state or behaviour that may need to be substituted in tests, dependency injection is preferable, which makes the code more flexible and testable.\",\"// The problematic Singleton\\nclass ConfigManager {\\n    private static instance: ConfigManager;\\n    private settings: Map<string, string> = new Map();\\n\\n    private constructor() {}\\n\\n    static getInstance(): ConfigManager {\\n        if (!ConfigManager.instance) {\\n            ConfigManager.instance = new ConfigManager();\\n        }\\n        return ConfigManager.instance;\\n    }\\n\\n    setSetting(key: string, value: string) {\\n        this.settings.set(key, value);\\n    }\\n\\n    getSetting(key: string): string | undefined {\\n        return this.settings.get(key);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A class that depends on the Singleton\\nclass UserService {\\n    getAdminEmail(): string {\\n        // A direct, hard dependency\\n        const email = ConfigManager.getInstance().getSetting('adminEmail');\\n        return email || 'default@example.com';\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// An interface for the abstraction\\ninterface IConfigManager {\\n    getSetting(key: string): string | undefined;\\n}\\n\\n// The implementation may be a Singleton internally, but that is a detail\\nclass RealConfigManager implements IConfigManager {\\n    // ... implemented as a Singleton, but that is hidden\\n}\\n\\n// A class ready for testing\\nclass UserService {\\n    constructor(private configManager: IConfigManager) {}\\n\\n    getAdminEmail(): string {\\n        const email = this.configManager.getSetting('adminEmail');\\n        return email || 'default@example.com';\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// In production code\\nconst service = new UserService(RealConfigManager.getInstance());\\n\\n// In a test it is easy to substitute the implementation\\nconst mockConfigManager = {\\n    getSetting: (key: string) => 'test@example.com'\\n};\\nconst testService = new UserService(mockConfigManager);\\n// The test is now isolated and predictable\"],[1690,\"Managing the network layer's dependencies means designing it so that it is not rigidly tied to a concrete implementation. The main approach is to use abstractions (interfaces) for the network clients and dependency injection. That lets you substitute mocks for real HTTP requests in tests easily, change libraries (for example, axios for fetch) without rewriting the business logic, and manage the configuration (base URLs, headers) centrally. Such a layer becomes an independent module.\",\"Managing the network layer's dependencies properly is a key aspect of a modern application's architecture. It separates the code that performs HTTP requests from the rest of the business logic, making the system more modular, testable and adaptable to change.\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- Abstraction: Define an interface (or an abstract class) for the network client. The business logic should depend on that interface, not on a concrete axios or fetch class.\\n- Dependency injection (DI): The concrete implementation of the network client is \\\"injected\\\" from outside, usually through the constructor or method parameters.\\n- Single responsibility: The network layer should deal only with communication: building requests, handling responses and errors. Parsing data and business rules are other modules' responsibility.\\nA practical implementation\\nConsider an example in TypeScript. First we define the abstraction, then the implementation, and show how to use it.\\n\\nAdvantages and uses\\nThis approach is used for:\\n\\n- Unit testing: You can pass a mock `HttpClient` into `UserService` that returns fake data, without making real network requests.\\n- Easily replacing libraries: To move from fetch to axios, you create a new `AxiosHttpClient` class implementing the same interface and change one line where the dependencies are created.\\n- Centralised management: The configuration (baseURL, authentication headers, interceptors) is encapsulated in one place — in the `HttpClient` implementation.\\nConclusion: Managing dependencies through abstractions and DI is worth applying in any project where network requests are a critical part. It is essential for medium and large applications, since it significantly simplifies testing, maintenance and the evolution of the code when external APIs or network libraries change.\",\"// 1. The abstraction (interface) for the network client\\ninterface HttpClient {\\n  get<T>(url: string): Promise<T>;\\n  post<T>(url: string, data: unknown): Promise<T>;\\n  // ... other methods\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A concrete implementation using fetch (or axios)\\nclass FetchHttpClient implements HttpClient {\\n  private baseURL: string;\\n\\n  constructor(baseURL: string) {\\n    this.baseURL = baseURL;\\n  }\\n\\n  async get<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {\\n    const response = await fetch(`${this.baseURL}${url}`);\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);\\n    }\\n    return response.json() as Promise<T>;\\n  }\\n  // ... the implementation of post and the other methods\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The business-logic service, which depends on the abstraction\\nclass UserService {\\n  constructor(private httpClient: HttpClient) {}\\n\\n  async getUsers() {\\n    // The business logic uses only the interface\\n    return this.httpClient.get<User[]>('/users');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 4. Composing (\\\"wiring\\\") the dependencies\\nconst httpClient = new FetchHttpClient('https://api.example.com');\\nconst userService = new UserService(httpClient);\\n\\n// 5. Use\\n// const users = await userService.getUsers();\"],[1691,\"When several threads write to an array at the same time, race-condition problems arise. That means the array's final state becomes unpredictable, since the order of the write operations is not controlled. Lost updates can occur, where one write overwrites another. Data corruption is also possible if the write operations are not atomic. This leads to errors that are hard to reproduce and debug.\",\"When several threads or asynchronous operations try to change the same array at once, a classic parallel-programming problem arises. The main difficulty is that memory read and write operations are usually not atomic in most high-level programming languages. That means the process of changing an array element can be interrupted by another thread, which leads to an inconsistent state of the data.\\n\\nThe key problems\\n\\n- Race condition: The outcome depends on the unpredictable order in which the threads run. For example, two threads read the same value, both increment it and write it back. Instead of two increments there will be only one.\\n\\n- Lost updates: One of the changes is completely overwritten by another and is \\\"lost\\\".\\n\\n- Data corruption: With operations that change the array's size (push/pop), internal pointers can become invalid, which may lead to the program crashing or to undefined behaviour.\\n\\n- Inconsistent state: Other threads may see the array in an intermediate, \\\"broken\\\" state.\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript (Node.js with worker threads)\\n\\nThe following code demonstrates the race-condition problem when incrementing an array's elements.\\n\\nIn this example 5 workers try to increase every element of the array by 1. The ideal result is for every element to equal 5. Because of the race condition, however, the actual values will be random, often less than 5, because the read-modify-write operations overlap.\\n\\nHow do you solve these problems?\\n\\n- Synchronisation: Using mutexes, semaphores or locks to provide exclusive access to the array for the duration of an operation.\\n\\n- Atomic operations: Using special atomic instructions (for example, Atomics in JavaScript) for compare-and-swap style operations.\\n\\n- Immutable data structures: Instead of changing the existing array, creating a new copy with the changes. This is natural in functional programming.\\n\\n- Data isolation: Designing the architecture so that each thread works with its own copy of the data, and the results are then carefully merged.\\n\\nConclusion: Concurrent-write problems are critical in multithreaded and distributed systems. To solve them you have to apply synchronisation mechanisms or redesign access to the data so as to avoid shared mutable state. In web development this is often encountered when working with a shared in-memory cache or when processing data with workers.\",\"const { Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData } = require('worker_threads');\\n\\nif (isMainThread) {\\n    const sharedArray = new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4 * 10)); // An array of 10 elements\\n    const numWorkers = 5;\\n    let finishedWorkers = 0;\\n\\n    for (let i = 0; i < numWorkers; i++) {\\n        new Worker(__filename, { workerData: { sharedArray } });\\n    }\\n    // Wait and print the result - it will be unpredictable\\n    setTimeout(() => console.log(Array.from(sharedArray)), 1000);\\n} else {\\n    const arr = workerData.sharedArray;\\n    // Every worker tries to increase each element by 1\\n    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n        // This is NOT an atomic operation! A race condition is guaranteed.\\n        arr[i] = arr[i] + 1;\\n    }\\n    parentPort.postMessage('done');\\n}\"],[1692,\"When testing an analytics-sending method you need to check successful sending under normal conditions, the handling of network errors and timeouts, the correctness of the data produced, behaviour in offline mode and the subsequent sending, the impact on the application's performance, and the security and confidentiality of the data transmitted. It is important to make sure analytics are not lost when something fails and do not disrupt the user experience.\",\"Testing an analytics-sending method is a critically important task for ensuring the quality of the data on which business decisions are based. The main goal is to guarantee that events are recorded and delivered reliably, even when the application is working in less-than-ideal conditions.\\n\\nThe key cases to check\\n\\n- Successful sending: Checking that an event is built correctly and sent to the server over a stable connection, and that the server returns a successful status (for example, 200 OK).\\n\\n- Handling network errors: Simulating loss of network, the internet being disconnected, the server being unavailable (5xx errors). The method should either retry sending with exponential backoff or save the event to be sent later.\\n\\n- Timeouts: Checking the behaviour when the server responds slowly or the waiting time is exceeded. Sending must not \\\"hang\\\" and block the application's main thread.\\n\\n- Correctness of the data: Validating the structure and contents of the JSON object sent. Checking the mandatory fields (event_name, user_id, timestamp), the data types and the formats.\\n\\n- Offline mode and the event queue: Checking that events accumulate locally (for example, in IndexedDB or localStorage) when there is no connection and are sent in a batch when connectivity is restored.\\n\\n- Performance: Assessing the impact of sending analytics on UI response time, memory and battery consumption, especially with frequent events (for example, scrolling, typing).\\n\\n- Security and confidentiality: Checking that no sensitive data (passwords, tokens, PII) ends up in the analytics, that HTTPS is used, and that privacy settings (for example, Do Not Track) are respected.\\n\\n- Boundary conditions and load: Sending very large events, calling the method very frequently, working at a low battery level or in a mobile application's background mode.\\n\\nAn example of test code\\n\\nConclusion: Comprehensive testing of an analytics-sending method is essential to prevent the loss of valuable data about user behaviour and to ensure the application works stably in real, often imperfect, network conditions. Particular attention is worth paying to fault tolerance and security.\",\"// An example unit test (Jest) checking the retry behaviour\\nimport { sendAnalyticsEvent } from './analytics';\\n\\ndescribe('sendAnalyticsEvent', () => {\\n  beforeEach(() => {\\n    global.fetch = jest.fn();\\n  });\\n\\n  it('should retry on network failure', async () => {\\n    // Simulate a first failed attempt, then success\\n    global.fetch\\n      .mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error('Network error'))\\n      .mockResolvedValueOnce({ ok: true });\\n\\n    const event = { name: 'button_click', page: 'home' };\\n    await sendAnalyticsEvent(event);\\n\\n    // Check that fetch was called twice\\n    expect(global.fetch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(2);\\n    // Check that both calls carried the same data\\n    expect(global.fetch.mock.calls[0][1].body).toEqual(global.fetch.mock.calls[1][1].body);\\n  });\\n\\n  it('should not send sensitive data', () => {\\n    const eventWithSensitiveData = {\\n      name: 'login',\\n      email: 'user@example.com',\\n      password: 'secret123' // This field must be filtered out!\\n    };\\n    // We assume sendAnalyticsEvent filters the data itself\\n    sendAnalyticsEvent(eventWithSensitiveData);\\n    const fetchCall = global.fetch.mock.calls[0][1];\\n    const sentData = JSON.parse(fetchCall.body);\\n    expect(sentData).not.toHaveProperty('password');\\n  });\\n});\"],[1693,\"Touch events in JavaScript are represented by the TouchEvent object. It contains lists of touches (touches, targetTouches, changedTouches), which let you track several fingers at once. Each Touch object within a list holds coordinates (clientX, clientY), a touch identifier and the target element. These events are essential for building interactive interfaces on mobile devices, such as zooming, dragging or swiping.\",\"In web development for mobile devices and tablets, the standard mouse events (click, mousedown) are not well suited to handling multi-touch gestures. For that purpose JavaScript has a special type of event — TouchEvent. It is generated when a touch on a sensor screen begins, moves and ends.\\n\\nThe contents of the TouchEvent object\\n\\nTouchEvent's key property is three lists of `Touch` objects:\\n\\n- `touches`: the list of all the touches currently active on the screen.\\n\\n- `targetTouches`: the list of touches that began on the same target element as the current event.\\n\\n- `changedTouches`: the list of touches that changed and caused this event (for example, new fingers for `touchstart`, or removed ones for `touchend`).\\n\\nThe Touch object\\n\\nEvery object in those lists represents one touch of a finger and holds important data:\\n\\n- `identifier`: a unique numeric ID for tracking a particular finger throughout the whole gesture.\\n\\n- `target`: the DOM element where the touch began.\\n\\n- `clientX / clientY`: the touch coordinates relative to the browser window (the viewport).\\n\\n- `pageX / pageY`: the coordinates relative to the whole document.\\n\\n- `screenX / screenY`: the coordinates relative to the device's screen.\\n\\n- `radiusX / radiusY`: the approximate size of the touch area (for example, to determine the pressure).\\n\\nEvent types and practical use\\n\\nThe main types of touch event:\\n\\n- `touchstart`: a finger touched the screen.\\n\\n- `touchmove`: a finger is moving across the screen.\\n\\n- `touchend`: a finger left the screen.\\n\\n- `touchcancel`: the touch was interrupted by the system (for example, an incoming call).\\n\\nThese events are used to implement gestures: a swipe for navigation, pinch-to-zoom for scaling images, dragging interface elements.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nA simple example of tracking a single touch to drag an element:\\n\\nConclusion: The TouchEvent object and its contents are the foundation for building responsive, intuitive touch interfaces in mobile web applications. It is worth using whenever you need precise control over multi-touch interactions that cannot be implemented through mouse events or standard CSS properties such as `touch-action`.\",\"let draggable = document.getElementById('box');\\nlet startX, startY, initialX, initialY;\\n\\n// Handling the start of a touch\\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchstart', function(event) {\\n  // Prevent the page scrolling\\n  event.preventDefault();\\n  // Take the first touch from the list\\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\\n  // Remember the initial coordinates of the touch and the element\\n  startX = touch.clientX;\\n  startY = touch.clientY;\\n  initialX = draggable.offsetLeft;\\n  initialY = draggable.offsetTop;\\n});\\n\\n// Handling the finger's movement\\ndraggable.addEventListener('touchmove', function(event) {\\n  event.preventDefault();\\n  let touch = event.touches[0];\\n  // Compute the offset\\n  let deltaX = touch.clientX - startX;\\n  let deltaY = touch.clientY - startY;\\n  // Apply the new position to the element\\n  draggable.style.left = (initialX + deltaX) + 'px';\\n  draggable.style.top = (initialY + deltaY) + 'px';\\n});\"],[1694,\"Checking how many times a method was called matters because it guarantees that the program's logic works correctly rather than merely firing. For example, a notification-sending method should be called exactly once, not zero or two times. Checking only that it was called may miss errors such as extra calls inside a loop or missed calls because of a condition. This helps avoid bugs related to performance or side effects.\",\"In unit testing, mocks or spies are often used to verify interaction with external dependencies. Checking that a method was called confirms that a particular path through the code was executed. That is not enough, however, to verify the logic's correctness fully.\\n\\nWhy the number of calls matters\\n\\nConsider a typical scenario: a method that sends an email to a user. If we check only that it was called, the test will pass even if the method was called twice (which would result in duplicate emails) or was not called at all because of a bug in a condition. Checking the exact number of calls (for example, exactly once) guarantees that the side effect happened the expected number of times.\\n\\nExamples of problems when there is no such check\\n\\n- Extra calls in a loop: A bug in a condition can lead to the method being called for every element of an array, when there should be one call for the whole block.\\n\\n- Missed calls: The method may not be called because of a premature return or incorrect branching logic.\\n\\n- Performance: Extra calls to an API, a database or expensive operations slow the application down.\\n\\nA code example with a test\\n\\nSuppose we have a notification service that should send a welcome email to a new user, but only if the user opted in.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\nThis practice is widely used in unit and integration testing, especially when working with:\\n\\n- External APIs (payment gateways, mail services).\\n\\n- Databases (checking the number of INSERT/UPDATE queries).\\n\\n- Logging (making sure an error is logged a particular number of times).\\n\\n- Caching (checking that an expensive method is called once and the result cached).\\n\\nConclusion: Checking the number of calls to a method is an important practice for writing reliable tests. It lets you make sure not only that the code ran but that it ran the correct number of times, preventing logic errors and inefficient use of resources. This check is especially critical for methods with side effects (sending data, changing state).\",\"class UserService {\\n  constructor(notifier) {\\n    this.notifier = notifier;\\n  }\\n\\n  registerUser(user, sendWelcomeEmail) {\\n    // Registering the user in the system...\\n    if (sendWelcomeEmail) {\\n      this.notifier.sendWelcomeEmail(user.email);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A test using Jest and mocks\\nit('should send welcome email exactly once when flag is true', () => {\\n  const mockNotifier = {\\n    sendWelcomeEmail: jest.fn()\\n  };\\n  const service = new UserService(mockNotifier);\\n  const testUser = { email: 'test@example.com' };\\n\\n  service.registerUser(testUser, true);\\n\\n  // Checking only that it was called:\\n  // expect(mockNotifier.sendWelcomeEmail).toHaveBeenCalled();\\n  // That is not enough!\\n\\n  // The correct check of the number of calls:\\n  expect(mockNotifier.sendWelcomeEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);\\n  expect(mockNotifier.sendWelcomeEmail).toHaveBeenCalledWith('test@example.com');\\n});\\n\\nit('should not send email when flag is false', () => {\\n  const mockNotifier = {\\n    sendWelcomeEmail: jest.fn()\\n  };\\n  const service = new UserService(mockNotifier);\\n  const testUser = { email: 'test@example.com' };\\n\\n  service.registerUser(testUser, false);\\n\\n  // Checking that the method was not called at all:\\n  expect(mockNotifier.sendWelcomeEmail).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(0);\\n});\"],[1695,\"Protocols (or interfaces) define a contract that a class or struct must fulfil, without tying the code to a concrete implementation. That lets you substitute stubs returning predefined data for real services (for example, network requests or database work) in tests. This approach isolates the module under test and makes the tests fast, stable and independent of external systems. The result is code that is more flexible and maintainable.\",\"Using protocols (interfaces) is a key technique in object-oriented and protocol-oriented programming for achieving loose coupling between a system's components. Instead of a class depending directly on another concrete class, it depends on an abstraction — a protocol. That makes it easy to replace the implementation, especially in tests.\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\nYou define a protocol with a set of methods or properties representing the functionality required. Your main code (for example, a service or a controller) then works only with that protocol. The concrete implementation (for example, a class for working with the network or a database) conforms to that protocol. In production code you inject the real implementation, and in tests a special test double (a mock or stub) that imitates the behaviour.\\n\\nAn example in Swift\\n\\nSuppose we have a service for loading a user's data.\\n\\nUse in tests\\n\\nIn a unit test you can create an instance of `MockUserFetcher`, configure its behaviour (for example, to return a particular user or throw an error) and pass it into `UserProfileViewModel`. That lets you verify the ViewModel's logic in isolation, without real network calls, which makes the test fast and reliable.\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- Unit testing, to isolate the code under test.\\n\\n- Integration testing, where some of the dependencies can be substituted.\\n\\n- Architectural patterns such as Dependency Injection, Clean Architecture, MVP, MVVM.\\n\\n- Working with external services (APIs, databases, file systems) that need to be imitated.\\n\\nConclusion: Using protocols to improve testability is especially useful when developing complex applications, where fast, stable automated tests matter. This approach lets you create stubs for external dependencies easily, making the tests independent of the infrastructure and focused on the business logic.\",\"// A protocol defining the contract for loading data\\nprotocol UserDataFetcher {\\n    func fetchUser(by id: Int) async throws -> User\\n}\\n\\n// The real implementation, which makes a network request\\nclass NetworkUserFetcher: UserDataFetcher {\\n    func fetchUser(by id: Int) async throws -> User {\\n        // A network request to the API\\n        // ...\\n        return User(name: \\\"John Doe\\\")\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A stub for tests\\nclass MockUserFetcher: UserDataFetcher {\\n    var mockUser: User?\\n    var shouldThrowError = false\\n\\n    func fetchUser(by id: Int) async throws -> User {\\n        if shouldThrowError {\\n            throw NetworkError.notFound\\n        }\\n        return mockUser ?? User(name: \\\"Test User\\\")\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The class that uses the fetcher (it depends on the protocol)\\nclass UserProfileViewModel {\\n    let fetcher: UserDataFetcher\\n\\n    init(fetcher: UserDataFetcher) {\\n        self.fetcher = fetcher // Dependency injection\\n    }\\n\\n    func loadUser(id: Int) async -> String {\\n        do {\\n            let user = try await fetcher.fetchUser(by: id)\\n            return \\\"User: \\\\(user.name)\\\"\\n        } catch {\\n            return \\\"Error loading user\\\"\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimport XCTest\\n\\nclass UserProfileViewModelTests: XCTestCase {\\n    func testLoadUserSuccess() async {\\n        // Arrange\\n        let mockFetcher = MockUserFetcher()\\n        mockFetcher.mockUser = User(name: \\\"Alice\\\")\\n        let viewModel = UserProfileViewModel(fetcher: mockFetcher)\\n\\n        // Act\\n        let result = await viewModel.loadUser(id: 1)\\n\\n        // Assert\\n        XCTAssertEqual(result, \\\"User: Alice\\\")\\n    }\\n\\n    func testLoadUserFailure() async {\\n        // Arrange\\n        let mockFetcher = MockUserFetcher()\\n        mockFetcher.shouldThrowError = true\\n        let viewModel = UserProfileViewModel(fetcher: mockFetcher)\\n\\n        // Act\\n        let result = await viewModel.loadUser(id: 999)\\n\\n        // Assert\\n        XCTAssertEqual(result, \\\"Error loading user\\\")\\n    }\\n}\"],[1696,\"To find out that JavaScript has run in a WKWebView, use the `evaluateJavaScript(_:completionHandler:)` method. It executes the script passed in asynchronously and calls the completion handler once execution has finished. In the handler you receive the result or an error, which confirms completion. This is the main way of communicating between native Swift code and web content.\",\"WKWebView is a component for displaying web content in iOS applications. There is often a need to run JavaScript code in it and to be notified when that operation finishes, in order to update the native interface or pass data along.\\nThe main method: evaluateJavaScript\\nThe key method for this is `evaluateJavaScript(_:completionHandler:)`. It takes a string of JavaScript code and a closure (the completion handler) that is called after the script has run. The closure receives an optional result (for example, the value the script returned) and an optional error.\\n\\nPractical use\\n\\n- Extracting data: Getting the page title, the contents of form fields or the state of elements.\\n- Interacting with a web application: Calling functions defined in the loaded web page in order to change its behaviour.\\n- Synchronisation: Waiting for a critical script to run before moving on to the next step in the native code.\\nIt is important to remember that the method runs asynchronously and works on the main thread, so no additional dispatching is required to update the UI. If the JavaScript code takes a long time, however, it can block the UI, so complex operations are better broken up.\\nConclusion: Use `evaluateJavaScript` with a completion handler when you need to run a script in a WKWebView and know exactly when it finished, for example to get data from a web page or to control a hybrid application.\",\"let webView = WKWebView()\\n// ... load content into webView\\n\\nlet javascriptCode = \\\"document.title;\\\"\\nwebView.evaluateJavaScript(javascriptCode) { (result, error) in\\n    if let error = error {\\n        print(\\\"JS execution error: \\\\(error.localizedDescription)\\\")\\n        return\\n    }\\n    // result holds the value the script returned (for example, the page title)\\n    print(\\\"The JavaScript ran. Result: \\\\(result ?? \\\"no data\\\")\\\")\\n    // Here you can update the UI or run other logic\\n}\"],[1697,\"On mobile devices the keyboard appears when an input field gains focus. That event can be detected through a change in the visual viewport's size (the Visual Viewport API) or through the resize event on window. When the keyboard appears, the visual viewport's height shrinks. You can also listen for the focus and blur events on input elements to determine the moment indirectly.\",\"Detecting the on-screen keyboard appearing is a key task for building convenient mobile interfaces. When a user taps an input field, the system shows the keyboard, which changes the content's viewing area. There is no direct \\\"keyboardshow\\\" event on the web, so developers use indirect methods.\\n\\nThe main approaches to detection\\n\\nThe most reliable modern way is to use the Visual Viewport API. The visual viewport is the part of the window currently displayed on the screen (excluding the areas covered by the keyboard).\\n\\nAlternative and traditional methods\\n\\n- The resize event on window: In many mobile browsers the keyboard appearing fires the main window's resize event. That behaviour is not standardised, however, and can lead to false positives.\\n\\n- Tracking focus: You can listen for the focus and blur events on input fields. The keyboard appearing usually follows focus immediately. That gives no information about the exact sizes but does indicate the context.\\n\\n- Checking the active element: Combining document.activeElement (which points at the input field) with a change in the window's size gives a more accurate picture.\\n\\nThese techniques should be applied to scroll to the active field automatically, to change the layout (for example, hiding the bottom navigation bar) or to switch input into full-screen mode.\\n\\nConclusion: Use the Visual Viewport API for the most accurate, modern detection of changes in the viewing area caused by the keyboard, especially when it is critical to know exactly how much space is left in order to adapt the UI.\",\"// An example using the Visual Viewport API\\nif (window.visualViewport) {\\n  window.visualViewport.addEventListener('resize', function(event) {\\n    const newHeight = event.target.height;\\n    const windowHeight = window.innerHeight;\\n    // If the visual viewport's height is significantly less than the window's height,\\n    // the keyboard is probably open\\n    if (newHeight < windowHeight * 0.7) {\\n      console.log('The keyboard has probably appeared');\\n      // Here you could, for example, scroll the input field into view\\n    } else {\\n      console.log('The keyboard is probably hidden');\\n    }\\n  });\\n}\"],[1698,\"An application's first launch can be detected by saving a special flag in persistent storage, for example in the browser's localStorage or in the file system of native applications. On start-up the application checks whether that flag exists. If there is no flag, it is the first launch, and we perform the necessary actions (for example, show onboarding or load the base settings), after which we set the flag. If the flag is already there, the application starts in its normal mode.\",\"Detecting an application's first launch is a common task that lets you perform one-off actions during initialisation. For example, showing a tutorial screen, loading the initial default settings, sending an analytics event or creating the necessary data structures.\\nThe main approach\\nThe simplest and most reliable way is to use persistent storage to save a flag indicating that the application has already been launched. On every start-up the application checks whether that flag exists.\\n\\n- If there is no flag — it is the first launch. The necessary actions are performed and the flag is written to storage.\\n- If the flag is there — the application starts in its normal mode.\\nAn implementation example in JavaScript (the browser)\\nIn web applications `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` is often used for this. `localStorage` keeps data indefinitely, while `sessionStorage` keeps it only for the session (until the tab is closed).\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is used in mobile and desktop applications (where the flag may be stored in a file or in the native settings), in web applications, and also in server applications (for example, to initialise a database on a container's first start).\\nImportant nuances\\n\\n- Clearing storage: The user can clear `localStorage`, which leads to a repeated \\\"first launch\\\". That has to be accounted for in the logic.\\n- Application versions: Sometimes you need to detect the first launch of a new version. For that you can store the application's version and compare it with the current one.\\n- Security: You should not rely on this mechanism for critically important operations, since data on the client can be altered.\\nConclusion: Detecting the first launch with a flag in persistent storage is a simple, effective pattern for performing initialisation tasks such as onboarding, setting up the environment or sending analytics.\",\"function checkFirstLaunch() {\\n  const isFirstLaunch = localStorage.getItem('appHasLaunched');\\n\\n  if (!isFirstLaunch) {\\n    console.log('This is the application\\\\'s first launch!');\\n    // The actions for the first launch\\n    showOnboarding();\\n    setDefaultSettings();\\n    // Set the flag so this does not fire again\\n    localStorage.setItem('appHasLaunched', 'true');\\n  } else {\\n    console.log('The application has been launched before.');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Call it when the application initialises\\ncheckFirstLaunch();\"],[1699,\"When handling JSON with missing fields you need to allow for two main scenarios: when a field is absent entirely and when it has the value null. In strictly typed languages, for example TypeScript, you can use optional types or default values. In JavaScript you can use the optional chaining operator (`?.`) or the nullish coalescing operator (`??`) for safe access. It is always worth validating the incoming data to avoid runtime errors.\",\"When working with JSON, especially from external sources, you cannot guarantee that every expected field is present. Handling missing data is a key part of building fault-tolerant applications.\\nThe main approaches to handling it\\n\\n- Checking that the field exists: Before accessing a value you need to make sure the field exists in the object.\\n- Using default values: If a field is missing or equal to null, a safe default value should be substituted.\\n- Validating the structure: Using libraries to check that the JSON matches the expected schema (JSON Schema, Zod, Joi).\\nExamples in JavaScript/TypeScript\\nConsider the typical scenario of parsing a response from an API.\\n\\nValidation with libraries\\nFor complex structures it is better to use validation libraries. For example, with Zod in TypeScript:\\n\\nConclusion: Handling missing fields is a mandatory practice for preventing failures. Use safe-access operators and default values for simple cases, and validation schemas for complex data structures, especially when the JSON comes from untrusted sources.\",\"// The source JSON, where the 'age' field may be missing\\nconst jsonString = '{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice\\\"}';\\nconst data = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\n\\n// UNSAFE: may raise an error if the field is missing\\n// const userAge = data.age;\\n\\n// Safe access with the ?. and ?? operators\\nconst userAge = data?.age ?? 25; // If age is missing or null, it will be 25\\nconsole.log(`Age: ${userAge}`); // Output: Age: 25\\n\\n// An explicit check\\nlet userName;\\nif ('name' in data) {\\n    userName = data.name;\\n} else {\\n    userName = 'Guest';\\n}\\n\\n// Using destructuring with default values\\nconst { name = 'Guest', age = 25, city = 'Unknown' } = data;\\nconsole.log(name, age, city); // Alice 25 Unknown\\n\\nimport { z } from 'zod';\\n\\n// Define the schema for the expected data\\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\\n    name: z.string().default('Guest'),\\n    age: z.number().optional().default(25),\\n    email: z.string().email().optional(),\\n});\\n\\n// Parse and validate the incoming data\\ntry {\\n    const safeUser = UserSchema.parse(incomingData);\\n    // safeUser is guaranteed to have the name and age fields with their default values\\n    console.log(safeUser.name);\\n} catch (err) {\\n    // Handling the validation error\\n    console.error('Invalid data:', err);\\n}\"],[1700,\"On mobile devices the keyboard appearing changes the size of the viewport. To handle that you need to listen for the 'keyboardDidShow' and 'keyboardDidHide' events. When the keyboard appears you can change the container's padding/margin or use the 'padding-bottom' CSS property set to the keyboard's height. In React Native there is a KeyboardAvoidingView component for this, which adjusts the layout automatically. It is important to test on real devices, since the behaviour can differ between browsers.\",\"The software keyboard appearing on a mobile device is an event that substantially changes the space available for displaying content. Handling this event is necessary so that interface elements (for example, input fields or buttons) are not covered by the keyboard, keeping things convenient for the user.\\nThe mechanics\\nIn the web environment browsers generate `resize` and `visualViewport` events when the keyboard appears or hides. The keyboard's height varies depending on the device and the screen's orientation. In native mobile applications (React Native, iOS, Android) there are dedicated APIs and events for this.\\nA practical implementation (a web example)\\nHere is an example in JavaScript for a web application that tracks changes in the visual viewport's height and adjusts the main container's padding:\\n\\nUse in React Native\\nIn the React Native ecosystem the task is solved more simply with the built-in components:\\n\\n- KeyboardAvoidingView: A wrapper that automatically raises the content when the keyboard appears. You need to choose the appropriate behaviour (padding, height, position).\\n- Keyboard and events: You can subscribe to the `Keyboard.addListener('keyboardDidShow', ...)` events manually for finer control, for example for animations.\\nConclusion: Adjusting the layout dynamically when the keyboard appears is a mandatory practice for building quality mobile and responsive web applications, and directly affects the user experience (UX). Use the frameworks' built-in components where possible, and the visual viewport API for web solutions.\",\"// Check for visualViewport API support\\nif (window.visualViewport) {\\n  const viewport = window.visualViewport;\\n  const mainContainer = document.getElementById('root-container');\\n\\n  viewport.addEventListener('resize', function(event) {\\n    // Compute how much the viewing area's height has shrunk\\n    const keyboardHeight = window.innerHeight - viewport.height;\\n    // Set a bottom padding equal to the keyboard's height\\n    mainContainer.style.paddingBottom = keyboardHeight + 'px';\\n  });\\n\\n  viewport.addEventListener('scroll', function(event) {\\n    // In addition you can scroll the input field into view\\n    const activeElement = document.activeElement;\\n    if (activeElement && (activeElement.tagName === 'INPUT' || activeElement.tagName === 'TEXTAREA')) {\\n      activeElement.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' });\\n    }\\n  });\\n}\"]]");export{e as default};